        <p>'PP McDOUGALL'S Paterit SELF-RAISING FLOUP THIETasT The Examiner Saturday, March 26 1904.</p>
        <p>W. P. Rylands. The Board of -Management was appointed as follows: The President and Vice-President Rev. union it rests with the Guardians to decide in whal , Mr. H manner relief, when it is needed, should be given John Yonge,enry Thornton, Mr. Thomas Birchall, Rev. Harry Vernon G whether in or out of the workhouse. The Board cannot Pigot, [qr. J. Douglas Buckton, Mr. eorge Thompson, Rev. Father J. P. Whittle, Rev. interfere with the discretion of the Guardians in this E. C. E. Carleton, Mr. W. J. Forshaw, Rev. William respect, nor for the purpose of ordering relief in any Bracecamp , with Mr. John Roberts, Mr. Thomas Burton,Watkins and Mr. T. Wilkinson as represent- individual case, being expressly prohibited by law from Mr. C. M. so doing. Mr. Bent contended that the Board was at liberty ing the Workingmen's Committee, and the members of to give what relief it thought fit. th 3 Medical Board, and the Hon. Treasurer. 1 Mr. A. J. JOLLEY pointed out that Mr. Bent had not Votes of thanks were tendered to the Board of Man_ paid sufficient attention to the words " subject to the agement Committee, Finance Committee,House Visitors, regulations in force in the Warrington Union." Committee, Workingmen's Committee, and Ladies' Ball The subject then dropped. Committee. THE CONTRACTS. The Mayor was thanked for allowing the use of the I The following tenders were accepted for the ensuing six , Council Chamber, and the proceedings terminated with months ;=leather, Messrs. E. Pickavance and Son; a vote of thanks to the President. brushes, Messrs. P. L. Richardson Ltd' meat, Mr. H. 1 , Singleton; coffins, Mr. A. E. Mason • clogs,Mr. William , Jones ;bread and flour and oatmeal,M'r. James Hallwood. The tenders for coal, shoes, groceries, cord and twill tcc., were referred to the House Committee. The Master was instructed to buy potatoes at the market price. The Board then rose.</p>
