      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>&amp; 1-13). Tins and in 6d. Square Tins.</head>
        <p>&amp; 1-13). Tins and in 6d. Square Tins.</p>
        <p>scholarships, prizes and maintenance of children in industrial and other schools. One hears so much about the working An's contribution to the rates, and it is used so frequently as an argument to stop almost every improvement or the slightest increase in the rates, that I felt it due to you who are responsible for this expenditure in education to see exactly how the matter stands, and I have given you the facts, and you are able to judge for yourselves how much the working classes of this town receive and pay for the advantages of a good education. I am sure I am expressing the feeling of the committee when I say how much we are indebted to Mr. Murray and his staff for the efficient way they have been able to carry out the instructions of the several sub-committees. We have a great deal. to do yet before we realize our ideal of a complete system of elementary and secondary education for the town.. This will come in due time if the same thought- ful consideration is given in future years as in the past to the work of the committee. It is a noble work, and I can only congratulate you onhavinghad the conscious pleasure of assisting in laying the foundation of what I believe can be made a truly national system of educa- tion worthy the name. (Applause.) The MAYOR, in moving a vote of thanks to Mr. Monks for his services in the chair during the past 12 months, said he must have given the question of educa- tion the most careful consideration. The committee could be congratulated upon having a chairman who could stick to his guns. Education had a great deal to thank Mr. Monks for in Warrington. (Loud applause.) Councillor ELLISON seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON supported the motion, and said he gave the Chairman every credit for being sincere in all the statements he made. They had differed on many occasions, and would no doubt do so again, but he should support the resolution with pleasure. The motion was heartily agreed to, and Mr. Monks briefly acknowledged the compliment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>A YEAR'S WORK UNDa 'THE NEW ACT.</p>
        <p>What the Ratepayers Will Have to Face.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the meeting, of the Warrington Education Committee held on Monday evening the Chairman (Mr. F. Monks) submitted the following review of the first year's work of the authority think we may fairly congrat9late ourselves on the steady progress made in the re-organization of ele- mentary education, and the establishment of a second- ary school. (A) Starting with littlemore than a chaotic voluntary system, struggling to balance income and expenditure out of the resource's provided by Government grants and inadequate voluntary aid, we have been able to re-organize the work on such a basis that ought to give a much needed stimulus to the teach- ing profession to improve their own position, by raising the standard of efficiency for a duly qualified teacher. If the teaching staff is not all that, we could desire, it arises from causes that are beyond our immediate con- trol, but we hope that the carefully revised and gradu- ated scale of salaries for all will gradually provide a class of teachers who will pass the necessary examin- ations, and bring to their work a higher level of mental training in the newer methods of instructing the young. The aim of the committee is to provide a well-educated teacher who has had the advantage of at least two years' mental training in one of the training colleges, in addition to the preparatory training in our own Secondary school, and this must necessarily be a work of time and careful supervision by the School Management Sub-committee. The re-organization of the staff of care takers who are now required to give the whole of their time, and are paid liberal wages for the duties they perform, will add very much to the health and comfort of the children. (Hear, hear.) The experience gained in the Secondary school points to th 9 necessity of increasing the accommodation for this school if the children are to reap the full benefit of its advantages, when ready- to pass out of the elementary stage. During he year a large amount of detail work has had to be done, adding very much to the labours of the sub-com- mittees, but this will not occur again. At the same time we cannot shut our eyes to the fact, that we are face to face with a large expenditure, requiring careful consideration, to provide sufficient school accommoda- tion to meet the growing needs of the town. The normal growth of the population will add yearly about 500 or 600 children to be taught. The require- ments of the Education Department to provide ten square feet of area for each child instead of eight as fiitherto, will reduce our available accommodation by one-fifth, and it is the constant complaint of the school attendance officers that there are not sufficient places for present requirements if they were to insist on a more rigid application of the Act. I estimate that we shall require within the next two years to provide at least 4,000 places. Taking the cost at £l2 10s. per head, this comes to £50,000 for school buildings and furnishing. Although this heavy expenditure will be met by a loan secured by the rates, we shall have to provide the interest and sinking fund, which I estimate will be about £2,500 a year, equal to a rate of 2d. in the £. As far as I can see there is no escaping this out- lay, and I suggest that we meet it boldly, and secure at once the advantages it will give us. It will be the most economical and satisfactory in the end. We shall have the usual outcry against any increase in the Tates, and the burden it will entail upon the working men. I contend in opposition to this that the working classes don't bear the burden of this increase in the rates for education. I have taken the trouble to ascertain the total amount of rates payable for the year 1904,and also who will pay them. The total amount payable is £77,550. £29,101, or 37.52 per cent. of this, is payable by the railway companies, limited companies and the Corporation, whohave not a single vote amongst them. £25,144, or 32.44 per cent., is payable by small manu- facturers, tradesmen, and private householders rented over £2O a year. £23,295, or 30.04 per cent., is payable by those paying a rent of 8s per week and under which will include the great bulk of the working class. From these figures you will see that those who will benefit most largely by the expenditure on education will not contribute quite one-third of the rate, or to put it in plain figures out of the balance to be paid locally es- timated at £10,407 19s sd. only £3,122 will be paid by those rented at 8s per week, and under. I must call your attention to another set of figures viz. : that out of the total estimated expenditure of £40,175 175. sd. as the cost of education for the coming year, not less than £30,962 13s. 6d., or 77 per cent. will be paid in salaries alone, and £1,086 9s. will be paid for</p>
      </div>
