      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>DAINTINESS AND DIGESTIBILITY.</head>
        <p>DAINTINESS AND DIGESTIBILITY.</p>
        <p>Daintiness and digestibility are the two essentials of good pastry. These desirable qualities can be easily secured if a small quantity of Brown and Poison's new raising powder called " Paisley Flour " is added to the ordinary flour (one part to eight or twelve) before making into a dough, omitting all yeast or baking powder. "Paisley Flour" is also a great help to plum puddings, dumplings, and all heavy suet puddings, as it prevents soddenness and makes them lighter and more easily digestible. Similarly, bread made with Brown &amp; Poison's "Paisley Flour" is digestible even when new. Buy a 7d. packet from your grocerand try it next cooking day. If you are not entirely pleased with it, your grocer will refund your money at Brown &amp;Poison's expense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>NEW BOOKS.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>Memoirs of Joseph Priestley.</p>
        <p>The British Medical journal says :—" Benger's Food has It 3 ts excellence established a reputation of its own"</p>
        <p>A centenary edition of the "Memoirs of Dr. Joseph Priestley," written by himself to the year 1795, with a continuation to the time of his decease by his son, Joseph Priestley, has been issued by Mr.H. R. Allenson, Ivy-lane, London, E.C. The work is reprinted from the edition of 1809, with numerous plates and illustra- tions, price 3s. The Rev. F. R. Freestoi4 who will be well-remembered as a former minister of Cairo-street Chapel, Warrington, edits the new edition and remarks in a brief preface that the centenary of the doctor's death suggested a reprint of his autobiography. "It tells firstly his life story at Daventry, Needham Market, Nantwich, Warrington, Leeds, Caine and Birmingham, up to the year 1787, ut which point he discontinued the account. This portion escapel the destruction of his papers during the church and King riots in 1791, a des- cription of which is here given,by permission, from the Letters of Catherine Hulton 1891. At Northumberland, Priestley continued his autobiography up to 1795, and had intended to complete the narrative, but its conclu- sion was supplied by his son. With this secondportion, the memoirs, therefore, comprise the whole seventy-one years from his birth to his decease." Those of our readers who, like Alderman Bennett, possess a warm admiration for the philosopher of 100 years ago and take some pride in the fact that he spent several years as a tutor in the Warrington Academy, will doubtless welcome this re-issue of the " Memoirs." The recent centenary celebrations here, and the book, combined should go far towards keeping green in Warrington the memory of Dr. Priestley L.L.D.</p>
        <p>SOLDIER SHOT IN BURY BARRACKS.I The Bury police report the death, by shooting, at Bury barracks of Private James Lucas, a recruit of the 6th Battalion Lancashire Fusiliers. Lucas hails from RDchdale, and enlisted on the 18th inst. When last seen alive he was holding a rifle in his hand with the butt end on the floor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>INFIRMARY AND DISHBAIY.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY AND DISHBAIY.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER YF.AIII3 WORK REVIEWED.</p>
        <p>More Accommodatiaa Waatad.</p>
        <p>The President of the Warrington Inarmary and Dis- pensary (Mr. J. C. Parr) presided at the annual meeting of governors, held on Tuesday afternoon. BOARD OF MANAGEMENT REPORT. • A REDUCED DEBIT BALANCE.</p>
        <p>The Board of Management in their report for the year ending December 31st, 1903, state that there has been an increase in the number of patients treated in the house of 7, as compared with the year 1902, the average number of beds occupied being 30.84, and the average cost per bed after allowing the usual is. 61. per head for out-patient, and 4s, per head for home patients, was £66 ss. 6d., as against £7O ss. in the preceding year. The receipts from ordinary sources have been £2,902 us. 2d., and from other sources £175 is. 4d. making a total of £3,077 12s. 6d. The payments includ- ing the debit balance brought forward, have been £3,121 lls. 3d. The year under review began with a debit balance due to the expenditure and income account of £193 9s. 7d. ; it closed with a reduced debit balance due to this account of £43 18s-. 9d, A table gives the comparison of the various items of income for 1902 and 1903, after which the report states that the most reassuring item in the income was the increase in the interest received from investments. Notwithstan ling the loss by death and removal during the past year of sub- scribers whose annual contributions amounted to a considerable amount, the total amount received in annual subscriptions was upward; of £47 in excess of any previous year. It was hoped the increase would be more than maintained in the current year. The Work- ing Men's Infirmary Committee Fund had, as usual, been energetically supported. Experience showed that by the more general adoption of the weekly collections in the various workshops greater satisfaction was given to the subscribers, and a larger sum was more easily collected. There was a slight decrease in the amount received from the Hospital Sunday collections, which might be accounted for by bad weather, which prevailed on the Sunday selected for the collection. There had been additions to the endowment fund during the past year by legacies from the late Mrs. Beamont, Mr. Robert Garnett, and Mrs. Mary Marrow, of Hatton. The number of life governors had been augmented and strengthened by the addition of the names of Messrs. Roger C. Parr, Vere B. Davies, James Parr. and William Flet3her. The Board had studied the expenditure with much anxiety, and given every attention to prevent its increase, but the necessary expenses incurred owing to the increase in the Number of patients treated, purchase of X-ray apparatus, instalment of electric lift and light- ing, as well as of general expenses, had contributed largely to an increase of expenditure on the year's work- ing. The Board record with deep regret the death of Mr. Robert Garnett, and of Mr. Thomas Fletcher, life governors.</p>
        <p>THE MEDICAL BOARD AND THE ABUSE OF HOSPITALS.</p>
        <p>The third annual report of the Medical Board, signed by Dr. Joseph (chairman), and Dr. Richmond (secretary), emphasizethe necessity for increase inthe numbprof beds strongly recommended to the Board of Management by the Medical Committee, which has received notice in the public Press. With much satisfaction it must be recorded that the Board of Management has taken energetic action with the view of Rlacing the efficiency of the hospital on a par with those of towns similarly circumstanced, and that the imperatively necessary work of extension is now being taken in hand. In other respects the previous recommendations of the Medical Committee have been most satisfactorily supplied notably the provision of electric lighting, electric lift and Rontgen ray apparatus. These are working well and have materially tended to the improved treatment and comfort of the patients. The adequate lodging of the nurses in a home separate from the hospital—an essential feature in all modern hospitals—has been approved by the Board of Management, and it is hoped that the next report will be able to hail this as an accomplished fact. It is desired to draw public atten- tion to the abuse of hospitals by patients who are in a position to pay for their medical attendance, and who are not proper persons for the receipt of its charity. This abuse undoubtedly exists, and should receive care- ful revision. Also it has been repeatedly forced on the notice of the Medical Committee that an impression exists in the minds of most of the patients, and even of some of the subscribers, that the honorary staff receive remuneration for their • work, which is, of course, entirely untrue. Though it may be unnecessary to issue a disclaimer to that effect—as has been the case with other hospitals—such an impression is mis- leading and may tend to foster the abuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>HOUSE SURGEON'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSE SURGEON'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>In his report, Dr. Hodgkinson, the House Surgeon, states that during the year 1903, 490 patients were admitted, of whom 322 were males and 163 females. This showed an increase of seven as compared with 1902. Of this number 410 were surgical and 80 medical cases. The surgical cases included 211 patients admitted as accidents and emergencies. Thirty-one deaths had occurred in the institution during the year, 17 as the result of accidents and 14 from disease. The average number of beds occupied was 30.84; an increase of 2.3 as compared with 28.54 of the preced- ing year. On several occasions it had been necessary to close the wards owing to the outbreak of infectious disease. That. in a small hospital, serionsh reduced the capacity of its work. With regard to the dispensary the report states that the new home patients treated during the year numbered 1,264, an increase of 176 as compared with the previous year. The visits paid to the home patients treated during 1903 numbered 4,729 Three thousand and sixty-eight new out-patients were treated at the dispensary, an increase of 263 on last year's numbers. The accidents and emergencies treated in the Accident Room numbered 2.793, less by 93 an compared with 2,891 in 1902. The total number of patients treated at the dispensary was thus 7;130. That represented a total increase in this department of :351, and during the last three years of 1,151 patients. The X-ray department was now in working order; fourcases of lupus had received treatment with very satisfactory results. Many of the fractures had been examined under the rays.</p>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT AND THE NECESSITY FOR ENLARGENIEN'T.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT, in reviewing the report, said he was happy to say it was entirely satisfactory. (Hear, hear.) The depression in trade had occasioned more applica- tions for the benefits of the institution, but that was only to be expected. Financially the year began with a debit balance of £193 9s. 7d., and closed with the amount reduced to £43 18s. 9d., the remainder having been paid off. (Hear, hear.) He hoped it would not be long before they would be able to start with a clean steet. The subscriptions had increased from £506 to £553, and for that increase much credit was due to the Secretary, Mr. liampson. (Hear, hear.) The donations had slightly decreased, but that was one of those things which occurred from time to time, and which next year might be the other way. The congregational collec- tions were also slightly under,but he was told that that might be accounted for by the very wet Sundays. The annual ball was going to provide for them a larger sum than had previously been realized since the Ball Com- mittee commenced operations. That would appear in the next balance sheet. The Working Men's Committee had also done splendidly, and the seem- ingly large decrease in the amount received from them as against 1902 was accounted for by the fact that in that year the cycle carnival raised £260. The rctual subscriptions from the working men were, he was happy to say, gradually increasing. There had been additions to the endowment fund during the year, and although they must regret the loss of their friends, it showed the ifterest they had taken in the institution, With respect to the hospital itself, they commenced operations in 1810 witha very modest littl association called the dispensary. In 1877 their new hospital building was opened by Lord Cross, and with some modifications and improvements that was the building they were called upon to recognise that day. Now they found that the erawth of the town and the population made it inadequate, and they must now con- sider what must be done in the way of enlarging it. The question had been before the committee and thoroughly discussed, but he thought the more discussion they could have cm the matter the better. What was done would depend to an extent on the amount they were able to raise, and his own feeling was that they should get in hand the money required and then consider how best it might be laid out. When the com- mittee had further discussed the question they could call a special meeting and ask the advice of the sub- scribers. (Hear, hear,) The RECTOR, in seconding the adoption of the report ' hoped the hospital collections on Sunday would be larger than they had been in the past. He thought on Ithe whole they had been very satisfactory. It was very 'delightful to them to know how generously the working men supported the institution. They had every confid- ence in it, and they showed them in the best way in their power, by contributions how satisfactory they felt the institution to be. (Hear, hear.) The report was then approved. RE-ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The following gentlemen were re-appointed to their several offices and were thanked for their past services: —Hon. treasurer, Mr. George Dakin ; hon. auditor. Mr. C. Jackson Holmes: hon. consvlting medical officer, Dr. E. A. Fox; hon. medical officers, Drs. G. W. Jozeph, C. E. Richmond and E. C. Bowden: hon. assistant medical officers, Drs. R. Smith and E. J. Fox. Mr. J. C. Parr was re-appointed president, and the following were appointed vice-presidents; Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., the Rev. Canon F. Willis, M.A., and Messrs. Thomas Henry Lyon, Frederick Monks, Charles White. John White, George Dakin, John Fairclongh. Ziba Armitage, Roger Charlton Parr. A. H. Crosfield and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>" COMPARISONS ARE ODIOUS."</head>
        <p>" COMPARISONS ARE ODIOUS."</p>
        <p>The old proverb is true ; yet nevertheless everything in this world is comparative. In comparing, very much of course depends upon point of view. There are millionaires who, if they were compelled to live on £lO,OOO a year, would regard themselves as little better than beggars. There is also a host of people who would regard such a scale of poverty were it applied to themselves, with more favour. The ultra- Socialist who demands the equal division of property thinks of the people who own more than himself; never of those who own less. Some comparisons prove little; others prove much. Here is one that proves little. An Irishman who congratu- lated himself on being unable to afford a feather bed, was asked to explain, and said that he had once tried to sleep on a single feather,and found it so uncomfortable, that he could not even imagine the torture of lying on thousands of them. Here is a comparison which proves much. It is made by Mrs. Mills, of 110, Victoria-road, Kilburn, London, N.W. "For years," says Mrs. Mills, writing on January 5th,1904 :—"I suffered acutely from indigestion. I consulted one doctor after another, and tried a great variety of medicines, but derived no bene- fit whatever. When at last I tried Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup the result was different. All my troublesome complaints left me, and my original good health was restored." Mrs. Mills con- tinues: "After eating, I used to be seized with violent pain at the chest, and a feeling of weakness as though I were about to collapse. I ate very little, for I was afraid of food, knowing its consequences. Another frequent result was pain between the shoulders and at my side. Many a time at night I have been obliged to sit up for hours, enduring a choking sensa- tion whixth made me gasp for breath. But this was not all :—I had constant attacks of dizziness and torturing headaches. Of course I became greatly weakened and depressed, more especially as I saw no prospect of relief. Such was my condition when I was urged by a relative to try Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. After much pressure, for I had tried several advertised reme- dies without getting any benefit, I consented to do so, and my health at once improved. Thus encouraged, I continued to use the medicine for a little while, with the most perfect success. I know of nothing equal to it, and never miss an opportunity to recommend it. My health is better now than it has been at any time sincell was a little girl."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>EARLESTOWN CRICKET CLUB.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the members of this I club was held at the Viaduct Institute on Tuesday even- ing. Mr. T. Ormand presided and there was a fair attendance of members. The following report of the working of the past season was read by the Secretary Mr. J. Alderson :—The report which I have to presen to you is as follows : 18 =matches were played, it won, 6 lost, and 5 drawn. This is a distinct improve- ment when compared with the previous year, as only 3 matches were then won whilst 6 were lost. The runs scored, totalled 2211 for the loss of 152 wickets, giving an average of 14•38. Last year the average was 12, and in 1301 it was 121. Our opponents on the other hand show a decreased average. rhey scored 1913 runs for 163 wickets hverage,ll.l2, against 15.2 in 1902 and 12,1 in 1901. This is much more satisfactory, and shows a general improvement in all departments of the game, and the standard of play we think has been generally superior to that of one or two seasons back. J. Miller heads the batting with an average of 32.7. and T. R. Howarth who bowled wonderfully well throughout the season, and who has been of great assistance to us, was credited with 69 wickets at the small cost of 9.17. Concerning the second eleven, the players have again shown much ability. They played 14 matches, winning 9, losing 3, and drawing 2, W. Rylance heading the batting with an average of 37, and several bowlers have excellent records. The four bats very generously presented by the Institute Committee have been awarded to the following :—G. Husband, J. H. Alderson, W. Rylance, I and W. Harrison, and the £2 also kindly given by them has been divided between T. Marsh, H. Davies, W. Hatton and H. Warburton. G. B. Leigh's fielding was 'one of the features of the season, and he has been b.warded the fielding prize very kindly given by Mr. Ormand. Matches have been arranged with Wigan, Warrington, Liverpool, St. Helens, Leigh, Newton-le- Willows, Manchester, Clifford, Haigh, and Whalley Range. The balance sheet shows that the total receipts for the year were .257 Bs. Bd., Members subscriptions £l3 75., gate money £l6 7s. 3d. being the principal sources of revenue. The chief items of expenditure are players expenses £l6 4s. 10d. and pro- fessional assistance and talent money £23 Bs. leaving a balance of £1 si. 3d. We have again to thankfully aoknowledge the generous assistance willingly rendered by the Institute Committee,as not only have they given the prizes which are te lye distributed this evening, but in addition have helped to furnish the club with play- ing material, and thus saving the club accounts from showing a debit balance. Two years ago the General Manager,Sir Frederick Harrison offered a silver challencTe cup for competition amongst the clubs who are connected with the various departments throughout the Company's System. A delegate represented this club at the meetings but as it would be necessary for our first team to devote almost the half of the season to playing in these matches, your committee deemed it unwise to join in the competition. This year, however, we have agreed along with the leading clubs Crewe and London to run second teams, so that for the first time we shall this season betaking part in what we hope will prove to be a series of interesting games. On three or four occasions it will be necessary to turn out three teams at the same) time in order to fulfil the cup engagements This will mean greater opportunities forjunior players, a large number of wham are bound to be prayed during the coming season. The final is to be played in London on Saturday August 6th, and we are hoping the cup will find its way to Earlestown upon this occasion. Since we last met we regret to have to report that owing to slackness of work we have lost the services of a number of prominent players, indeed it is expected that the composition of the teams will be greatly altered next season. We do not however anticipate that the playing strength will be much reduced, as we have already promises of one or two who will be new to Earlestown cricket. The matches are both numerous and of good strength, and we are looking forward to a prosperous season. The report was adopted. In the election of officials, Mr. C. W. Emmett was re-appointed captain of the first team, and Mr. J. H. Alderson vice-captain; Mr. A. Limond was re-appointed captain of the second team, and Mr. F. J. Reade, vice- captain. The following were elected as committee : Messrs. J. Owen, J. Seddon, F. J. Reade, G. Husband, T. F . Roberts, T. Marsh, J. Sanders, J. Smith and G. Wilson ; auditors, Messrs. J. Gooding and H. Tarbuck ; treasurer, Mr. T. Ormand ; secrethry, Mr. J. Alderson ; president, Mr. H.D. Earl. Presents of bats bags, and boots for proficiency in batting and bowling were made to a. Husband, J. Alder- son, W. Rylance, W. Harrison, T. Marsh, H. Davies, W. Hatton. H. Warburton and G. Leigh. A vote of thanks to the Chairman terminated the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>REAA.IIIO.BLE ATTEMPTED SUICIDE.</head>
        <p>REAA.IIIO.BLE ATTEMPTED SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>As a London express was entering Snow Hill Station, Birmingham, on Wednesday evening, a respectably dressed man jumped from the platform in front of the approaching train and laid his held on the off-side rail. The guard of the engine caught his head and threw him into the space between the rails, and the engine passed over his body without inflicting any other hurt. The injury was found to be severe but not very dangerous. The man is in the hospita].</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CAARRII AND INFLUENZA.</head>
        <p>CAARRII AND INFLUENZA.</p>
        <p>CATARRFI is the result of a series of oolds ; the symptoms are: —Nose stopped up, difficulty in breath- ing, loose cough, with continual hawking and expector- ating. VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE, quickly Cures this condition. INFLUENZA, . is epidemic and contagious, most prevalent in low-lying districts in spring months. During the severe epidemic which raged in North America, in 1897, VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE was the remedy chiefly used, and it reduced the death rate to such an extent- that doctors never hesitated to pre- scribe it. It is peculiarly well adapted to the speedy cure of both Influenza and Catarrh. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CUR.E. Price 91d., Is. lid., and is. 9d., at Chemists and Drug Stores everywhere.</p>
        <p>WHAT IS MR. I3ALFOUR'S</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Improved Diet for the Inmates.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the W irrington Board of Guardians was held in the Board Room at the Workhouse on Wed- nesday morning. Mr. A. Brooks presided and there werealso present :—Mrs. Clay, Rev. Father Wilson, Rev. T. R. Matthews, Messrs. S. Davies, J. Billing°, W. Hickman, E. i. Bent. P. Langton, T. H. Ashcroft, William Houghton, J. Ball; T. Ritchie, G. Mather, J. Turtan, R. Forshaw, A. Jolley, A. J. Jolley, G. Pilling, with J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master) and A. Wright (superintendent Padgate. Schools).</p>
        <p>_ THE OLD HEARSE. MR. HICKMAN_MAKES AN EXPLANATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. HICKMAN asked permission to make a statement with reference to the remarks made at the last meeting of the Board on the subject of the sale of the old hearse. He had always thought, he said, that the members of the Board were a very melancholy lot until he read the report of the last meeting. He found he was mistaken. He never thought there was so much poetry, romance and humour on the Board. (Laughter.) With reference to the hearse he would like to state that after the expenses of storage, advertising and removing the hearse had been paid, the Board would receive .£2 for it instead of £1 as reported at the last meeting. He should like that to be reported. He was afraid that the hilarity of some of the members ran away with their discretion, And he did not know what they would have done if one gentleman had not had the temerity to pull them up a it. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>AN IMPROVED DIETARY. GRUEL AND SKIM MILK DISPENSED WITH.</p>
        <p>The House Committee reported that the sub-committee appointed to consider the dietary in force at the Work- house had submitted a revised dietary table, and recom- mended a revision of the issues of dry tea and sugar t 6 those inmates, placed upon classes a and b under the scheme of classification by character, now in existence. They recommended that for the purposes of diet classification, men and women in classes a and b con- stitute•classes 2 and 4 and that the issue of dry tea and sugar to inmates in class b be discon- tinued, and to inmates in class a, that only one half of the usual allowance be given, as both these classes would be given tea both for break- fast and supper, which they hitherto had not received. The sub-committee were thanked for their labours, and it was uaanimously resolved by the House Committee to recommend the revised diets for the adoption of the Board. The Medical Officer in connection with the proposals of the sub-committee wrote as follows:—"I have carefully considered the proposed changes in tho dietary tables at the Workhouse and approve of them.— George Braide, Medical Officer." Mr. PILLING, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said the House Committee having had their attention drawn to the unsuitability of the existing dietary in the House for the aged and deserving classes, had carefully gone into the question and found the grievance to be a genuine one. At present the deserving received the same as the able-bodied and undeservin,,, a condition of things which, he believed, no single Guardian would wish to continue, as it had already been the object of the Board to distinguish between the two classes, and to afford every comfort and indulgence to those who had been obliged to enter the House through other adverse circumstances. The deserving classes had hitherto had gruel for breakfast and skim milk for supper, both unpopular foods for aged appetites. The committee had substituted bread, butter and tea night and morning, a meal which would be more acceptable, especially to the old ladies. In other respects the dietary had been varied, anda }lb. snot pudding had been added to the roast beef dinner, a distinct addition, as under present circumstances, these inmates did not receive pudding in any shape or form from oho year's end to another expect Christmas Day. Cocoa had been eliminated fro* the dietary list, as very few appreciated it. The committee had every confidence triat this dietary would answer the Board's purposes for years, and that it would be rendering a useful service in making a dis- tinction between those who, through increasing infirmity and creeping oldage, were compelled to seek alast rest- ing place here, and those v;ho were idle, worthless and objectionable. No one would, he felt sure, question this distinction, and he had pleasure in moving that the Chairman's signature be appended to the new dietary table. Mr. HICKMAN asked whether, the new table would involve additional oost ?</p>
        <p>Mr. PILLING said there would be a saving in some cases but a little more cost in others.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLY thought the Board might rest abso- lutely assured that the changes foreshadowed would mean a reduction of cost, not an increase of it. As Mr. Pilling had said, there would be a slight increase in some items but with regard to the mostexpensive ite:ns there would be a saying. The recommendation with reference to dry tea and sugar would effect a saying of 18s per week and the Guardians would be able to effect other economies. He felt that the Board would find that the new dietary would involvs a saving per head and not an increased cost. (Hear, hear). Mr. FORSFI kW wished so much had not been said about it. The house was full already.</p>
        <p>Mr. BILLINGE: Will it require the approval of the Local Government Board?</p>
        <p>--... _ .._..... The CLERK: When the original order is not departed from it is within the discretion of the Board and the tledical Officer to make alterations. Mr. BILLTNGE: And submit them for the approval of a Local Government Board? Mr. A. J. JOLLEY: No; for record only. Mr. BILLINGS proceeded to speak in support of tke proposals. Mr. R. Vonsnam: I thought there was no opposi- tion to your seat. (Laughter.) The minutes were passed. THE ESTIMA.TES.—A SEVENPENNY RATE. -</p>
        <p>In subnsitting a statement on the estimates the CLERK said :—lt is unnecessary to say to the 'present Board that serious increase has arisen under each of several important heads of expenditure, psrticularly in respect of House and Cottage Homes outlay, in-door aqd out- door relief of the poor, maintenance of lunatics, public vaccination, loan interest repayments, &amp;c. Although it may be deemed practicable to defer some of the im- provements that are intended and needed at both Institutions, there does not appear for the present to be any prospect of reduction under other heads; on the contrary, a probability of increase of cost. In my early experience (then as a Guardian) the calls made upon the Overseers were. as a minimum, a shilling for the winter and tenpence for the summer llalf-year. They fell penny by penny to an average of about sixpence for each half-year. Until the now expiring financial year it was the rule to add the county rate to the union precept, adopting the last paid rate as the basis of calculation for the following rate. This was much to the oonvenience of overseers, andalso to the Guardians. It because necessary to abandon this course, for County demands in each successive half-year sprang serious leaps, not in equal portions against townships, nor upon the union rateable values, with the effect that the Union lodger balances became very unequal. The following parishes, as certified at the recent audit, show as actual deficieacies :—Haydock, £63 19s. 2d. ; Newton. £174 2s. 9d. • Poulton, £lB 95. 8d..; Great Sankey, £23 9s. 101. ; and Winwick, £32 17s. 44. The estimated particulars of common charges for the half-wear ending at Michaelmas, 190 k, are £15,177 us. 94., against £15,856 in the corresponding half of the year 1903. Mr. FORSHAW called attention to the fact that vaccin- ation was estimated to cost £300; that was equal to £1,609 a year. The CLERK : I am afraid it ig going to cost you more than that, if the present lfsw continues owing to the in- crease of population. Mr.FOI33II.SW : I think it is q9ite time it was al- tered. _ .</p>
        <p>The CLERK : The Poor Law Association is doing everything possible to get the Lotal Government Board to make some modification of the existing arrangement. Mr. MATHER : Is tire not a chance,of the nation becoming insolvent before a remeAly is found? Ths CIARK : What may become of the ratepayers, I cannot say. The estimates were then approved. They will involve a poor rate of sevenpence in the £ which is equal to that of a year ago. ACCOMMODATION AT THE SCHOOLS. MR. BENT AND BOARDING OUT. Mr. FORSHAW said he understood there were 212 children in the schools. He believed the accommoda- tion was only for 200 children. Could not some of the children he boarded out? Mr. ASHCROFT said there wai no intention .to increase the accommodation at the schools. There was accommodation for 220. The number would be reduced shortly by removing some of the older children. Mr. BENT said the members would 'remember that he had previously referred to the advisability of boarding out some of the children. The alum : T don't think you see what the adop- tion of the Boarding Out Act means. It does not refer to the boarding out of children within the Union but outside it. Mr. BENT said he had advocated the boarding out of an orphan family. The CHAIRMAN observed that Mr. Bent and the Clerk seemed to be playing at cross-purposes and it would be better if they consulted together. If they could not agree 'gr. Bent could give notice and bring the matter</p>
        <p>up at the next meeting. Mr. BENT said he had written to the Local Govern- ment Board on the matter and the reply was as follows: Subject to the regulations in force in the Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>POLICY?</head>
        <p>POLICY?</p>
        <p>WHAT IS RETALIATIONI</p>
        <p>AN EXPLANATION REQUIRED.</p>
        <p>THE most loyal supporter of Mr. Balfour and his Government would be puzzled to explain the Prime Minister's policy on the Fiscal question. Mr. Balfour himself not only appears unable to do so, but he has recently told us that he does not intend to do so. He seems to think it is an impertinence to ask him to explain. He says that his policy is "Retaliation," but what he means by the use of that word we are not to be told. "The Government does not intend," says Mr. Balfour in effect, "to take any immediate steps to reverse the Fiscal policy of this country, and you have, therefore, no right to expect any explanation of their ultimate or 1 prospective policy." Meanwhile the electors are expected blindly to support Unionist candidates without any guarantee as to how those candidates will act when returned to Parliament. It is not surprising, therefore, that the constituencies are shewing less in- clination to elect them.</p>
        <p>Retaliation at Work. ALTHOUGH Mr. Balfour will not explain his policy of Retaliation in detail, we have been given some idea of its general principle. This principle is that, where any country subjects British produce to unfair treatment, the British Government should retaliate against the produce coming from that country. Now before we can accept any such principle it is absolutely necessary to ask for further explanation. 1. Against what countries is it proposed to institute retaliation? 2. If retaliation does not succeed in its imme- diate object, will the retaliatory duties become nermanent ? 3. Is it proposed to continue retaliation for the purpose of securing better terms from other countries if such a policy proves to be injurious to the people of this country? There are many other points upon whiCh information, is desirable, such as whether it is proposed to retaliate against British Colonies; but it will be sufficient, for the present, if we deal with the above.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Whom* Shall We Hit?</head>
        <p>Whom* Shall We Hit?</p>
        <p>UNIONIST speakers have given us lurid pictures of the dastardly conduct of the "foreigner" towards British trade: We have been asked in stirring tones: "Are you going to take this lying down ? "—the infereneo being that we should be very little Englanders, without pluck .or spirit, if we quietly sub- mit. Now, with all these wild, raving appeals to racial passions we seldom get one word of sober sense. Men will 'cheer until they are hoarse when you talk about "hitting the foreigner," but when you ask them which foreigner we are to hit they are dumb. Even if they can tell you which foreigner is to be hit, they cannot tell you either when, where, or how we are to strike the blow. It is characteristic of the men who rushed this eOuritry into the blundering South African war that they want to rush us into a trade war without being able to shew—</p>
        <p>How we are, going to -fight. Whom we are going to fight. When we are going to fight. Where we are going to fight, Or, what we are going to get by fighting. The country is not likely to entrust the fate of its trade and commerce to the blunderers of Mr. Balfour's Government or Mr. Chamber- lain's diplomacy without some lucid explana- tion of what Protectionists or Retaliators propose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Permanent Retaliation.</head>
        <p>Permanent Retaliation.</p>
        <p>WE have been informed, on the authority of permanent supporters of the Government, that if Retaliation does not succeed in inducing foreign nations to relax their duties upon our goods then Retaliation must become per- manent. There is much reason in this view of the case. Once we admit the wisdom or justice of Retaliation there is no other alternative.</p>
        <p>For example, supposing we adopted Retalia- tion against Germany, how would things work out? We should place a duty upon imports from Germany • then Germany, in turn, would increase the duties upon imports from Great Britain. We should have to reply by increas- ing our duties on German goods, and Germany would quickly follow suit. So the battle would go on until the duties upon German goods would be so great that it would be almost impossible for Great Britain to import any gcodg from Germany, or for Germany to impolrt goods from Great Britain. In other words, trade between the two countries would become stagnant. Neither side would., or could, give in without the consent of the other. The result would he that we should have to maintain these high duties against Germany rot for. months, but for ever, or until we Saw the error of our ways. Thus what was com- menced as Retaliation would end as Protection.</p>
        <p>Vetaliatinz Upon Ourselves. IT must be evident to the least informed Protectionist that if Retaliation caused stag- nation of trade between Great Britain and Germany, the result would be bad for both 'countries, and especially had for Great Britain. The less we imported from Germany, the less we should export to Germany in papnent of the imports. Not only should we ultimately suffer from the decrease of exports, tut we should immediately suffer from the decrease of imports. While our experts to Germany consist almost entirely of fully-manufactured articles, .our imports from Germany contain a very large proportion of 9noto-ials used by cur manufacturers. TO shut cut these materials would inflict a serious injury flpon those manufacturers. Germany could afford to do without our manufactured coeds, 'cut we could not afford to do without German materials.</p>
        <p>What applies to material's applies to foods. Russia and the United States of America are among the most serious offenders in putting heavy duties upon British goods. Yet the greater part of the imports we get from them consist of foods and raw materials. It would be impossib:e to retaliate upon them without taxing these. Such a tax would fall heaviest upon our poor, without giving any hope of inducinc, America ,or Russia to take their Buties off our goods. They can do without our manufactures, but we cannot do without their foods and materials. To sum up</p>
        <p>Retaliation must result in Protection. Retaliation must mean a tax upon food and</p>
        <p>raw materials. Is it because Retaliation must lead to this result that Mr. Balfour declines to explain his policy?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CHINESE TRAGEDY.</head>
