      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>- --THE LATE DUKE OF CAMBRIDGE, R.G. The death of the Duke of Cambridge rs moves not only a popular personality, bif also an historical landmark. He was tl doyen of the Royal Family and, since till death of Queen Victoria, who was twa months his junior, the last link with early part of the last century. Born on the 25th of March, 1819, for a few hours he was heir presumptive to the Throne, only to be deposed by the birth of a son to the Duke of Clarence, afterwards William IV. This child only lived a few hours, and Prince George was once more heir presumptive until ,two months later he was again superseded by the birth of his first cousin, the late Queen Victoria. Coming of a long-lived stock, the Duke, as he was always affectionately called, until the last few weeks has always been wonderfully hale and hearty, but during the last month it has been an open secret that his days were numbered. Last week he was attacked by hemorrhage of the stomach, and on Thursday .morning he passed peacefully away at Gloucester House, his town residence, in the presence of his family, who are well known as the Fitz Georges. During the whole of the Victorian era the Duke of Cambridge, with whom the historic title dies under the Royal Marriages Act, has been a leading figure in society and public life. Though holding aloof from the sphere of politics, he was a very keen soldier and for nearly forty years was Commander-in-Chief of the British Army. He entered the . junior service in 1837, taking rank at once as a lieutenant-colonel. He won promotion whilst serving on the staff in Crete and was ap- pointed Inspector-General of Cavalry in 1852. Going out to the Crimea, he did brilliantly in charge of the Guards Brigade and dis- tinguished himself by his great pluck, again winning promotion, this time on the field of Inkerman. Upon being invalided home, in 1856 he was appointed Commander-in-Chief and continued to hold the post until 1895. During that prolonged period he showed him-</p>
        <p>.rruc warfare off the eastern portion of the Isle of Wight, and the submarines with the destroyers, were preparing to make an attack. Submarine Al, which was the :argest vessel of the flotilla and was taking a prominent part in the manreuvres, had aot been long submerged before a Union 7.:astle liner, the Berwick Castle, bound from Southampton to Hamburg, passed the Nab tnd signalled that she had been struck by iomething. Not much notice was taken of he signal at the time, but later on when F----L-1---- ----- --_-1--_-_-_-,,,,---:,---------- ________ '''-... ---7 -..---- .• .....=... ---,-.... ---=_- ........--",-_ - I _ _-_- --_-_-_---- 1...... . --_,....-,___ ._.--,-</p>
