      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DUKE'S GRAVE.</head>
        <p>THE DUKE'S GRAVE.</p>
        <p>self very zealous and keen, and, though not what would be termed a scientific soldier, tor all that he did a great deal for the Army, proving himself no mere figure head. In addition, he was always to the front in all matters of charity and public good and, despite an exterior roughness of manner, endeared hii-nself to all with whom he came into contact. Socially he was also a great leader. A rich man with his Parliamentary allowance and various salaried posts, he was left a considerable fortune by his mother, and his aunt, the Duchess of Gloucester, also made him her heir, leaving him Gloucester House in Piccadilly, which he always occu- pied, leasing Cambridge House to the Naval and Military Club. His marriage to Miss Fairbrother, the actress, in Dublin in 1837 was one of the great romances of the last century, and though never officially recog- nised at Court, it was ,most happy in its results. The Duke was always a most devoted husband and father, but under the Royal Marriages Act neither his wife nor his children could bear his title or rank.- ^ His three sons, the Colonels Fitz George and Admiral Fitz George, were always his constant companions and are well known in society. Next to the King himself, the Duke of Cambridge held more titles and orders than any other members of the Royal Family, but they, of course, all lapsed at his death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SIR DONALD CURRIES MUNIFICENCE.</head>
        <p>SIR DONALD CURRIES MUNIFICENCE.</p>
        <p>Sir Donald Currie is emulating Mr. Andrew Carnegie, his brother Scot, in the matter of university endowment and the furtherance of education. For it turns • out from a recent letter of his to Lord Rosebery that it was he who anonymously offered the magnificent gift of ioo,ooo to aid the incor- poration of ,Uni- versity College, London, and the London Univer- sity and to assist the work of medical educa- tion in the Metropoli (- Fun&amp; had b collected to - off the debt University C leze and to pt:r- chase the site for the new mtdical school buildings, the new boys' school with suffi- SIR DONALD CURRIE. cient over to</p>
        <p>erect the nem buildings on the latter site, but £BO,OOO was re- quired for the erection of the buildings on the former site. This , was the one obstacle to the incorporation scheme, and with character- istic generosity Sir Donald Currie has promised not only the full amount rweded for the buildings of this school for advanced medical studies, but also an additional 2(:),000 to provide a suitable nurses' home in the neighbourhood of University College Hospital. SAD LOSS OF A SUBMARINE _ .</p>
        <p>In connection with the manceuvres at Portsmouth a painful disaster resulting in the loss of Submarine Al and all hands occurred last Friday afternoon off the Nab lightship: It appears that a full force of submarines, destroyers and warships were engaged is</p>
