      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>DANIEL J. SULLY'S FAILURE.</head>
        <p>DANIEL J. SULLY'S FAILURE.</p>
        <p>The greatest excitement, and quite a small bnancial panic, was caused in New York at the end of last week by the simultaneous hammering of Mr. Daniel J. Sully, the "Cotton King" of recent times, on three different exchanges: Like all his predecessors, he over- reached himself and came toppling down with a fearful crash, though his firm announce their hope of being able to meet their liabilities in full. In cotton alone, however, his losses are variously e s t mated from ,000,000 to £2,500,000, and in addition, he was involved in coffee, wheat and stocks. The most in THE BROKEN "COTTON " excitement pre- KING. vailed when on Friday last he was hammered on the Cotton Exchange, the Coffee Exchange and the Stock Exchange: and the scene beggars all description. 00n the first named prices broke as much as 250 points, till things steadied themselves and the whole floor was in a wild ferment. At first Mr. Sully refused to see anyone, but was later on found transacting his business and going into his affairs as calmly as though nothing had happened. He was carrying forwaid about half a million bales, valued at about Z 8,000,000 when the crash came. Not content, however, with his gigantic gamble in cotton, he had also endeavoured to corner cofiee, but had been driven out of the market by the genuine dealers : and his failure was not altogether unexpected. Few can have any sympathy with him in his down- fall, when they remember the awful misery caused amongst the Lancashire operatives by his attempted corner.</p>
