      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>NEW M.P'S.</head>
        <p>NEW M.P'S.</p>
        <p>Two new members have been added to the House of Commons *under circumstances of more than ordinary interest. The one is Mr. " Lulu " Harcourt, as he is generally known, and his entrahce into active politics has come just after the announcement of the meditated retirement of his famous father, Sir William Harcourt. The advent of the son, who has or years been a familiar figure at Westmin- ster as his father's private secretary, was warmly received by the House of 'Commons, of which both sides welcomed the son of such an illustrious parent without any ques- tion of party. On every hand the announce- ment of Sir William Harcourt's retirement has been receivekwitl: genuine regret, and he</p>
        <p>2R.' LULU HARCOURT. MR. CHARLES LYELL. vf.ceived a great ovation as he walked up the floor of the House to introduce his son, who was al so supported by Mr. Herbert Gladstone, the chief Opposition Whip. Mr. '4 Lulu" Harcourt sits for Rossendale and was returned unopposed. The other new member, Mr. Charles Lyell, is also a Liberal and won a seat for his party at the recent East Dorset election. The constituency lost its former member, when Mr. Humphrey Sturt succeeded his father as Lord Alington after having represented it for more than twelve years. At the General Election in 1900 his majority was 96, but this Mr, Lyell ;reversed into ,a Liberal majority of 820, defeating Mr. Van Raalte, the Unionist candidate.'</p>
