      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION CUP • COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP • COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>SECOND ROUND. Warrington's Sensational Win Against Wigan.</p>
        <p>RECORD CROWD AT WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>A tremendous amount of interest was evinced locally in Warrington's cup-tie with Wigan, and at the colliery centre there was little less excitement. Both teams were confident of winning and played as selected, with one exception, Rees having to take the place of Harris at centre for Wigan. The visitors were on the scene early, notwithstanding a showery morning, and several thousand Wiganers accompanied them. By three o'clock there were 8,000 people on the ground and when the game started, this number had swelled to 11,000, con- stituting a record for the ground. The gate receipts totalled £315. The turf at Wilderspool was in fine order and the conditions all favoured a fast game. Teams:—</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dicken- son, and Harris, three-quarter backs; Bate and Davies, half backs ; Boardman, Thomas' Jolley, Cook, Swift, Thomas, Naylor, and Fairhurst, forwards. Wigan.—Mason, back; Barr, Rees, Leytham, and Sharrock, three-quarter backs; Davies and Sedgurick, half backs; Hilton, Brown. Collier, Topping, Barton, Whittaker, Halliwell, and Yates, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Kennedy, Broughton. _</p>
        <p>Wigan won the toss, and 'Dickenson started for War- rington in the face of a light breeze. Wigan quickly got possession, and by strong forward play menaced the home lines. Hallam was early called upon and respon- ded gallantly. The home backs nipped the visitors' pass- ing in the bud, and eventually play was transferred to the Wigan half. Warrington got possession, and smart passing bet-,een Bate, Davies, Dickenson and Isherwood looked promising, but a groan went was the latter made a mull of the finalutransfer to Fish. Nevertheless War- rington attacked warmly, and the left-winger was soon in evidence though again badly served, and he fell a victim to Sharrock. Davies broke away for Wigan and ran to centre before Thomas upset him, and strong touch kicking sent War- rington back to their own quarters. Here the forwards were penalised and Leytham made a good attempt at goal from long range. A piece of slackness among the home backs looked like causing disaster, but Fish snapped up the ball and cleared with a fast run and kick to touch. The game went on in ding dong style, Wigan having theadvantage of possession, and they shortly experienced hard lines. Barr and Hilton followed up a kick so speedily as to charge Hallam's return and the llne was at their mercy. Barr attempted to pick up but knocked on, and though he finally dribbled over, the try was not allowed, much to the relief of the home crowd. Half time arrived with no score.</p>
        <p>Turning round the lively Wiganers soon took up an offensive position, and it was with difficulty that Hallam and Harris forced Barr in touch at the corner. Leytham and Rees were also tackled close in, the War- rington defence standing the strain well. Then Davies (Wigan), took a shot at goal, and there were some pre- mature rejoicings for the ball passed under the bar. After this the visitors apparently set themselves out for a draw, and keeping the ball close they used touch with such persistency that Warrington could make no head- way. The home forwards were quite outplayed and a pointless draw seemed assured when, five minutes from time, Harris was seen dashing down touch on the right. Mason safely brought him down, but Warrington were well in the Wigan quarter and the hopes of the home supporters rose. The forwards made a desperate effort and secured the ball, Bate and Davies handled quickly, and Dickenson dashed over with a sensational try amid a deafening uproar of cheers. Fish calmly took the goal- kick and missed by inChes, but it was all over. Wigan made a great effort in the last moment but it was re- pelled and Warrington won, luckily, by a try to nil. BROUGHTON RANGERS v. RUNCORN'.</p>
        <p>At Broughton in wet weather, before 10,000 people. From the start Broughton pressed strongly, and Wilson soon scored. Hogg also was credited with a couple of tries, which were unimproved. In the second half, the game was roughly contested, and Oram and Jolley were ordered off. James kicked a penalty goal for Rangers. Result—Broughton Rangers, 1 goal 3 tries (11 points) ; Runcorn, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>LEIGH v. HUNSLET.</head>
        <p>LEIGH v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>At Leigh,beforesooo spectators. The visitors,through W. Goldthorpe, opened the scoring. Hunslet continued to press, and Everson scored, Albert Goldthorpe im- proving. Leigh had several openings, which were net utilised. In the second half; Ganley almost got over. Hunslet played a dashing game in the closing stages. Result—Hunslet, 1 goal 2 tries ; Leigh, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>PONTEFRACT v. BATLEY.</head>
        <p>PONTEFRACT v. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>At Pontefract, before 8,000 spectators. The game was fast throughout, and afforded capital sport and in- tense excitement. Early on, Auty put Batley ahead with a grand try, which Sutcliffe failed to improve. Then Smith placed a penalty for Pontefract, and Ward dropped a clever goal from a pass by Booth. In the second half, Driscoll scored, and Smith landed a goal. Pontefract pressed all the second portion, and won by 9 points to 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HULL v. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>HULL v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>At Hull, before 10.000 spectators. In the first half, Brown got over in the corner for Salford,Lomas failing. Shaw then dribbled over, and Lomas goaled. Parry re- taliated with a try for Hull, Jackson failing. At half- time Salford led by 8 points to 3. After the interval, Shaw,Lomas and Brown got tries for the visitors,Lomas kicking three goals. Jackson scored a penalty goal. Result—Salford, p points ; Hull, 5 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>BARROW N. HALTPAX</head>
        <p>BARROW N. HALTPAX</p>
        <p>At Barrow, before a large gate. Halifax started, and soon put pressure on the Barrow line. Fox scored for Barrow before half-time. In the second half, Halifax asseqed themselves, and Morley, Williams, and Hadwen scored, Morley converting one. Barrow now pressed, and Wilson scored, Mellor failing. Result—Halifax, 11 points; Barrow, 6 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>HOLBECK V. BRADFORD.</head>
        <p>HOLBECK V. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>At Holbeck, before 10,000 people. Bradford proved themselves much the cleverer team behind, and in the first half Sinton'scored two unimproved tries after cleve,r combination. Bradford had the better of play after- wards, and Heseltine scored, Mosley failing at goal. The game got rough towards the finish, and Eddison (Holbeck) was sent off. Result—Bradford, 3 tries (9 points) ; Holbeck, nil. -LEEDS v. KET.GHLEY.</p>
        <p>Played at Headingley, before 8,000 persons, in fine weather. In the opening half a very fine passing move- ment among the Leeds backs ended in Jenkins scoring, Littlewood landing an easy goal. Though defending for the greater part of the time, Leeds led at the in- terval by 5 points. Afterwards Grace and Andrews scored excellent tries for Leeds, and the Headingley men gained an easy victory. Result—Leeds, 13 points; Keighley, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " v. BROUGHTON !RANGERS "A."</p>
        <p>This postponed match was played on the ground of the Salford Trinity Club on Saturday. The first half was of an even nature, and nothing tangible was re- corded by either side. Turning round, the game opened fast and Kenyon and Mahoney made several smart runs for the visitors. Short was hurt,and took no further part in the game. Eventually the Rangers took up a strong position, and after passing between Houghton and Kiley, the ball was sent across to Bailey, who scored. Houghton next kicked a goal and from a scramble near the close, Bailey recorded a second try, Which Worth- ington improved upon. Final: Broughton Rangers, two goals two tries (ten points); Warrington, nil.</p>
        <p>The workmen employed at the Maryport Flour Mill, owned by Messrs. Carr, biscuit manufacturers, Carlisle, on Wednesday received a week's notice that the mill will be closed when the presPntl stock of wheat is ground. The manager declined to give any reason. About 60 workmen are affected, together with casual dock labourers. The mill supplies much of the flour used in West Cumberland, and has been an important industrial element in Maryport.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>No Breakfast Table Complete without</head>
        <p>No Breakfast Table Complete without</p>
        <p>ws+ 7,2-7 t r</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-CW:4, FORTING:</p>
        <p>4... , Fr. • it,. A</p>
        <p>The Most Nutritious and Economical.</p>
      </div>
