      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SUPPLY AND TRAINING OF PUPIL TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>SUPPLY AND TRAINING OF PUPIL TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>How the Demand is to ha Met in Warrington.</p>
        <p>This important subject is ably dealt with in a report presented to the Education Committee by Mr. J. Moore Murray, secretary!and director, and adopted at the meeting on Monday evening. The following are some of the principal suggestions contained in the report It is desirable that advantage should be taken of the suggestions contained in the circular letter from the Board of Eduction,and an advertisement issued inviting applications to sit at the forthcoming examination. T hose candidates who are successful would commence their apprenticeship in August, 1904, or August,l9os. Those who are successful in passing the examination, but are not to be apprenticed until 1905, will attend the preparatory classes for twelve months, and be paid £5. Twenty-eight scholarships are to be offered for com- petition at a local examination to be held in June. Eight of these scholarships should be reserved for boys , and twenty for girls, who will be 14 years of age before the Ist August, 1904, These scholarships will be awarded , upon the same conditions as those of last year. It is doubtful whether many candidates will be found in the elementary schools unless the Committee can obtain the hearty co-operation of the head teachers. Ma.ty of the latter have realised their responsibilities, but there are still some who do not make efforts to encourage their scholars to take up teaching as a liveli- hood. Some improvement might be obtained if the head teachers were appealed to on this matter. It is proposed to issue, at once, a syllabus of the scholarship examination to all head teachers, and it is suggested that Mr. Sinkings, one of His Majesty's inspectors, should be invited to conduct the examination. If these suggestions are adopted, and a sufficient supply of candidates obtained to fill the twenty-eight vacancies, there will be a sufficient number of pupil teachers to meet the demand until 1907, when it is hoped the Grammar and Secondary schools will be able to provide all the pupil teachers required, and so avoid the necessity for preparatory classes. In order that the secondary school may fulfil this expectation, it is necessary to keep up a flow of scholars from the elementary schools. These children should come to the school before their thir- teenth birthday to gain the full benefit of the instruction. His Majesty's Inspector has already stated that the staff of the school should be increased, as the classes are too large. There is little possibility of any of the present scholars leaving at the end of the year, and admission has been refused to several since the school opened. The removal of the offices to Sankey-street will liberate two rooms which can accom- modate another section of twens,y-eight children. Little change is necessary in the training of the pupil teachers at the classes. Arrangements must be made so that they may receive some kind of physical drill. It is desirable that some change should be made in the exist- ing system of practical training. The placing of pupil teachers in schools, regardless of the qualifications of the staff and the equipment,should no longer be allowed. This side of the pupil teachers' training is of equal im- portance to the theoretical, and demands as careful oversight. If is suggested that certain schools be selected for this purpose, regard being had to the different interests. One school of each type (mixed, boys', girls', and infants') for each denomination would probably be sufficient. Some action should be taken with reference to Training College accommodation,and it is suggested that the Lancashire County Committee be asked whether it is their intention to proceed further n this matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>VALUABLE GIFT.</head>
        <p>A VALUABLE GIFT.</p>
        <p>Every reader who is musically inclined will be glad of the gift of new music folio ! This folio measures 11 inches by 8i inches, has 14 pages of clearly printed music from engraved plates, a coloured cover, and undoubtedly makes a welcome addition to any collec- tion of music. Every composition it contains has been specially composed, is entirely original, and will not be published separate 4. This folio or budget has just been produced by the BiJe Bean Co., and they offer to give a copy to every reader of this paper who makes application to them for one, and forwards two penny stamps to pay package and return postage. A coon song, a march, a sacred song, and a waltz' are among the compositions, which are throughout of first quality. On this subject it will be sufficient to point out that the well-known composer, J. Michael Watson (composer of the Coronation March to King Edward VII.) is responsible for two of the longs. All applica- tions for this music are to be posted to the Bile Bean Co.'s Central Depot, Leeds, and this paragraph from the "Warrington Examiner" must be also enclosed. This is certainly a unique offer, which should not be missed by our readers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Medical Officer's Annual Report.</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph Adams, Medical Officer of Health to the Runcorn Rural District Council, has just issued his annual report. The Runcorn Rural District, he states, comprises the whole of the Runcorn Union, with the exception of the Runcorn Urban District, and consists of 40 townships. The district area comprises 49,309 acres. At the 1891 census, the population was 24.665. At the 1901 census, the population was 23,203. A de- crease, in ten years had thus taken place of 1,462, even with the addition of the three townships of Latchford Without, Grappenhall, and Thelwall. He estimates the population, to the middle of 1903, to be about 23,871. During the year 1903, 654 births, and 320 deaths (the same number as in 1902), were registered as having occurred within the district, compared with an average for the preceding ten years of 650 births,anci 329 deaths. Fourteen deaths occurred in Dutton Workhouse and Kingswood Sanatorium of persons not belonging to the district. Two persons from the district died in public institutions beyond its borders, so that the net total of deaths belonging to the district was 308. Comparing births and deaths in 1903, with the averages of the pre- ceding ten years. there is an increase of four births, and a decrease of 41 deaths on the averages. The net total of deaths has only once been lower, in 1901, when the net total was 300; last year there were 311. The death rate per 1,000 of estimated population is the lowest yet recorded, 12.9 per 1,000. In 1901, the death rate was equally low; it is 2.3 per 1,000 below the average for the preceding ten years. The birth-rate was equal to 27,4 per 1,000, being a very slight increase on that of last year-27•3 per 1,000. It is the highest birth rate since 1896. Zymotic diseases accounted for 36 deaths, including 11 from whooping cough. During the year 124 cases of infectious diseases have been notified, which is below the average.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>SMALLPDX OUTBREAK.</head>
        <p>THE SMALLPDX OUTBREAK.</p>
        <p>The year 1902 closed with every expectatioa of an invasion of smallpox from some adjacent district, and 1903 had scarcely begun when this expectation was fully realised. On January 3rd the first' case was notified, from Ellesmere-road, Stockton Heath. The Smallpox Hospital at Moore had been overhauled and rendered fit for the immediate reception of patients, not having been used since the outbreak in 1893. Altogether 11 cases wereadrnittedto hospital, but no death tookplace. The hospital was open for 23 weeks. The disease still occurs in adjacent areas, states Dr. Adams, and we can only hope it will not re-appear in your district in .1904. Several false alarms have occurred, when investigation has demonstrated the cases to be chicken-pox. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>THELWALL NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE THELWALL NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Reporting on this subject the doctor states :—ln last year's report, referring to the " Alleged Tannery Nuis- ance" and its alleged causation of diphtheria an€l numerous deaths from this disease, I pointed out that the whole subject had been referred to Professor Sheridan Del‘pine, Director of Public Health Labora- tory, Owen's College, Manchester, for him to examine and report upon. This report was presented early in 1903. and effectually disposes of the accusations laid against the tannery, while corroborating the views ex- pressed by me in a long series of special reports which, from time to time, I have laid before you.</p>
        <p>WATER. SUPPLIES</p>
        <p>. For a rural area, the district is exceptionally well supplied from excellent pub lie sources, derived partly from adjoining urban supplies, and partly from Water Works constructed ' within the district itself. The Warrington Corporation Water Works has extended its mains into the District so far as now to supply the whole of Stockton Heatii*Walton Inferior, with the ex- ception of a few houses, Walton Superior, Moore, a portion of Acton Grange, the greater part of Daresbury, Latchford Without,Thelwall,and a portion of Grappenhall The remaining portion of Grappenhall is supplied by a private water works, belonging to one of the principle owners and erected at his own cost. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>SEWAGE PURIFICATION.</head>
        <p>SEWAGE PURIFICATION.</p>
        <p>After alluding to the Helsby sewerage scheme which has been completed, and to the proposed scheme for Halton, Dr. Adams states :—I would again request your observation of the continued improvements which are being made in methods of sewage purification, and im- press upon you the desirability of adopting modern methods wherever practicable, and of bringing your already existing methods of sewemge disposal into line with modern requirements. Extensive sewage works are not applicable to rural districts, but much good work might be accomplishtd by the construction of small in- stallations of septic tanks and filter beds, or other de- vices for purification of sewage before it is allowed to discharge into streams and ditches.</p>
        <p>The fire at Eton College last Whitsuntide, in which two lives were lost, has caused the College authorities to present the District Council with a steam fire engine, while the Council has decided to borrow £5,000 to build a new fire station, communicating electrically with the College. At the Local Government Board injuiry on Wednesday it was stated that the Council had already had the latest system of electrical communicae tion installed at the residence of each member of the fire brigade at a cost of about £l,OOO, the Eton Colleg authorities contributing £6OO.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said Mr. Pilling was absent from two meet- ings in June. Mr. Pilling asserted that he was not. The Clerk referred to the register of attendances at the Board of Guardians meetings, and said Mr. Pilling was absent from two meetings in June. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>. Rheumatism, - Bronchitis, Coldg, ,•.? Strains, and • Tired Muscles IN TUBES inf. ~ , • ‘-'• : - -</p>
        <p>7; Baguley, nil; Eaton, 3; Billington, 8; Chadwick, 11. Mr. Pilling said the figures were not correct. He had not missed one attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Interesting Business.</p>
        <p>This Council held its monthly meeting at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. S. Woodward presiding. There were also present :—Messrs. G. Pilling, W. Mclntyre, C. E. Linaker, J. L. Jones, J. Mounfield,C. R oylance, J. Spruce, J. Edwards, T. Wright, A. G. Guest, T. Darlington, H. Mawdsley, J. Bolshaw, E. Clarke, James Whitlow, T. Lewis, E. Bretherton, Niekson, R. Billington, and T. Antwis, with Dr. Adams (medical officer of health),Messrs. G. F. Ashton (clerk) W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyor), and J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances).</p>
        <p>_ A "BIIRDiN" ON HELsBr. Mr. A. G. Guest made complaints of alleged serious defects in the Helsby sewerage scheme. The Chairman said the matter was urgent ana stould be dealt with as soon as possible. Mr. Linaker moved that Mr. Guest's complaints be set out seriatim and forwarded to the Helsby Parochial Committee, with a request that they would.send a reply to the charges, in time for consideration at a special meeting of the Council a fortnight hence. The matter reflected very great want of foresight on the part 9f some person or persons, and Helsby would have a very serious burden on its shoulders for many years to come. This course was agreed to. THE LIGHTING I,E, WESTON. It was reported that the Weston Parochial Committee had approved the recommendation of the Lighting Sub- committee that, provided satisfactory terms could be arranged with the Runcorn Gas Company, gas lighting be adopted in the parish.</p>
        <p>A NORTON IMPROVEMENT. A letter was read from Mr. J. S. Jeacock, clerk to the Norton Parish Council, stating that at the Council's last meeting a resolution was passed asking the District Council to permit the large tit on the road leading from Norton village to Walton Green to be filled up. It was in a bad state, unsightly and unsanitary. The soil from the new water gipes which would run close to the pit could be used to fill it up. Mr. Wright described the pit as a nuisance, and said farmers and other residents in the village were very anxions that it should be filled up. The Council granted permission for the pit to be filled up. NORLEY WATER SCHEME-LATE OPPOSITION. Messrs. W. Timmins and Sons, Runcorn, wrote sub- mitting approximate cost for pipes, mains,valves,meters and chamber in connection with the Norley Water Supply. They had visited the site and laid down on a 25 inch ordn- ance map thearrangements of mains, valves, and meter chamber. They had also seen the deputy water engineer of the Liverpool Corporation respecting the junction with the Vyrnwy water mains and the most suitable site for meter subject to the Head Water Engineer's (Mr Parry's) approval, who would no doubt fix their own meter and connection as before. The cost of the under- taking will be approximately as follows :—Cost of 3in. connection, with 3in. " Kent " water meter, sluice valves and chamber, £45; 1,178 yards of Bin. mains with 41n. screw-down hydrant, surface boxes, wall plates, and special pipe connections, £225 35.; engineer's charges and expenses, £l5 155.; total, £285 18s. Mr. Nickson moved that the scheme be not approved He contended that the majority of the householders concerned were "dead against" the proposed supply. There were already nine tanks and three public wells and it was unfair that the whole parish should have to contribute to a supply for a limited number of houses. The Clerk said the loan that would have to be ob- tained would be chargeable to Norley. but the water rates received would go towards the payment of prin- ciple and interest. If there was a deficiency the parish would be charged with it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said it was rather late in the day for Norley to come with an objection of that description. A considerable portion of their time had been occupied by the water question, and he had protested again and again against the non-appearance of opposition to the scheme from Norley. They had gone to the trouble of negotiat- ing with the Liverpool Corporation and getting the best terms possible, and plans had even been prepared, but now they were met with an objection from the representative of Norley who asked them to throw out the scheme. It was very pertinent that the Council should at least ask Norley what they were going to do in face of the Medical Officer's and Local Government Board's reports that the parish had been badly supplied with water for years past. If the Norley people could establish sound opposition to the scheme they would have an opportunity of doing so at the Local Governments Board inquiry. It was decided that the scheme be approved and placed before the Local Government Board in order that an inquiry could be held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PRESTON- O'I7WHILL AND DUTTON WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>PRESTON- O'I7WHILL AND DUTTON WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The Council sealed the contract with Messrs. Bennie and Thompson, of Warrington, for the laying of mains to the parishes of Preston o'th'hill and Dutton. MR. LINARER AND THE SURVEYOR'S DUTIES. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Notice had been given by MrTlinalier. -th;twlie—v;ould submit the following suggestions :—(1) All highway and petty cash accounts be in future paid through the Clerk's office, and the surveyors be relieved of all book- keeping; (2) all sewerage matters, except house con- nections, be taken out of the surveyor's hands and trans- ferred to the inspector of nuisances who has more time to devote to them ' • (3) new works of sewerage to be in future prepared by outside engineers or surveyors. Mr. Linaker said that as that was the last meeting of the Council as at present constituted, and the matter would probably elicit a great deal of discussion, he would ask permission to allow it to stand over for con- sideration by the new Council.</p>
        <p>This suggestion was agreed to. TILE MEDICAL OFFICER OF HEATH.</p>
        <p>On the motion- of .IC-1; Wright, seconded by Mr. Roylance, the Council approved the re-appointment of Dr. Adams as medical oificer of health at a salary of 1150.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ESTIMATES.</head>
        <p>THE ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>t Mr. Wright submitted the half-yearly financial state- ment and estimate. The total Qapital for the current half year was £5,624 ; prQbable expenditure, £4,294 ; leaving a balance of £1,330. Probable expenditure during the next half-year was :—General expenses (public health), £666; highway expenses, £3,309; balance and contingency, £1,328 • total, £5,304. Deducting the balance in hand the total amount required from the overseers was £3.974. The asesssable value of the district was £160,011, the rate of 50. would produce £3,663 and a call for £3,974 was recommended. The Council approved the estimates and also a pro- posal by Mr. Linaker that in future, copies of the estimates be forwarded to the members of the Council a week before they are brought forward.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL STATEMENT. Mr. Wright reported that the balance in the hands of the treasurer was £462 17s. 10d.</p>
        <p>1 The following parishes had not paid the calls due on February 13th:—Alvanley,Antrobus, Dutton, Frodsham, Keckwick Kingswood, and Seven Oaks. Mr. Linaker said he had that morning signed a cheque for the Frodsham call. It was a monstrous thing that i townships should be behind in paying the calls. I It was decided to send letters to the overseers calling i their attention to the non-payment of the amounts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ATTENDANCES OF MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>ATTENDANCES OF MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the attendances of members during the year, out of the maximum of 17, had beenas follows :—Messrs. Roylance, 9; Greenway, 8; Bolshaw, 10; Knowles. 2; Talbot, 0; Clarke, 16; Mclntyre. 9; Stokes, 8; Whitlow, 4; Darlington, 16; Baxter, 13; Linaker, 16; Williams, 10; Lewis, 13; Bradford, 8; Wright, 16; Mawdsley, 7; Collier, 9; Woodward, 16; Ellison, 0 ; Pilling, 15 ; Horton, 1; Bretherton, 5 ; Edwards, 14; Nickson, 4; Spruce, 14; Davenport, 1;• Wallington, 1; Admiral Jones Parry, 9; Mounfield, 13' Turner,2; Guest, 13; Antwis, 10 ; Jones,since December,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT:</head>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT:</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported two cases of scarlet fever at Bartington and Dutton respectively. Several cases of chicken-pox had been notified from ApPlAton, Stretton. and Whitley Superior. .The Vicar of ppleton had written asking that Appleton sglool should be closed, as 18 out of the 73 scholars were absent through illness, chiefly measles. He (the doctor) would visit the school and close it if necessary. Weston school had been closed owing, it was stated, to the prevalence of influenza, and re-opetied again without any authority from him.</p>
        <p>THE NEW ISOLATION HOSPITAL. The Isolation Hospital Committee recommended that Mr. John Eyes, of Hartford, be appointed clerk of works to superintend the erection of the isolation ho.pital at Dutton. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Council deferred its decision for a fortnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>"I WILL HAVE VI-COCOA."</head>
        <p>"I WILL HAVE VI-COCOA."</p>
        <p>Your grocer and every other grocer, too, can tell you of men and women who week after week regularly use Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and who, to use a common term "swear by it" as a nourishing and stimulating Food Beverage.</p>
        <p>Are you a regular drinker of Vi-Cocoa? If not, why not?</p>
        <p>Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, by taking the place of stimu- lants of an ardent nature, has made happy many a home which otherwise to-day would have been wretched, and the ultimate effects of which it is impossible to foresee. For breakfast, dinner, supper—indeed at any hour of the twenty-four—Dr Tibbles, Vi-Cocoa will be found light and refreshing; it leads the way among all bever- ages, and nothing can equal it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Free Church United Communion Service.</p>
        <p>The annual interchange of pulpits arranged by the Free Church Council took place on Sunday. A united communion service was held in Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening, the Rev. G. H. Carnson pre- siding over a good attendance. An appropriate address was delivered by the Rev. John Yonge. Local Confirmation Services.</p>
        <p>The Lord Bishop of Liverpool conducted confirmation services at the Parish Church, Warlington,and at Christ Church,Padgate,on Saturday. The service at the Parish Church was held in the afternoon, in the presence of a large congregation. About 150 candidates received the holy rite, and after the laying on of hands the Bishop delivered a helpful address.—His lordship attended at Padgate Church in the evening,and confirmed 94 persons including candidates from Padgate, the Industrial School, Orford, Woolston, Croft; and Hollinfare.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>St. Peter's Men's Service.</head>
        <p>St. Peter's Men's Service.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the quarterly a open Sunday" in connection with the men's service which was held in St. Peter's Church, Birchall-street, on Sun- day afternoon. Mr. T. S. Steel presided and the ad- dress was given by the Rev. D. R. James (curate). Miss Eckersley effectively sang the two sacred solos "0 Divine Redeemer " and " I will give you rest,"and Miss Maud Hill creditably rendered "There is a green hill." Mr. W. R. Roberts was heard to advantage in" When I survey the wondrous Cross" and "The heavenly rest." Mr. Handley's Modern English Concertina Band gave two selections, "The Hallelujah Chorus" and "The Gloria," in pleasing style. The offertory was on be- half of the funds of the Infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual meetirg was hold on Sunday, when there was a good attendance. Prizes to the number of 882 were distributed. Many of the books chosen by the members were of an exceptionally good standard, and the committee had worked hard to get the books ready, at an early date.—Mr. J. Starkey, a Brother of the P.S.A.' presided. Mr. A. Scriven, at short notice, con- sented to fill a gap, as the speaker appointed could not come. He spoke upon "How to get rid of the evil within us." Sins and evil, he said, were great enemies, and nothing short of the grace of God, the help of God, and the power of God was sufficient to deliver us. Thirteen new members joined, making the total mem- bership 449. Master Pearce rendered a solo on the organ. Mr. D. Owen and F. Morrall took part in the meeting. The usual welcome was given to the new members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Services for the People.</head>
        <p>Services for the People.</p>
        <p>The last of the present series of services at the Royal Court Theatre was held on Sunday evening last, when the Rev. H. Dunnico again preached, his text being taken from Psalms 27, 13 :" I had fainted unless I had believed to see the goodness of God." This was divided into two portions in the form of questions, viz: (1) Was the Psalmist justified in believing in the goodness of God? and (2) Is not a belief in the goodness of God the only thing that can save a man from pessimism and despair? Most men•believed in the existence of God, but many people could not reconcile all the sin, suffering and misery in the world with the idea of a good God. Every kind word, every good deed and every act of self-sacrifice revealed in men and women were but fleeting expressions of the love and goodness of God, and served to show that behind all the phenomena of life there reigned a good God and a kind Father. Coming to the second point of the sermon, the preacher stated that no one could face the facts of life as they were to be found at the present day without being driven into pessimism, unless he believed in the goodness of God. After denouncing some of the evils of the time the Rev. H. Dunnico stated that the only thing that could enable a man to live a better life was the redemptive grace of God, as revealed in Christ, and closed with an earnest appeal to his hearers to take their stand beneath the Cross of Christ and spend their lives in doing good, as Christ had spent His. The service on Sunday will be held in Parr Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Emmanuel (Reformen) Church,</head>
        <p>Emmanuel (Reformen) Church,</p>
        <p>On Thursday a congregational social evening was.. spent in the school-room, to welcome the newly- appointed minister, the Rev. F. Vaughan. Mr. Thomas Allen and Mr. NV. H. Dickens (church wardens), and the Rev. J. R. Parker (incumbent of Chrish Church, R.C.E., Tuebrook) gave a hearty word of welcome to Mr. and Mrs. Vaughan and family, and a letter from the Rev. J. F. Matthews was read expressing regret that the annual meeting under the auspises of the Free Church Council prevented himself and colleagues from being present to offer to Mr. Vaughan that welcome which they desired to extend to him. Mr. Vaughan feelingly thanked the meeting and members of the congregation. for the very kind reception they had given him and his wife and in r3viewing past work and present aims referred to the principles of the 'Reformed Church which he hoped to be able under God and with the prayers and assistance V the church workers to make wider known to the extension of the Church and the upbuilding of its members in their most holy faith. Advantage was taken of the occasion to make a presenta- tion to Mrs. A Landon of a set of beautifully embossed electro-silver stands, as an appreciation of her long and faithful service as organist. Mr. Dickens made the presentation in the name of the congregation and Mrs. Landon who was completely surprised and overcome tendered her thanks for the handsome present. A happy musical evening was passed which was brought to a. conclusion by singing the Doxology.</p>
      </div>
