      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>5-4',XANIINEE4</head>
        <p>THE 5-4',XANIINEE4</p>
        <p>MARCH 26, 1904</p>
        <p>LORD STANLEY Of THE EDUCATION Alderley, speaking at the PROBLEM:. meeting of the National Education Association on Wednesday, said that there is a time for com- promise and a time not to compromise. " This," he added, "is not a time for compromising. When going into action we should make up our minds to beat the enemy and then dictate our terms." These are wise words. Almost identical expressions were used by Dc. CLIFFORD in his powerful speech on Wednesday night at Plymouth. Lord STANLEY is one of the gteatest educational exports in the, country; Dz. CLIFFORD is the acknowledg3d leader of the opposition to the Education Act. A policy stamped with the imprimatur of their joint authority must carry great weight. Conaproinise is often only another name for weakness. There are vital principles at stake in this educational struggle on which com- promise is impossible. By that we do not say that the Education Acts of 1901 and 1903 require ti be annulled from preamble to imprint. For good or ill we must accept the now machinery ; but it is necessary to change its impulse and dismiss some of its drivers, There are, as was pointed out b y LORD STANLEY of Alderley, two points on which compromise is out of the question. First of all, we must give to the people absolute control over all schools maintained out of public funds. The present unnatural divorce between control and expenditure offends against every sound maxim of local governmeot. Given unrestricted popular control, religious tests and disabilities 'instantly disappear. in the second place, schools maintained out of the public funds, and to which children of all creeds are liable to be soot under cpmpulsion must be in character and eisance, national and not ecclesiastical schools. Those are vital prin- ciples on which there can be no surrender.</p>
        <p>Dr. CLIFFORD spoke strongly against "right of entry" by religious teachers of the various denom- inations as a solution of the difficulty as to religi- ous teaching. In that attitude he is fortified by the valuable opinion of Sir GEORGE KEKENVICH. Another great educationist and sound Churchman the late Sir JOiIIGA FITCFI, protested almost passionately against "right of entry." Doubtless it might be so applied as to afford every denomination opporuunity in all schools to impart its distinctive teaching to children of its own faith. There is a certain appearanciii of fair- ness in this proposal which appeals to many par- sons unfamiliar with the details of school life. But in operation it would seriously interfere with that corporate life of the school to which every experienced teacher attaches the greatest import- ance. And who would think without a pang, of little children being separated into groups and class-rooms for distinctive teaching, familiarized at the vary dawn of their intelligence with the clash Of sects, the divisions, tile intolerances, and the rivalries of religious denominations ? The right of entry would perhaps be fair to the denomi_ nations, but it would be fearfully unjust to the children.</p>
        <p>Ministers succeeded CHINESE LABOUR. on Monday night in upholding the Chi- nese Labour Ordinance in the division lobby, but they were unable to justify it In debate. It may be that the Chinamen who are to be imported into the Transvaal will not be technically slaves, but most certainly they will not be free men. Indentured labour has been known before in the British Empire, but never before under our free flag have men been submitted to the degrading conditions which are to be imposed on the yellow men who are corning in to :enrich the Transvaal plutocracy. We are strongly opposed to the im- portation of Chinamen on racial, ethical, and econ- omic grounds, and we cannot accept the dictum of the Archbishop of Canterbury that their mportation is "a necessity though a regret- table one." We deplore the argument from neces- sity in such a case as this. Bet even from this low standpoint, the Government failed to make out a justification for their policy. Mr. LYTTEL- , TON recited the efforts of the mine owners to ob- tain black labour in various parts of Africa. He made out no case whatever for the refusal to em- ploy the labour of Indian coolies. If the con- ditions imposed by the Indian Government had been accepted, the whole problem might have been solved by this time, and the importations of Chinese labour, so intensely distasteful to the people of these islands,and the colonies of Australia and Nes Zealand would have been averted. Why this course was not taken nobody knows. But what we do know is that the Indian Government refused to permit the emigration of British Asiatic coolies to the Transvaal on the terms specified by Lord MILNER and his Council. This point was put very forcibly in the "Times " by Sir HARRY JOHNSTON, who cannot be accused of taking very narrow views of Colonial development. He holds that if the mines cannot be worked by white labour the Indian coolies should be admitted without restrictions. " Let us throw the country open to the Asiatic, prefer- ably to the natives of British India, or of any other portion of the Asiatic doirinions of the British Crown. But in that case let the native of India or any other type of Asiatic whom we may agree to regard as a desirable settler, come there as a free citizen, free to go where he will, and engage in whatever avocation he pleases. In short let us offer to the patives of Luria those conditions of residence in the Transvaal of which the Indian Government would approve." The adoption of this policy would have spared us ad the turmoi I provoked by the action of Mr. LYTTELTON. There would have been no such objection to the settle- ment of Indian coolies in the C Amy, as every- body, the Government included, makes to the settlement of Chinese. The Labour Ordinance would then have been relieved from its most arduous restrictions.</p>
        <p>The Education Committee has an earnest, enthu- siastic chairman in Mr. F. Monks, who may congratulate himself upon successfuly piloting the committee though the numerous difficulties attendant upon the sweeping changes which have been made during the past; twAve months in the local educational system. There was a time when matters did not run smoothly and Mr. Monks re- signed his poßition, but experienc,‘i has proved the wisdom of those members who took prompt steps to induce Mr. Monks to reconsider his decision and succeeded in their object. All thought- ful citizens will peruse carefully his review of the committee's first year's work, a report of which will be found in another column. Not the least interesting portion is tnat dealing with the financial aspect of the situation. Mr. Monks is not a believer in cheap economy. On the contrary he is firmly convinced that money well spent wil bring a satisfactory return, and he is prepared to boldly face the large capital outlay which is nec3ssary to supply the deficient school accom- modation. Whether the ratepayers will do so with equanimity is another matter. He estimates that the expenditure under this head will be some- thing like £40,000 during the next two years, the interest and sinking fund of which will make a call on thp rates of .21d, in the E. Mr. Monks seems dis- posed to minimise the workingman's proportion of this a3ditional cost by ',showing that his total con- tribution will be a little less than one third of the whole amount accruing from rateable sgiurces. Nevertheless the: small property-owner and shop- keeper, has the solid fact before him that another 21c1. on every pound of his rateable value will shortly be demanded. Education is indeed a heavy responsibility, and one that can neither be lightly faced nor shirked!</p>
        <p>A second sale of 10:al passive resistors' goods seized on distraint took place in Warrington on Thursday, and after the necessary sum to satisfy the rates had been realised, the usual impromptu meeting was held and the Education Act de- nounced and defied in uncompromising terms. The Rev. J. F. Matthews affirmed his disbelief and disapproval of any religious instruction whatever being given in the schools. Toe adoption of a scheme oi purely secular instruction would, per- haps, be the easiest gelation of the religious in struction problem, but in advocating this course the RAT. J. F. Matchews stands almost alone arssong, ministers, and for our own part we should be loth to see all Bible teaching banished from the schools. It is, of coarse, the duty of the 0 iristian churches to give whatever religious instruction the children require, but their opportunities for doing so out- side the day school are necessarily limited. We are very unwilling to abandon the idea—though many broad-minded Churchmen dissent from it entirely—that some basis of Bible instruction could be devised which would be c mimeo to all the Christian Churches, and which would at least give the children a rudimentary idea of religion, and teach them the difference between right and wrong. If no agreement on these lines can be come to between the various Christian denomina- tions then we see no hope of religious teaching in the day schools ever being imparted on equitable and practical lines. The complete abandonment of such instruction will, however, be a serious menace to the righteousness of the nation.</p>
        <p>Warrington electors will, we hope, take to heart the fact that by sending Mr. Robert Pierpoint to represent thorn in Parliament they hairi provided a staunch supporter of the Randlords in their endeavour, backed up by the Government, to introduce Chinese labour into the South African mines under conditions tantamount to slavery. On perusing the division lists we find that at the close of the memorable debate so powerfully led by "0.-B." on Monday, Mr. Pierpoint once more gave his vote in favour of the discreditable ordinance. There are other familiar local names in the" Pro-Chinese " list, including those of the Ron. Alan de Tattou Egerton and Cononel R. Pilk'ngton. No doubt these gentlemen will hear more a,bout their action at the next election. The Warrington Free Cimrch Council has voiced the general indignation at the conduct of the Governmeot by a wiaiii•nous reso- lution, Any proposal which interrferes with indi- vidual freedom should be a m Atter oi spicial con- cern to the chorches nod we are pleased that the representative-3 of local Nonconformity have ex- pressed their rEpugnance of indentured YelloW labour on the R titd.</p>
        <p>Aldermanie honours have their drawbacks as well as glamour. A paragraph in last night's papers„ alludes to " the traditional aiderrnanic spirit being displayed in the London Court of C•ommort Council during the consideration of the Ernplor ment of Children Act. Alderman Sir Thomo</p>
        <p>Brooke-Hitching is credited with a speech in support of a motion to reduce the age limit from 12 to 11 years. "He did not believe that going to work early in life Was deleterious to awhild. In the interests of the Children themselves he did not think that it was advisable to curtail their hours of labour too much." 4`Great injustice was done,"said this children'sladvo- nate, "by too much interference on the part of the State with the hours of children in the mills in Yorkshire. There were no such hampering ~_restrictions in the mills of our competitors abroad." The worthy aldetman with the double-barrelled name is right in one particular—that stated in his Concluding sentence. One can readily recall des- scriptions of the aborniLable child-labour in the tJnited States by'which children of tender years are Worked till they fall asleep at the looms and sus- tain permanent physical injury. We should be sorry indeed to think that Sir Thomas Brooke- Ilitching's remarks exemplified "the traditional aldermanic spirit." Then surely would the ald-T- --men be a curse to their generation! It is more Pleasing to reflect that the motion raised a "storm of Protest" and was rejected. What we regret most is the fact that the ratepayers have no oppor- tunity of rejecting Sir Thomas Brooke-Hitching!</p>
        <p>There is a remarkable feature of the local Council and Guardians elections at Earlestown, Where four publicans are opposed by four teetotallers. Tne local temperance party are taking active steps to secure the return of the teetotallers, and they have certainly a unique opportunity, together with a great incentive for hard work. we are not aware how much influence "the tra le" Posseeses in the Earlestown district, but if an attempt is being made tcb fill each vacancy on the local governing authorities with a publican' then the outlook is serious. The genial Boniface is Often enough most estimable as a man, but he is seldom a free agent, and it should be the very last desire of the ratepayers to be represented by moo Whose hands are tied in any way. We hope the arlestown voters will have more sense than return four nominbes of" the trade 'S which loolu, after its own interest first, and that of the rate- Pays only when it can make a virtue of doing so.</p>
        <p>A paragraph to which the " Times" gave con- eiderable prominence the other day was of some I.nterest as illustrating the growth of the engineer- -1,,, m engineer- ing industry in Grant Britain. The fir. of Graham, Morton, and Co., of Leeds, have Just erected large works at Hunslet which alreauy give employment to 350 men, and bid fair to become Aell.e of the most important concerns in. the dis- rict. The speciality of the firm ict colliery and gas-making plant, and it appears that this class of ',.luachinery is beim?, carrind to such a pitch of per- lection that orders are now being executed for all Pa.rts of the world. Li particular it will be noted 1771 th great satisfaction .that a contract for the I:4:ns gas-works of the value of £60,000 is now ~eing carried out. When the London, County lajouncil placed an order for ster4 tramway rails in eigium, Nit.. Chamberlain declared that Britisa Lab.nur was being deprived of employment in pur- ou. of Free-Trado ideas. But it one Paris autho- ritiei had len guided by similar views British engineers would have lost this important order.</p>
      </div>
