      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Personal.</head>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>The Rev. John Yonge has been appointed to repre- sent the School Managetnent Committee of the orough on the Council of the Society for University Extension in Liverpool.</p>
        <p>We regret to learn that Mr. John Harrison, who for 80 many yeas filled the office ot elective auditor, is seriously ill at his residence in Egypt-street. He has .been ailing for some tin].) but his condition was ag- gravated this week by an unfortunate fall. We understand that Mr. A. H. Crosfield's team, Playing at Cannes this week, has won the golf trophy offered by the Grand Duke Michael of Russia.</p>
        <p>- Coming Events. Rev. H. Dunnico will preach in the Parr Hall tolnorrow night on" The Church and Modern Social Problems." A. choir of 60 voices will lead the sing- ing, and Mr. Frank Hickman AIM give an organ recital Previous to the service. At Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church to-morrow (Sunday) evening the choir will render the musical service "The way of the Cross." An open meeting of Wycliffe P.S.A. Society will be ileld to-morrow (Sunday) afternoon under the presid- ency of Mr. T. Birchall, J.P. Stainer's oratorio "The Crucifixion" will, be tendered at Wycliffe Church, to-morrow (Sunday.) With the object of raising funds to relieve local distress a concert will be held in the Parr Hall on Monday evening.. „The foreign missionary anniversary in connection vutil Bold-street Wnsleyan Church will be celebrated -morrow (Sunday). On Monday evening the Rev. -"'• - Wilki ens R es will lecture on "Japan and the an aap ese. ' The Rev. Henry H. Barley, from Cal- ritta, will preach at a service on Good Friday morn- g, and in the evening a public meeting will be held. Industrial Exhibition at Bold-street. A. successful industrial exhibition has been held in 4601d-street Wesleyan School this week and the pro- ceeds, in aid of the Society funds, will amount to 1-I_Pwards of £25. The opening ceremony took place lull:. Wednesday afternoon, Mr. George Bailey, one of j.e society stewards, presiding in the absence of Mr. 0 B. Isaac. Mr. J. B. Withinshaw, of Penketh, wPenecl the exhibition and encouraged the church _orkers'th do their utmost to further any objects that ;ere for the good of the church. A. vote of thanks .thas accorded to Mr. Withinshaw on the proposition of rae Rev. W. C. J. Neville, seconded by Mr. W. Hick- Bessie Dutton of Moore, re-opened the Zibition on Thursday and dolivered interesting add On Mr. George Weaver (senior circuit steward) -V_eosided. Miss Dutton was cordially thanked on the V; Position of Mr. J. C. Holland, seconded by Mrs. .:v• j• Dunn. The exhibition was again open last • 'lig. There have been numerous attractions, anclud. 4. lug competitions, and all have been well UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. .r.Fson CLASS AND CHINA. of every description try • GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 'JD, Bridge -street. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>a a lOU</p>
