      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Richard Toft's Will. The will of the late Mr. Richard Toft, tobacconist,of Horsemarket-street and Sankey street, has just been. proved. Mr. Toft, who died on the 20th February-, has left estate valued at £3,539.</p>
        <p>Warrington District Bowling League. At a meeting held at the BoAlands Elotel on Thursday in connection with this league, it was decided that eight clubs should constitute the league. A silver cup has been presented by Mr. A. H. Crosfield to be competed for annually. Medals will be awarded to th%winning team. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS IVIDIERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Billiard Match at the Liberal Club. On Wednesday evening the members of the Ser- geants Mess from Orford Barracks visited the Liberal Club and a billiard matcheight a side, was played. The Liberals won every game and obtained a majority of 263 points. On Tuesday next the old-time fixture with the Conservative Club will be renewed, the first match being held at the Liberal Club. FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Price of Bread. At the adjourned meeting of the Master Bakers Association held on Wednesday evening to take into consideration the price of bread, it was decided that as flour had droppec: a little,no action should be taken to raise the price of bread, unless flour should again take an upward tendency. The public generally no doubt will appreciate the action of the master bakers BA.SSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GA.RLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Canada and Preferential Tariffs. Under the auspices of the Chamber of Commerce a lantern lecture on "The Montreal Congress" was given on Monday afternoon at the Town Hall by Mr. Charles Lancaster of Liverpool. The lecturer made an interesting comparison between Canada and this country in relation to preferential tariffs, and this part of the lecture we hope to have space for next week.</p>
        <p>„—. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER the CLOCK for Bee BOOT ; in LAWCASHIRE.—Advt. Local Distress. At a meeting held at the Town Hall to farther con- sider the advisability of opening a public fund for the relief of distress, the replies of local manufacturers and head teachers were read. It was felt that private charity would be able to meet the necessities of the case without opening a subscription list. No public appeal will therefore be made. The Corpora- tion are endeavouring to find work for some of the most deserving of the unemployed. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING oP • TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. The Cycling Club. The members of the Warringtm Cycling Club enter ' upon the 1931 season to-day when the opening run takes place to Warburton. A. neatly arranged hand • book has been issued by the energetic secretary' (4r. E. Pickavance, jun.), and contains a most attractive programme of runs, bowling mains, fishing matches, Bcc. On Thursday evening .a successful smoker was held at the Patten A.rms Elotel, Mr. D. Hodge, captain, presiding over a company of about a hundred mem: bers anefriends. A. splendid musical programme was submitted, the artistes being : —Messrs. P. Bate, J. M. Bate and A. Fieldhonse (vocalists), Messrs. A.. Grounds and W. J. Williams (humorists), M. W. Whittaker (whistling soloist), and Mr. T. Boothroyd (violinist). Mr. W. H. Kendrick ably accompanied. Tip concert, which was thoroughly enjoyable, closed with a vote of thanks to the artistes.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUITSINGHA.M'S HIGH-CLASS MDTERA. WATERS.—Advt. Death of Mr. Charles ,Sudlow. Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mr. Charles Sudlow, boot and shoe dealer, Win- wick-street, who passed away at his residence on Tuesday. Mr. Sudlocv, who was 65 years of age, suffered from a severe illness at the time of the death of his wife in December last. He never thoroughly regained his strength, and a week last Tuesday caught a chill, pneumonia supervening. Mr. Sudlotv. was well known and respected throughout the town, and at one time took an active part in upholding Liberal principles. He was a familiar figure on various local bowling greens, and was quite an enthusiast in the sport. He was one of the oldest Warrington tradesmen and for a long period had been connected with Orford Barracks in a business capacity. For many years he wa4 a regular worshipper at St. John's Presbyterian Church.—The, funeral took place at the Cemetery yesterday afternoon. In addition to members of the family and relatives, there were present :—The Rev. E. C. E.- Carleton, Dr. Braide, Alderman Bennett. Mr. S. McDowell, Mr. T. Longdin' Mr. W. C. Litton, Sergeant-Major Warriner and Quartermaster-Ser- geant Hollin (King's Liverpool Regiment), Mr, Plunkett (Orford Barracks) and many members of the congregation of St. John's Church, as well as the employees of the deceased. A large number of wreaths were placed over the grave including one from the minister, office bearers and members of St. John's Church, and another from the sergeants of the Bth and 40th Regimental Districts, BaCE BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—Advt . Drowned In Walton Lock.</p>
        <p>Late on Friday night the body of John Philemon Gough, a wiredrawer, who lived with his parents •at 15, Percy-street, was recovered from Walton Lock. The circumstances of his death were inquired intoby Mr. Brighouse and a jury on Monday evening.—Joha Gough, father of the deceased, said his son was 24 years of age and was very fond of boating' on the river.—Thomas Whittle, a plumber, of 53,-Church- street, stated that on Friday night he and his brother and deceased were engaged in doing some work to witness's sailing yacht, which was lying beached near Walton Lock. They had finished their work about 9 o'clock, and the deceased went to return a bucket he had borrowed frpm the lock-keeper's hut. In the meantime, witness locked up the cab nof the yacht, aud had just finished when he heard a stash, and thinking the deceased had fallen into the water he ran along, but saw nothing but a ripple on the water, which was about 12 feet deep. He raised an alarm and called assistance. A life-buoy was thrown out but nothing was seen of the deceased. Grappling was commenced and the body was recovered at 11-5 p.m. —ln answer to a question by the Coroner, witness said the deceased could not 91N 11:11. —A verdict of "Accidental. Death" was returned —The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon many relatives and friends, including several well-known townsmen, witnessing the last rites. A. .large con- tingent of deceased's fellow workmen at Longford Wire Works assembled to pay a tribute of respect. Mr. Smith of Buckley-street Mission, conducted the service. There was a large number of beautiful wreaths, including two from the employees at Long- ford Wire Works.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S lirl'm TONIC is the Kr:sci Toncs.-63. SINKEy-sTR.EET, Sgarrington.—Xdvt.</p>
        <p>ALate Notification of Death. Mr. Brighouse, county coroner,conducted an inquiry on Monday evening, respecting the death of a boy named George Hesketh, aged eight, who resided with his parents at 37, King street, and who died at the Infirmary on Saturday morning.—From the evidence of the father of the boy,who is a shunter in the employ of the London and North Western Railway Company, it appears that about 8.30 on Friday morning, the de- ceased went downstairs in his shirt. The boy's father was in bed, having been working a night turn, and his mother was too unwell to get up. Screams were heard, and on the father going downstairs he found that the boy had run out of the house with his shirt on fire. He snatched the shirt from off the boy's back and put out the flames. After applying oil to the barns deceased was taken to the Infirmary. Witness saw the boy at that institution, and he told him that he would never go near the fire any more."—Nurse Garnett said the deceased was admitted to the Infirm- ary on Friday moraing.—The Coroner (to witness) Do you know how it is I have only just heard about this case ?—Witness : No.—The Coroner : Don't you telephone to the police when any death takes - place ?—Witness : The surgeon does it as a rule. The Coroner's Officer (Mr. Barlow), said that he was informed of the death by telephone at 4.30 the previ- ous afternoon.—The Coroner : Why don't they telephone immediately death takes place ?—The Coroner's Officer said he could not account for that. It was generally six or seven hours after death before he was informed of. the occurrence.—The Qoroner (to the father of the deceased) : Didn't they tell you there was to I,e an inquest ?—Mr. Hesketh : No, sir. They told Me what steps to take, but did not mention the inquest.—A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 26, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 26, 1904</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. GUEST, in seconding the resolution, said he considered it an honour to do so, especially because his father was the only collector for the society for mahy years ia Warrington. His brother succeeded him in the same work, and he himself had the honour for many years of being the secretary of the society. (Hear, hear.) The differences which marred the Church to-day, continued Mr. Guest, would be settled on the platform—the plat- form of the Bible. (Hear, hear.) The 250,000 guineas which the society hoped to secure would, he felt sure, be raised, because. all classes of the community would cheerfully contribute. The resolution was then carried with acclamation. lAN MACLAREN AND THE ROCK OF HOLY</p>
        <p>SCRIPTURE. The Rev. Dr. WATSON, (" lan Maclaren ") moved the following resolution :—"That the past history of the society and its work gives it a permanent claim upon the sympathy and support of all Christian neople and it is hoped that the recognition of that claim will be in future specially manifested in Warrington and the surrounding district." He would like to thank them, in a word, he said, for the hospitable welcome they gave to a Liverpool stranger—(laughter)—when he appeared for the first time on a public platform in their ancient town. Also he wished to say how proud he was to stand face to face with them—citizens of no mean place—(laughter)—as the advocate of the Bible Society. His pleasure was all the greater,when he had the honour of coming there in the company of the Bishop of the Dincese—(applause)—because that surely represented as in living illustration, their deeper unity in the Lord and their common' standing upon the rock of Holy Scripture—(applause)—and he was only telling an open secret when he said that there was rot one of them in Liverpr‘ol outside the range of his lordship's ecclesi- astical authority who did not vie with the members of his own church in admiration for his unceasing labours and affectionate—for that was the word—regard for his character. (Applause.) With the Bishop, indeed, he never imagined he could have any quarrel, and it was only that evening that he had ever had cause to com- plain of him. (Laughter.) As a matter of fact the resolution he had just proposed fell into two clauses, and the first had reference to the past history of the society and its noble work, as constituting a claimupon their sympathy and liberality, but all that he could have said and many things that would not have occurred to his (Dr. Watson's) morelumbering intellect—(laughter) —to imagine, had been said with much greater felicity by the Bishop, so that the first clause of the resolution had been snatched from his mouth by a rapacious pre- late. (Laughter and applause.) Furthermore anything he might have dared to say upon the recognition of the claims of the society by Warrington had been said with much greater weight and candour by his friend Mr. Yonge. (Laughter.) Now, what did remain for a poor Liverpool speaker? Well, at any rate, it remained that he should offer to the chairman and the local committee of this noble sociev his sincere congratulations that another great meeting had been added to the preceding list in celebration of 100 years' successful work; and more than that, in celebration of theundying vitality of the Holy Scripture. (Applause.) When that series of meetings had been concluded) reaching from the south to the north of England, and audience after audience of 1 various denominations and all conditions of societyhal assembled with the enthusiasm they had manifested that night, was there a man who would have the faceto write in public or who would have the face to say in private that the Bible was losing its hold upon the reason and conscience of England? (Hear hear.) He trusted that one result of that centenary celebration would be not only to raise the sum of money desired; not only to rouse up the War- rington people to do better than they had been doing— which of course they could do easily—but that it would, establish the faith of Christian people in the Bible and give grace to timid Christians to strengthen their knees and lift up their hearts and speak with louder voice and bolder face in defence of their faith. (Applause.) One of the great faults of believing people was their extreme modesty and their culpable timidity, because his reading of the history of controversies about the Bible had been that Christian folk were in a chronic state of panic over attacks on the Bible. BIBLE PANICS. _</p>
        <p>In referring to what he called the first panic of modern times, Dr. Watson said it was the outcome of a book, published anonymously, dealing with geology. It created a tremendous sensation at the time. "If this book is correct about the age of the world," it was said, "what about the chronology of the Bible and what about the veracity of Moses." (Laughter.) "They forgot," continued Dr. Watson, "that the chronology is not a part of the Bible at all, and they forgot also that that great man who under God was the founder of Israel was not, in addition to his other accomplishments, a teacher of modern geology." (Hear,hear.) Dc. Watson proceeded to refer to what he called the extreme anxiety which came over Christian folk when Darwin gave the result of his laborious investigations into natural science to the world. It raised the question of whether we could believe any longer in a living and intelligent and active Creator. This was going to the very roots of the matter, and people felt that if they accepted the law of evolution, what of the central faith of all religion, the faith in the Eternal himself? Ho did not know how many books were written from the side of faith against Mr. Dar- win, but he earnestly hoped they had been all burnt long ago. (Hear, hear and laughter.) Mr. Darwin was pursuing legitimate investigation and had rendered enormous service to science, and he had left behindhim, along with Mr. Huxley, a character of entire candour and entire honesty. (Hear, hear.) Christian people, he hoped, had discovered that whether the law of evolution had been perfectly proved or not, or whether it could or could not be accepted as a final edition of what he might call the legislature of nature, it did not conflict with the active power and intelligent will of a Creator. (Hear, hear.) The law implied a force to work it and if the Eternal was able to work by this law or any other law they had only another illustration of his unsearchable wisdom and only another stepping stone by which we passed behind a law charged with marvellous intelligence to the mind and to the heart of God. (Applause.) He came now to a later panic, that resulting from what was called the Higher Criticism. This criticism which was the application of literary criticism to the form of Holy Scripture was unavoidable. He would have been amazed and disappointed if the power of literary criticism that had been brought to bear on classical history and classical literature should not have been exerted in the examination of the sacred scriptures of our religion. After referring to the two kinds of critics, those who came to the scriptures with the pre- supposition,which was entirely unscientific—that miracles were impossible, and those who came with a perfectly open mind, Dr. Watson said he would ask the critics to remember how dear the book was to a multitude, no man could number, and how great had been its history. (Hear, hear). "At the same time," continued Dr. Watson," I say to the critic, whatever learning you have regarding the history of the past, and whatever learning you have regarding the construction of litera- Aare, use it freely. Tell me all you can about the 'history of the book ; tell me all that you can about the construction of the book, and if there is any light thrown upon it by your work I shall be pleased, for the more light thrown upon this book the better for my soul and for the Church. ,(Applause.) If it should be the case that the prophecy of Isaiah has been written, not by one than but by two men, does that take away from me the 53rd chapter of Isaiah, charged with a vision of the saving Messiah, charged with the hope of a sorrowful race for salvation through that Messiah?" (Hear, hear.) Proceeding, Dr. Watson said we might now he on the eve of another panic. He understood that it was not intellectually that the Bible might be attacked, but from the moral side. After alluding to possible objec- tions by critics to the Bible on account of the moral deficiencies of some of the patriarchs,Dr. Watson pointed out that the Christian relirion did not rest on patriarchs or the glory of kings but on the glorious perfection of Christ himself. (Applause.) It was not the literary critic who could appreciate Scripture so much as the man in the back streets of life who had nothing else to help and comfort him. It was he who knew the value of the Bible. In that audience there were doubtless many timid Christians who when they went home would cry in spirit "I always have believed and always will believe initheßible,"but they would ask nervonsly"What about the attacks on it by all those clever people? That would not do. They must look up and say " know in whom I believe." (Hear, hear.) Yes, some of them said" Amen "to that. They must remember that on that very Platform were men of various denomina- tions in the Christian Church whc? were saying "Amen" with them. The saints of the Church in the past, yea, the heroes and martrys of the Church of God wereeven beginning to say "Amen" with them. The word was go- ing back and the apostles rose and said " Amen " too. Men might come and men mirght go : theorists might come anti theorists might go, but the Word of the Lord endnreth forever. (Loud applause.) Mr. WILLTAIVI OWEN brieffv seconded the resolution. He announced as treasurer of the Warrington Auxiliary that he had received about £ll5 towards the special fond. Of that, Mrs. Garnett had given P-50, as had been already stated ; Councillor Dodds had given £5 : and 50 had been received in collections. Mr. David Garnett had also promised .£lO. (Applause). The resolution was carried unanimously. VOTE OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. PF:RCY lqn.cocK nroposed a vote of thanks to the Lord Bishop and Dr. Watson, and said thrutks were also due to Mr. Pai,•^lough, for nmsiiing : to the united choir ; and to-Mr. Randall and Mr. Payton. who had so ably conducted the musical portion of the evening. (Applause). The Rev. and 'lion. C. F. CROSS seconded and the thanks were heartily accorded. A large united choir, composed of members of choirs of all denominations, efficiently sang the anthem "Send out Thy light," and the well-known " Halleluiah " chorus from Handel's "Messiah." Appropriate hymns were also sung. Mr. Percy Randall conducted the singing and H. Payton, Mus. Bac. presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BIBLE.</head>
        <p>THE BIBLE.</p>
        <p>, THE ONE HUNDRED YEARS' WORK I OF THE BIBLE SMIETY.</p>
        <p>Celebration Meeting at Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>In continuation of the local celebration of the centenary of the British and Foreign Bible Society, a large and enthusiastic meeting was held in the Parr Hall on Monday evening. Almost all shades of religi- ous thought in the town were represented and never before has the Bible Society received such a large measure of public support and appreciation. Mr. John Fairdough presided, and he was supported on the platform by the Lord Bishop of Liverpool; the Rev. John Watson, D.D. (lan Maclaren), Revs. Canon Willis, Hon. C. F. Cross, W. Bracecarnp„B. Percival, J. Davies, J. F. Matthews, J. S. Hughes, John Yonge, E. C. E. Carleton, R. Wilkins Rees, G. H. Carnson, J. L. Milne, W. C. J. Neville, H. Dunnico, John Gunnell, W. J. Roberts, J. R. Jones, H. R. Wild, C. I Harvey Cook, T. W. Barber, F. W. E. Chadwick, A. Inglis, D. R. James, J. Gardham Reed, F. Evans, H. Wall, Joseph Howard, D. Kerrin and T. R. Matthews (Earlestown); Alderman Bennett, Councillor Brough; and Messrs. William Owen, S. P. Silcock, E. J. Guest J.P., C. E. Parker, R. E. G-arnett, D. Garnett, Philip C. Garnett, W. Silcock, Thomas Birchall J.P., J. W. Hayward J.P., Francis Dixon, G. Thompson, E. W. Bindloss, G. Wallington, Edward Wallington, A. Conley, J. G. Withinshaw, A. Olaeld, W. Jackson, F. Taylor, Robert Wyllie, Francis Brown, Adjutant Thomas Everness (Salvation Army), Mrs. Broadbent, Miss M. Broadbent, Mrs. Milling, and Miss Pierpoint. A portion of scripture was read by the Rev. G. H. Carnsun, and prayer was offered by the Rev. E. C. • E. Carleton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>WARRINGTON AUXILIARY.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON AUXILIARY.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMII referred to the excellent work of the Bible Society during the first century of its existence. The society was asking this year, he proceeded to state, for a special gift of 250,000 guineas to clear off old debts and to open up new work in fresh quarters, so it had applied to the Warrington Auxiliary for help. He had received a letter from a well-known lady, Mrs. Garnett, of Penketh, enclosing a cheque for £5O as her contribution to the Centenary Fund. (Applause.) She gave it in memory of her husband and his father, to whose heart the claims of the Bible Society were ever dear. (Hear, hear.) .Mrs. Garnett also informed him that Mr. John Guest had sent her Ll. The committee had also appealed to the various places of worship for assistance. Many had done what they could, but he was sorry to say not all, and he was looking forward to that meeting helping very substantially, and also having a good and convincing influence on the people towards the society. (Hear, hear.) The contribution from War- rington last year was only £5O, and it ought to be much more. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Percy Silcock, the secretary, would be glad to give any information that volunteers flr this splendid work might require. (Applause.) WHAT THE SOCIETY HAS DONE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE, quoted a special statement on the work of the society, showing that in the 100 years of its existence it had been the means of circulating over 180 million volumes, and at the present time copies were going forth at an average rate of one in every five seconds. (Hear, hear.) At home the work of the society was still indispensable. Besides meeting the steady and unceasing demand for the Authorized. Version in English, the committee had decided to issue the Revised Version in a cheap and convenient forml For the Welsh people—(hear, hear)—who had no smal share in the society's foundation, a real need was to be met by the publication of a new edition of the Welsh Bible. The demands of the society at home were great, but it was' from abroad that the calls for help were loudest and most insistent. The work of translation and publication must at least keep pace with the pro- gress in commercial, educational and missionary enter- prise. It was computed that there were still over 400,000,000 of human beings without a single word of the Bible in any language they could understand. Scriptures were also required in embossed type for the blind of all nations. All that served to show the great work that still remained to be done. To enter upon new work and so meet present and urgent needs the Christian people were asked for 250,000 guineas at least as a thank- offering for what the Bible had been to them in per- sonal life, in church life, and in national life, (Applause).</p>
        <p>THE BISHOP MOVES THE FIRST RESOLIMON. The BISHOP of LIVERPOOL moved the first resolution which read as follows :—" That this meeting desires to , express its thanks to. Almighty God for the work that the Bible Society had by His blessing been enabled to do during the hundred years of its existence in allparts of the world." There were three things the Lord Bishop said, for which they had to thank God in con- nection with the Bible Society. First, for the wonder of its origin. There were some rivers which, like the Dee, burst from their source full grown; there were others which dripped, drop by drop, from the great , hummock on the mountain side. The Bible Society was I like the latter. Its first drop, if he might venture to I call it so, was the love of a little Walsh child for the Scriptures, which led her to traverse barefoot some 28 miles over the mountains to Bala to ask that saintly man Thomas Charles, for a copy of the Scriptures. The second drop was the sympathy and love of Charles,. of Bala, moved by the distress of the child at not being able, at first, to receive the Bible for which she had I travelled so far. That set him thinking, and at last caused him to devise a plan of a Bible Society for Wales. The third drop was the inspiration of the Secretary of the Religious Tract Society, Mr. Hughes who said that if they had a Bible Society for Wales why not for the United Kingdom, and if for the United Kingdom why not for the world. (Applause.) TS fourth drop was the meeting of, they were told, "respectable persons" on the morning of March 7th, 1304, when the weather was fair and without frost— a good type he thought, of the history of the Bible Society—(hear, hear)—when 300 men representing almost every phase of religious opinion, met in the London tavern in Bishopgate under the presidency of Granville Sharp to hoar the proposition laid before them and there and then decided to start a Bible Society for the world. (Applause). The last drop was the founda- tion of auxiliary societies, not only throughout England but gradually throughout the world, which slowly and surely added to the volume of the great river which started, now more than 100 years ago in the mountains of Bala. (Applause). Now that mighty river was run- ning for the healing of all countries, carrying its ferti- lising waters into every continent, and was justified in asking for what seemed a magnificent birthday gift of 250,000 guineas. For that wonder—the wonder of its origin—they thanked God to-day. Secondly, they thank- ed God for the divineness of its work. They had no need to fear for the Bible—(hear.hear)—they might be sure that the Bible would take care of itself. The Bible, as a great man in the third century pointed out to the Church, was a human element as well as divine, and if in the past men perhaps had forgotten the human element in the divine there might be a tendency at present to forget the divine element in the human. But</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BIBLE CRITICISM</head>
        <p>BIBLE CRITICISM</p>
        <p>could never put out the light of Holy Scripture. (Hear, hear.) All that criticism could do would be to remove some of our unfounded and false notions about the Bible. (Applause.) There were times, when some very clever book or a series of clever newspaper articles were published, when men threw up their hands in horror as if the days of the Bible were doomed. One thought of Voltaire, who spoke of Christianity as a dying religion. He himself heard only a few years ago, a great admirer of the book" Robert Elsmere " say that that book bad dealt Christianity its death blow. To-day "Robert Elsmere " was almost forgotten and Christianity lived on still. (Applause). The Bible would not be hurt, but only helped by its critics,and it was this book, which had stood the test of many hundreds of years, which the Bible Society sought to translate, to print and distribute. The Bible Society was the most catholic and thorough society that the Church knew. It was catholic because it sought to give the people of the earth the Bible in their own , language. If it were not for the British and Foreign Bible Society, missionary work would be paralyzed if not destroyed. The venerable society for the Propagation of the Gospel every year drew from the Bible Society two thirds of the translations used by its missionaries. Every other church and Nonconformist Society looked to this groat Bible Society above all for copies of the Scriptures to carry to the people whom they wished to evangelize. At the present moment, they were told,thetible Society had translations of the whole or part of the Scriptures which would reach seven-tenths of the human race ; and to-day they were asked so to enlarge their funds ,that translations might, in due time, be prepared for the re- maining three-tenths. (Hen, hear). Not only was the society catholic in its work, it was thorough and never ceased in the way ofrevising and re-revising its translation He believed most fully and he was sure every minister of every denomination believed, that as the Bible was I God's book so the Church wat God's institution, and by the Church he meant, in the language of their Prayer-book, all who professed and called themselves Christians. (Applause.) The Bible, he continued, was for the healing. What was the secret of national great- ness? He replied, with history before him, the know- ledge of the word of God—the word of God—honoured and enthroned in the heart of a nation and its precepts carried out. (Applause.) He wanted to ask them did they read the Bible? He was afraid that though the Bible was studied more than it used to be by a few scholars it was not read by the masses of the people as their forefathers read it. (Hear,hear.) The best thanks they could give to the Bible Society would he to learn to love, re bd, study and pray for the Bible • they endeavoured .to distribute throughout the world. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BOARD OF GUARDIANS ELECTION.</head>
        <p>THE BOARD OF GUARDIANS ELECTION.</p>
        <p>On Monday next, the triennial elaction of Guardians will take place. The only contest in the Borough is in Latchford Ward, where Miss Constance Broadbent and Mr. George Pilling are the candidates. Polling will commence at 12 o'clock noon and close at 8 p.m. There will ba two polling stations in Latch- ford Ward, one at St. James' school, Old-road. and the other at Christ Church school, Wash-lane. The ballot boxes will be conveyed to, Mr. Sutton's office and the result declared there the same evening. In Great Sankey the issue is between Mr. Thomas Halton, who has sat on the Board continuously for nine years and Mr. George Thompson. The polling station will be at the public schools, Liverpool-road. Mr. Ber- foot will be the presiding officer. The votes will be counted in the schoolroom and the result declared the name evening. Miss Broadbent's Candidature.</p>
        <p>A meeting in support of the candidature of Miss Constance Broadbent for the Latchford Ward for the Board of Guardians, was held in St. James' school- room on Thursday night. There was a moderate attendance. Mrs. James Fairclough presided, and there were also on the platform the Rev. H. Gibson Smith, Mr. W. Hickman, Miss Constance Broadbent, Mrs. Clay Mrs. Houghton, Mrs. Rylands, and Mrs. Clarke (Man- chester Board of Guardians). There were also in the audience, Alderman Pierpoint, and Councillors Dodds and James Evans. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Letters of/apology were read from several gentlemen. —The Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis) wrote that he understood that Miss Broadbent was a candidate for alection as a guardian for Latchford Ward. He had no hesitation in saying that he thought Miss Broadb ,ent well qualified for such a post, where intelligence, sot:nd judgment, kindness of heart, and real interest in he welfare of the poor were all important. He felt too that the presence of a lady, with her knowledge of matters which concerned women,would be of real value on the Board of Guardians.—The Rev. Morley Steven- son sincerely wished Miss Broadbent success, and was ; sure that she would do valuable work—The Rev. J. F. Matthews hoped that all denominations and political parties would rally round Miss Broadbent.—Mrs. Frost also wrote hoping that Miss Broadbent's candidature would be successful as she was very eligible for the position of Guardian. Mrs. FAIRCLOUGH having made a few remarks, Mrs. RYLANDS said her ooject in coming there was not to make a speech but to rally in support of her friend, Miss Broadbent. She considered that she was erainentlyfitted for the post for which she aspired. She had long been a visitor to the Workhouse and the Infirmary, and was secretary to the Brabazon Employ- ment Society, so that she was enabled to learn a great deal of the requirements necessary for the position of guardian of the poor. Mrs. Clay was not being opposed, she was glad to say. and she was glad that her services were being appreciated as they deserved. She (the speaker) felt strongly the necessity of having ladies on boards of guardians, as there were so many subjects they were able to do work upon so much better than the men, such as the details of household management, &amp;c. She hoped that all efforts would be taken to secure that Miss Broadbent would be successful at the poll. (Applause). Miss BROA.DBENT, who was cordially received,thanked the meeting for their sympathy and support. Her only reason why she wished to become a guardian of the poor was because there were certain portions of work with which it was not fitting that men should deal. (Hear, hear.) She was sure taat anyone who seriously con- sidered the question must see how desirable it was that there should be several women on the Board to deal with those matters connected withtheir own sex. (Ap- plause.) She could not enter into details but she felt sure they would understand what she meant. She stood for Latchford because it was her native place and she loved it andcould not take the same interest in any other ward. (Applause.) Of course she would feel very strange to the work at first, but she would try to enter upon it energetically, faithfully, and well, if they did her the honour of electing her. (Applause.) Mrs. CLARKE proposed " That this meeting considers it necessary to obtain the co-operating of women in order to administrate poor law successfully and effi- ciently, and therefore pledges itself to use every legiti- mate means to place Miss Broadbent in whom it has the greatest confidence at the head of the poll at the election of Guardians on Monday next." Out of 25,000 guardians in the county, there were only 1,000 lady ; guardians, and she contended that this disproportion should be decreased. ' Mr. IlicKmaer seconded, and said that his nine years' membership on the Board of Guardians had taught him the value of lady guardians. Mrs. Clay had done excel- lent work, and she had great moral courage in being the only lady guardian out of 28 guardians. He was glad now that she had a colleague in Mrs. Wilkinson for the Orford Ward, and there was a good prospect of Mrs. Hayward in Haydock, while he was sure that Latchford people would rally round and return Miss Broadbent. I The resolution was carried unanimously, and votes of thanks terminated the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>DISPUTE IN THE BEDSTEAD TRADE. Two Warrington Firms Affected.</head>
        <p>DISPUTE IN THE BEDSTEAD TRADE. Two Warrington Firms Affected.</p>
        <p>A dispute has occurred in the bedstead trade with reference to the payment of a bonus of 15 per cent to all workmen over 18 years of age in the manufacturing departments. It appears that an agreement was made in 1890 under which the Masters' Association undertook to pay this bonus on the understanding that the Work- men's Association enforced the payment of the bonus by all masters in the trade. It transpires that Bor 9 firms who e:uploy some 300 workmen, in addition to those of London and Glasgow., have been permitted to employ labour without paying the bonus, notwithstand- ing that in some cases the men are of the Workmen's Association. These firms,it is contended by the Masters' Association, have been placed in a more favourable position in competition for businen than those who have consistently paid the bonus, and at a meet- ing of employers and representatives of the workmen held in July last, the disadvantages under which the bonus-paying employers ware placed, were pointed out. The Workmen's Association was urged to secure the adoption of the bonus for all workmen, at least outside London.</p>
        <p>The result has not been satisfactory to the employers connected with the Misters Association, and •notices have been served on the workmen of their intention to discontinue the bonus.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening a crowded meeting of members of the Bedstead Workmen's Association was held at the Old Central Hall, Corporation-street, Birmingham. A resolution was unanimously passed endorsing the action of the officers of the association in taking a ballot of the trade, and setting forth determination not to work without the bonus. It is clear now that unless the employers are prepared to continue the 15 per cent. bonus there will be a conflict in the trade. The number of.papers received up to Wednesday night was 1,753 expressing unwillingness to work without the bonus. There were three dissensions. Fourteen firms, employ- ing about 400 men, are going to continue work and pay the bonus, but, on the other hand, thirty-three firms are standing by the notice to discontinue the 15 per cent. bonus, which affects 2,000 men, and perhaps altogether n o fewer than 5,000 people. The dispute affects two Warrington firms, namely, Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., Standard Bedstead works and the Mersey Bedstead Company's works in Knutsford road. Both firms have paid the bonus and unless they withdraw the notice and pay the bonus, the men will cease work to-day. There are 75 workmen in Warring- ton connected with the Workmen's Association, and if they cease work, some 200 workpeople will be thrown out of employment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>GRAPPEN HALL</head>
        <p>GRAPPEN HALL</p>
        <p>YOUNG MEN'S CLUB.—A grand assault-at-arms and gymnastic display was given in the Large Room on Thursday evening. The gymnasium in connection with the Young Men's Club was brought to a close on the above occasion, and it was thought to be a fitting oppor- tunity to prove that the members had benefited by their exertions and exercises, and also to make .the financial side more satisfactory. The proceedings were greatly helped by the following non-commissioned officers from Orford Barracks: Sergt-Instructor Sweeney, Army Gymnasium Staff (instructor of the Young Men's Club), Sergeant-Instructor Stamford, Army Gymnasium staff, Colour-Sergeant Newall, South Lancashire Regiment and Colour-Sergeant Mahoney, who gave displays on the parallel bars, foil and sabre fencing, bayonet versus sword and bayonet versus bayonet engagements. Pri- vate Rooney, South Lancashire Regiment and M. Jeffries, ex-nine stone champion of Lancashire, gave an interesting boxing exhibitionos also did Private Bright Liverpool Regiment, and Private Brindling, South Lan- cashire Regiment. Indian Club, dumb-bell and bar-bell exercises were gone through in a very creditable manner by the members themselves who also sholved a very fair amount of proficiency on the parallel bars. A very amusing item was the cockade fighting. The audience highly appreciated the different items many of which, beside being interesting, were decidedly novel.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>OVAL</p>
        <p>RICH TEA</p>
        <p>BISCUITS. (In -11 b. Packets, Szc.)</p>
        <p>THE ":CRAWFORD" QUALITY.</p>
        <p>MINNI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>PROCEEDS.</head>
        <p>THE PROCEEDS.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM OWEN, the local treasurer, informs us that the amount of the collection at the pUblio meeting was £33. The total sum realized by the "Warrington Auxiliary" towards the "Centenary Fund" (including special collections at various churches and chapels in the district together with donations) amounts to £157 Is. id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
