      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE WARRING- TON COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Chinese Labour Condemned.</p>
        <p>THE INJUSTICES OF THE EDUCATION ACT.</p>
        <p>Liberalism and Nonconformity.</p>
        <p>The important questions of education and the in- troduction of Chinese labour into the South African mines were to the fore at the annual meeting of the Warrington and District Free Church Council held in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, on Thursday even- ing. A fair attendance was presided over by Mr. C. E. Parker, president of the Council, who was supported on the platform by the Revs. Alexander Mackennall, D.D., Bowdon, W. J. Townsend, Prestatyn, John Yonge, G. H. Carnson, J. S. Hughes and J. F. Matthews (secretary). THE ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS presented the annual report as follows :—" Exactly 12 months to-night the last annual meeting was held in this place, when we had the advantage of listening to our friend, Rev. 3. Hirst Hollowell, who fought so splendidly for Education and Liberty in that stronghold of reactionary Chamberlain- ism—Birmingham. Early in 1903 a Vigilance Committee was formed expressly to watch eauc itional matters as affecting Free Church men in the town. With the excep- tion of two members at the time of formation nothing has occurred that hasnecessitated its interrention:Theservices held weekly at the Workhouse have more than doubled in attendance during the past 12 months. The arrange- ment of these services, we have watched for about four years. In all that 'time we have never known the Free Church services so well attended. To what the advance is due we do not know. This one thing we know that the Free,Churches are under an incalculable debt of gratitude to Miss Crampton, who goes regularly to play the organ at the weekly service; and there is no more welcome visitor at these services, especially among the women, than our friend. The time and services are 'generously given, and merit unstinted praise. The chief feature of the Council's work, however, has been the development in relation to the Winwick scheme. The land has been secured on lease; a trust has been created, and a deed drawn up; a subscription list has been opened, and nearly £2OO given and promised. The Executive hopes that during 1904 the I churches will contribute at least one collection each to the fund and that a distinct advance may be made in obtaining further subscriptions towards the work. Already the first half-year's rent for the land is due,and the Executive is under a stipulated promise to put up the building at Winwick within</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>NEXT. 18 MONTHS.</head>
        <p>THE NEXT. 18 MONTHS.</p>
        <p>There is no reason why the hall should not be no and opened by the autumn of the present year. As well as all their own concerns, this joint effort of the churches can be and should be carried through successfully. We began the year 1904 with a clear balance sheet. This is only because an attempt has been made to secure donations and subscriptions from friends who are inter- estee in our work, in addition to the small annual subscriptions from the churches. The modest sum of £l5 covers all the expenses of the Council, including hire of committee room, printing, posting and other matters. We began the year with 23 churches in mem- ship with the Free Church Council, one having with- drawn from our fellowship. We can only say further as all interested in the success of the Free Church Council that its future work and prosperity can only be secured by the regular attendance of the delegates, and by their cordial co-operation in the work of our Common Master, Jesus Christ. CHINESE SLAVERY CONDEMNED.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he considered that he would best serve the interests of the meeting by confining his remarks within a narrow compass and to one subject. The moral issues raised by the introduction of Chinese labour into the Transvaal under restrictive and servile conditions were such that if they as Free Churchmen kept ' silent they would be disloyal to the conceptions of freedom and of human liberty upon which their Churches, their civilization, and indeed their Christian- ity itself largely rested. To find a parallel in our own history to the proposed conditions, we would have to go back in the development of our British civilization to the 14th century. Six hundred years ago the workers in England were kept in compounds. The compound was tie manor, the lord of the manor had the right to the labour of all within that compound. No labourer could wander beyond the limits of the manor without permission. If he did so he could be hunted as a fugi- tive, as is proposed in South Afrioa,and anyone harbour- ing such a fugitive was,as in South Africa, amenable to the law. But even in that early time a labourer was free on the payment of a tax to go and seek work elsewhere. This was denied to the propowd serfs in South Africa. Was it not significant that we should have to go back six hundred years in English history before we could find anything similar to the conditions which it was proposed not only to perpetuate but also to aggravate in the new Colony, the Transvaal. They were told that they could not from such a distance underltand the conditions existing in South Africa, and that they must accept the opinion of those who were on the spot. He would give greater weight to that point if the opinions were those of the permanent residents in the Colony, both of British and Dutch blood. As far as</p>
        <p>they could gather, the opinion of such was strongly against the introduction of Chinese labour, as he thought it would have been shown if the author- ities had agreed to the request for a plebiscite of the whole population. The demand sprung from those who would be materially advantaged directly and indirectly by this system. (Hear, hear). He freely granted that those on the spot were better able to see and to under- stand the•material det,ils of the question than they in England. About these details, if he entered into them he would desire to speak withproper reserve. But their objection to this proposal did not rest on detail. It was based upon principles which were unaffected by the details of their application, and which they in England could apply, perhaps more justly than those whose nearer vision vps blurred by the hope of individual gain. "We take our stand" said Mr. Parker, "on the principles of human freedom of in- dividual liberty, and of the rights of those who toil. The enjoyment of these rights, the attachment of these principles, we have rightly been taught to believe are the heritage, the birthright of the English race, they are inseparably associated with British soil, and have ever been safeguarded by the British flag. The English race through many centuries of sore travail has won for itself these liberties, and the high price paid deepens our estimation of their value, and one determination to maintain them at all costs, and not alone for ourselves, but also for all who come to labour on British soil. For these reasons I ask you as representatives of Christian Churches, recognizing the part that Christian- ity, often as an unseen and unacknowledged force, has played in winning these liberties, I ask -you to record your protest against this outrage to what is best in the tradition and the sentiments of Christian England in the following resolution:" "That this meeting of the Free Churchmen ofjWarring- ton and District, views with repugnance and indignation the action of His Majesty's Government in allowing and approving, the introduction of Chinese labour into the Transvaal Colony under conditions closely allied to slavery." The Rev.. JOHN YONGE seconded. He said he was perfectly sure that the resolution in that meeting would need no advocacy. He thought it was a safe thing to say that they were united in the belief that freedom was a human birthright. They claimed it for themselves as Englishmon, Welshmen and Scotsmen, and they were not, therefore, likely to consent very easily to impose its opposite upon other people. (Applause.) The resolution was unanimously carried. THE NEW MINISTER OF EMMANUEL CHURCH'. The Rev. J.F. MATTHEWS expressed on behalf of the Rev. F. Vaughan, the new incumbent of Emmanuel Church, his regret at being unable to attend. His good wishes were with them, and he hoped soon to make their acquaintance. (Applause.) DR. IVIACKENNAL AND RELIGIOUS STRIFE:</p>
        <p>The Rev. ALEXANDER gAcKENNAL, 8.A., D.D., then addressed the meeting. He said they had frequently been asking themselves the question of recent years "Why don't the men go to Church ?" Judging from his experience of local Free Church Councils he should be disposed to say "Because the local Free Courch Council does not invite them." One of the characteristics of their local councils had been that the number of men present reminded one of the palmiest days of Church history whenn the men attended in no smaller numbers that women. Dr. Mackennal proceeded to allude to the formation of the National Free Church Council. At tha time they looked with a little surprise at some of their more earnest friends as they, not contented to labour for the full co-operation and fellowship of the Evangelical Free Churches, were looking forward to the gathering of free and established into one great national communion, and were even prepared to antici- Rate a growing movement which should gender it possible to think of one catholic church in all the world. It was with a feeling of solemn and overwhelming grati- tude that they believed they had come to the beginning of the days of peace. But the days of peace had not come—neither peace in the churches, peace in the nations, nor peace in the world. Some of them had spent one or two of the saddest years they had ever known when they had seen how soon the nation could beat its plough shares into swords and make its pruning hooks into spears. A still more distressful experience had been theirs, as they had seen how the very churches themselves had been compelled to draw themselves up in hostile array and how, in addition to the particular question which was giving them most agitation now—one in which they were personally concerned as Christians land members of churches with a church doctrine of their own—they had been compelled to recognize other questions of equal importance. They who had loved to think that disestablishment—the freedom of the</p>
        <p>Churches from State control was going to be the next and almost solitary question to be fought out in the cause of freedom, had with shame and sorrow been compelled to recognize that if they were not alert andl in earnest, slavery would re- assert itself as an element in English life. He made the affirmation ths%t their English Noncon- formity never was felt to be a greater power in England than it was to-day. (Applause.) Mr. Balfour thought they were of so small concern that be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>DID NOT VEIL HIS CONTEMPT FOR THEM.</head>
        <p>DID NOT VEIL HIS CONTEMPT FOR THEM.</p>
        <p>He wondered if iii::"l3;ii-o'u;Valf;:g- iinTic-h contempt for them to-day? (the Rev. J. F. Matthews: No. He has not). They knew he had been casting his glances hither and thither among the Nonconformists to see if there were any who would throw out the painter and tow his barque in. (Laughter.) They would throw out the lifebuoy—they had no personal hostility to him—but they would never tow his barque "My optimism." continued the speaker, "leads me to say that, sorely as I regret the present conflict, I am not at all sure that we did not need something of the sort—(hear, hear)—to make us fully conscious of the strength and rectitude of our own cause—(hear, hear)— and to recall our attention to the fundamental differ- ence between us and the two great Church organizations —the one of which identifies itself with the English nation, and the other of which has its seat in Rome. I think ourgriends of the Established Church will learn what they for several years past seem to have for- gotten—that we Nonconformists have a doctrine of the Church of God—(hear, hear)—and that we hold it as firmly as they hold their doctrine of the Church of God and that there are some conceptions of the Church of God and of Christ as the Head of the Church which we can never surrender while we are true to our faith in Jesus Christ." Continuing Dr. Mackennal emphasised the point on which the early separatists left the estab- lished church—that the church must be a self-governing body. He firmly believed that many of their friends in the Church of England thought they were utterly blind as to the autonomy of the Church and the headship of Christ in thel Church. They were, how- ever, coming to learn that Nonconformists too believed that the full Gospel of Jesus Christ included a doctrine of the Church, that that doctrine was an antagonistic as well as a constructive doctrine, that that doctrine had no room for the priest, or for a National Church, and had no room for any individual now alive and treading this planet as the head of the Church. If it were not for their confidence in that, they could make terms as easily and comfortably as they pleased, and instead of their present unhappy conflict, they could be quiet and placable. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>DR. TOWNSEND AND THE EDUCATION QUESTION. NONCONFORMITY AND LIBERALISM.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. J. TO WNSEND, D.D., alluded at the out- set to the National Free Church Council which he said, was only a little infant compared with what it was going to be. What could they have done in the grea educatioe fight without the Free Church Council How could they have organised their forces and carried out their opposition? Within forty- eight hours of the introduction of the Bill they had held a meeting of representatives from all parts of England, arranged the great St. Jarnes's hall demonstration, and practically laid ont the plan of campaign. He did not want them to be carried away by the signs that were in the air just now in regard to compromises, concordats, and round table conferences. The time for that kind of thing had gone. (Applause.) They had nothing to do now but to stand square to the enemy and fight out the battle to its bitter end until they had won the victory on the side of justice and religious equality. The Archbishop of Canterbury wrote to Dr. Horton proposing a round-table conference. The Archishop of York wrote a letter considerably in the same direction. All that had been gone through and they could not ko through it again. Eighteen months ago the Bishop of London invited them to a conference at Fulham Palace. There was some use in having a conference, then, because the Bill was before the House of Commons and by a word from the clerical party it might have been modified in any direction. The Free Church representatives laid down as a sine qua non and an irreducible minimum that they must have on their side the majority of managers of every denominational school that was to come upon the rates and taxes. (Hear, hear.) With one or two exceptions the Bishops were not prepared to relax in the slightest degree their hold of supreme power on the boards, and the Free Church representatives' request was refused. He then said "If it means a 30 years' war we will fight the matter out." They did not look to the Bishops to-day, nor to the clerical party, nor to any other class in the nation, but on that great question of national and popular education they stood square to the State and to the citizens, and they would take no verdict, receive no instructions, and ask no counsel from any class in the State except the great body of the people which would settle the matter once for all in the interests of the nation and of the nation's children. (Applause.) Hs was sorry to see the state- ment of the Rev. R. J. Campbell, of the City Temple, in regard to the Liberal party. He did not know whether Mr. Campbell said he was losing faith in the Liberal party or whether he was losing faith in the front bench of the • Opposition but he (Dr. Townsend) somewhat regretted the remark, and he referred to it for the sake of removing any wrong impressions that might exist. They had their meetings with the leaders of the Liberal party, and they had been received by them with the utmost frankness. The leaders of the Liberal party were pledged to the hilt to make the reversal of the Education Act the first plank in their programme. (Applause). He was quite aware that one or two of the front bench men had been very silent on the Education Act. Mr. John Morley never opened his lips on the subject in Parliament, and he (Dr. Townsend) had not been able to trace one word of his on the Act with the exception of a passing allusion to "the reactionary Education Bill. Of course they were very much dissatisfied with Mr. Morley on that account —(hear, hear)—that the very man whom they might have expected to stand up in defence of righteousness and liberty in every direction should have maintained almost entire silence on the subject. Sir Henry Fowler had also been exceedingly reticent, but with those-ex- ceptions the leaders on the front bench had been exceedingly helpful to them in the struggle and they had every reason to have entire confidence in them. They were not going to be blindly led and they were not going to forfeit any of the principles that were dear to their hearts in regard to national educa- tion, being free and unsectarian and of the highest possible standard that any nation could secure. Those points they would insist upon together with the abolition of religious tests for teachers. He trusted they would be prepared to stand by the Liberal party in the next election and strain every nerve to return the largest number of stalwart Free Churchmen that they possibly could get to stand, in order that the battle might be carried on. The Liberal party had never been so well prepared for a fight as it was at the present moment. Dr. Towns- end alluded to the contrast in the reception accorded by Mr. Balfour to the Nonconformist deputation and to the brewers. It was enough to make the whole nation hide its head for shame that Mr. Balfour should stand there, the premier of a great nation, and to a contaminating trade speak in terms of such shameful flattery and submission. The Government wanted to give the brewers a sop, just as they had given sops to the landlords and the clerical party. To have the enormous endowment given to the liquor trade that aCompensation Act would give, Would mean the decadence of the nation, and ultimately our entire downfall. Dr. Townsend entered in detail into the Education question, pointing out that Free Churchmen had to pay for the teaching of doctrines which they regarded as blasphemous. The Confessional was practised in day schools to a very con- siderable extent. The most diabolical instrument of demoralisation ever known in Christendom was the , confessional. It was a low, insidious education in hypocrisy and in lying and every form of vice. They had to pay for their children being contaminated by that miserable instrument of evil. They must rise up in strength and power and put down the cruel, unjust and re-actionary Act. They had to be heroes in the great , fight, and cry like Cromwell "For God and the right." (Loud Applause). On the motion of the Rev. G. H. Carnson, seconded bythe Rev. J. S. Hughes, the chairman and speakers were heartily thanked.</p>
        <p>Fire Precautions at Schools. Mr. J. Moore Murray, has issued on behalf of the Education Committee, a circular giving some "Sugges- tions for the guidance of head teachers in dealing with outbreaks of fire." They include the following : " Children furthest from the means of exit, or, in two storied buildings, those on the top floor, should pass out first • the school doors should never be locked whilst the children are in school; an assistant teacher should be placed at any point where a crush is likely to take place, and at all doors which open inwards ; the children shonld pass out into the street, and there be marshalled in classes ; where infants and girls use the same door, the infants should pass out first ; and the children must be instructed not to go to the cloak rooms for their clothes. In schools of more than one department the head teachers must agree on some special signal which can be recognized as an alarm. Occasional experimental clearances of the school should be made and a record kept in the log book of the time occupied in !getting the children out. Head teachers should ascertain the nearest telephones that can be used to summon the fire brigade, the tele- phone number of which is 24. The committee feel assured that the head and assistant teachers are sensible Of the great responsibility of their positions in cases of emergency, and that they will cordially co-operate in taking every precaution to secure the safety of the children.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SIiOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—Act7t.</p>
      </div>
