      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAtiISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAtiISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A HUSBAND'S SAD LOT.—WIFE AN HABITUAL DRUNKARD. Yesterday at the Warrington Police Court, Bridget Nevin, 283, Winwick-road, was charged at the instance of her husband, with being an habitual drunkard. The • husband applied for a separation order. He said he was obliged to bring the case before the magistrates because his wife had been drinking almost regularly for the past six years. She had continually robbed him of furniture and clothing which she pawned to obtain drink. She was hardly ever sober—and in fact, never was sober if she could get any money. She was a dangerous woman when in drink and on one occasion threw some bricks through the window, the missiles just missing him and „ a child aged six years. Since their marriage he had had three grocer's businesses, but had had to abandon them all on account of his wife's conduct. —lnspector Hackett spoke to visiting the house when the parties resided in Appleton-street. On arriving there he heard screaming, and on looking through the window saw the defendant, with a little girl in her arms. She was threatening to throw the child into the fire. He went into the house and in excuse for her conduct, defendant said the girl did not clean the floor properly.—The magistrates suggested that the husband, instead of obtaining a separation order, should send his wife to a home. Complainant expressed his willingness to adopt this course, but defendant absolutely refused to go to a home.—The Chairman (Mr. H. Roberts) remarked that defendant was evidently a hopeless case. A separation order was granted, the husband to have the custody of the chil- dren and to pay his wife 6s. per week alimony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough' Police Court.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough' Police Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Messrs. Birchall (in the chair), R. T. Fairclough, and Hayward. INSISTED UPON BUNG LOCKED UP.—Joseph Clancy, School-brow, entered the Police Station in a drunken state at midnight on Friday and told P.C. Rylatt that he wanted to be locked up. As he refused to leave the premises he was ejected, and when in Arpley-street he butted the officer with tits head, and knocked him down. He was then placed in the cells and now appeared in the dock on the charges of having been drunk and dis- orderly and assaulting P.C. Rylatt.—He was fined 10s. and costs, with the alternative of 14 days' imprisonment. MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (presiding), Aldermen Francomb, and Smethurst Councillor Thornton, and Messrs. Birchall, Hayward and Wright. AFTER WORK—PLAY.—Harry B roadhurst,lo,Well ng- ton-street, and William Webb, 20, Tilley-street, were summoned for having made loud and unseemly noises in Church-street on March 10th.—From the evidence of P.C. Clough it appeared that defendants had been work- ing all night at Messrs. Rylands's works. On leaving at six o'clock in the morning they began to indulge in recreation by kicking a tin can about the street and shouting.—They were ordered to pay for the sport to the tune of 7s. 6d. each. VAGRANCY.—George Steel, of no settled abode, was found sleeping in the grounds, of Miss Broadbent, The Hollies, Latchford, early on Sunday morning. He had no visible means of subsistence and a constable tsok him into custody.—He was sent to goal for seven day WEDNESDA.Y.—BeforelAldermen Sutton (in the chair) and Hutchinson, and Mr. Birchall. WOULD NOT WORK.—William Finch, an inmate of the Workhouse, was charged with refusing to perform a task allotted to him that morning.—Prisoner was told to pick 21bs of oakum butrefused, stating that he would rather go to gaol.—He now told the bench that he wanted to leave the Workhouse but was informed that he would "have to serve seven days." That was his reason for not performing the task. He was sentenced to a month's hard labour. FRlDAY.—Before Mr. H . Roberts (presiding), Alder- men Smethurst and Pierpoint, Councillors Roberts and Thornton. SENT TO A TRAINING SHIP.—John Forster, a small boy aged 13, was charged on a warrant with wandering abroad and not being under proper guardianship.— School Attendance Inspector Bamber said the boy was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>40. OCO</head>
        <p>40. OCO</p>
        <p>is an Absolutz,ly</p>
        <p>PUT?. Artlae can-</p>
        <p>not be controverted</p>
        <p>'tr.-I—view of the results , of analysis which, in our hands, this</p>
        <p>excellent article of food has yielded."</p>
        <p>eontinually on the streets.—The magistrates ordered him to go to the training ship " Clio " until he is sixteen. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A FORGIVING MOTHER.—Samuel Warburton, 369, Winwick-road, was summoned for assaulting his mother, Martha Warburton, on the 21st inst.—Defendant promised to "turn over a new leaf" and complainant withdrew the charge.</p>
