      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WARRINGTON POLICE INSPECTED. The Force Highly Efficient but " Much too Small."</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON POLICE INSPECTED. The Force Highly Efficient but " Much too Small."</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning Lieutenant-Colonel Eden, H.M. Inspector of Constabulary for the Northern District, made his annual inspection of the Warrington County, Borough Police Force. He was met at Bank Quay Station by the Chief Constable (Mr. Luke Talbot.) He first asked if any increase of the force had taken place since the last inspection. Being answered in the:nega.tive he said that the force was very inadequate to the popula- tion and increase of police duties, and that he must insist upon an augmentation. Nearly the whole of the force, with the exception of a few men on duty in the street, was on parade at the Police Buildings where Colonel Eden was met by the Mayor, Deputy-Mayor, and a representative body of the Watch Committee. After the men had been examined individually by the Inspecting Officer they stood to their kits, arranged in military fashion around the parade Room. A minute scrutiny of all the accoutrements, clothing and appointments followed. The Force was then fallen in and put through drill by the Chief Constable subsequently advancing in review order, under Chief 'lnspector Phillips, to a Saluting- base where Colonel Eden and the Watch Committee received their general salute. This was a new and pleasing feature of the Inspection. The Government Inspector then traversed the whole of the building, minutely inspecting the cells and residential quarters, and afterwards proceeded to the Chief Constable's Office There the whole of the books, documents and records of the Police Force were subjected to a very searching audit at his hands. Several times in the process of inspection the Inspecting Officer was moved to strong expressions of approval, and at the termination of his visit announced his entire satisfac- tion with, and admiration of, the men's physique, equip- ment, drill and discipline. He considered the books and records were in a high state of efficiency, and the arrangements .and accommodation or the new Police Buildings first class. He had absolutely no suggestion of improvement to make, except that the Force at its present numerical strength was much too small. He was told that this was about to be immediately remedied by the appointment of four men, a number which, the 'lnspector said, was quite few enough for present requirements. Col. Eden's report to the Home Government will be in accordance with the gratifying result of his visit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WHAT IS "BILIOUS GREEN " ?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS "BILIOUS GREEN " ?</p>
        <p>A Question for Ladies.</p>
        <p>Every lady knows that her choice of colours in dress depends upon her complexion. Some complexions can't "carry" certain colours at all. How aggravating it is therefore to a woman of limited means when, after having carefully casidered this subject, and bought suitably coloured material, her complexion changes to that faint greeny-white tint which throws all colotua out of harmony. This colour of the skin—which may be termed "bilious grem "—is due to the presence in the blood, of substances which should be extracted by the liver ; and it is most quickly rectified by a short course of bile beans. It is a distinct sign that the liver is not doing its work properly, and is often accompanied by head- ache, nausea, sensations of weakness, and sometimes dizziness and fainting. Where the liver is out of order there is generally constipation, and this ailment often sets up serious disorders, such as piles, anaamil, &amp;c. As well may one try to make a foul stream look clear by covering it with white paint as try to keep up a healthy fresh complexion where there is a disordered unhealthy liver. When Mrs. Rebecca Snowden went to live in Claren- ton-lane, Bolton-road, Bradford, from the country, she had a fresh complexion. The change of air brought on constipation and disorders of the liver and digestive systems. These ailments soon had a serious effect upon her complexion. She says :—" My complexion turned to a sickly white colour. I had a constant bad taste in my mouth. Instead of feeling bright and cheerful, I began to feel heavy, depressed, and miserable. T also began to suffer from frequent attacks of acute bilious- ness, which at times nearly prostrated me. Pimples and blotches,some of which were of considerable size, broke out on my face, and proved very painful. Altogether I found myself in a very serious condition. I lost weight and was very much wasted. Of course, I tried various remedies, but until I tried Chas. Porde's bile beans nothing did me good. "When I commenced to try them the constipation speedily ceased to trouble me, and once this was cured the other symptoms (which really arose from that all. ment) gradually diminished. I had only been taking the beans a short time when I found myself quite cured and restored to my forme- good health." Bile beans, which effected this cure, are obtainable from medicine vendors everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
