      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>UP-HOLLAND GRAMMAR SCHOOL v. COWLEY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>SCHOOL</head>
        <p>SCHOOL</p>
        <p>Played on the Up-Holland ground on Wednesday. The home team pressed from the start ald forced several corners but the Cowley goal-keeper only let two shots th-ough in the first half one from J. Webster (captain) and the other from T. Wilson. On changing over the warm sun seemed to have told on the players. The Cowley forwards were never danger.?us ad H. Witter and S. Thompson as full backs easily stopped their attacks on the Up-Holland goal. J. Webster and W. Winstanley scored again. Final score :—Up-Holland Grammar School, 4 goals; Cowley School, nil.</p>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS LOVE TRAGEDY. Leopold Benuison, tbe young man over whom a charge of attempted murder is hanging, was expected to come Mersey Golf Club. up for trial at St. Helens Town Hall on Tuesday or 1 Wednesday, but it transpires that his recovery has not leave tb;:.Providence Hospitaltettilm_l of week. Miss There was a ffood attendance at the annual general been so rapid as was expected, and he will be unable to meeting of the above club, held at the Patten Arms Grady, is sweetheart,.h e attack, was Hotel. The treasurer's report was found to be satis- discha gad from hospital last Thursday. factory. Captain Michael Hughes' was re-elected 1 president; Colonel Pilkington, M.P. and Messrs. .. J. B. Aitken, A. Browne, A. H. Crosfield, and W. D. I Jeans, sen., were elected vice-presidents; Mr. C. R. A young married woman, who was taken to church a bride two years ago, has just died at Romford, and in Adamson was elected captain; Dr. Murray hon. accordance with her ging wish has been laid out in treasurer; and Mr. T. Miniati, hon. secretary, for the her wedding dress, in which she is to be buried. present year. Messrs. R. Forster, F. W. Gilbert, W. "I think the black list has been a complete failure," Greenhouse, H. Hatton, W. S. Knowles, 0. Lofthouse, said Chief Constable Stirling at the Grimsby Borough 1 Police Court on Wednesday. "Binding defendant over andJ. Moore Murray constitute the committee. is much more effective."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Examiier, Saturday, March 26 1902.</head>
        <p>The Examiier, Saturday, March 26 1902.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Monthly</p>
        <p>tThe monthly meeting of the Warriugton Rural Dis- rict Council wi,s held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednes- dayafternoon. Mr. T. Halton presided and there were also present: Dr. Murra7, Messrs. Backhouse, Knowles, Longtan, Hoag-Iton, Forshaw, Brooks and Mather, together with Messrs. J. C. Sutton (clerk), F. W. Ker- foot, (inspector), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor), H. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor), and Dr. Sephton (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>DANGEROUS CORNERS.</head>
        <p>DANGEROUS CORNERS.</p>
        <p>It was decided to instruct the surveyor to write to the surveyor to the County Council asking him to con- sider .theadvisability of erecting notice boards at all dangerous places in ths disrict warning drivers of motor cars to drive slowly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>LONGFORD NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE LONGFORD NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPHTON reported that on Monday last he visited the Longford Depot in company with the Inspector. They found no improvement in the conditions which obtained. The improvements and the work of altera- tions were being continued, but it would be two months before they were completed. The INSPECTOR also reported on the same subject. Very little progress had, he said, been made with the alterations owing to the non,arrival of the new machinery. Bacterial beds for experimental purposes had been laid down and were working satisfactorily. The nuisance was still very bad. Mr. KNOWLES: Have all your papers in order for the first meeting of the new Coupon with a view to getting Local Government powe:s which will enable them to deal with the Corporation for carrying on an offensive trade.</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR e-Iplained that the Council had those powers and the matte: was allowed to drop, it being understood tbat it would be re-introduced at the first meeting of the new Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>FLOODING AT LONGFORD.</head>
        <p>FLOODING AT LONGFORD.</p>
        <p>At the last meeting of the Council the Surveyor reported that in consequence of the partial &amp;mining up of the Sankey Brook by Messrs. Crosfield's Limited, Cue wai;er in the brook was being held up and was flooding the district at Longford. He was instructed to write to Messrs. Crosfield's asking them to cleaa out the brook near their tip and remove the obstruction. On Wednesday Mr. NEWCOMBE reported that, while denying their liability, Messrs. Crosfield's had agreed to do the work suggested by the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>TENDERS.</head>
        <p>TENDERS.</p>
        <p>The Sur;myor recommended the acceptance by the Council of a number of tenders, but the Council not having had time to consider the same it was decided to adjourn the matter to a special meeting to be held oa April sth. MEDICAL OFFICER'S ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The medical officers report for the year ending Decem- ber 31st, 1903 was laid before the meeting. The esti- mated population was 11.387, and during the year there had been 307 bi erhs and 150 deaths. The bLa,las and (baths in the sub-registration districts were :—San'Lay 167 births, 84 deaths ; Winwick 82 births, 31 deaths; Rixton 58 births, 35 deaths. The birth rate was equal to 25.9 per 1.000, and the death rate to 31.1 per 1,000. Fifty two of the deaths which occurred were of children under five years of age, while 85 were of persons of the age of 25 and upwards, 39 being over the age of 65. The infantile mortality had been high, being equal to 1107 per every 1,000 caildren bora. Durin-3 the year 278 deaths had occurred at the county asylum, Winwick, which gave a death rate o? 139 pez every 1,093 of the inmates.</p>
        <p>During the year scarlet fever had been prevalent, and outbreaks of measles had occur:ed in °third, Padgate and Croft, though in the case of each of the dismses only one death had occurred. No cases of smallpox had occurred in the district. _ _ _</p>
        <p>With regard to hospital accommodation. Dr. Sephton reported that the Warrington Co:poration still con- tinued to receive patients from the district into their hospital, and during the year seven cases of scarlet fever and one of entc7ic fever occurring in the district had been treated in the Warrington district. With regard'to the requirements of the distr:ct, Dr. Sephton says "I would still advise your Council to have a thorough system of drainage at Fearnhead, to have a better water supply for Rinton and Martinscroft if possible. The nuisance from the Longford Depot reported in my last annual report, and frequently brought before you at your monthly proceedings, is at last receiving attention, and szch extensive alts-ations are being made at the works, that I hope will do away with the nuisance so long complained of."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE ALLEGED ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Monday, Arthur Imison, a well known Widnes youth, was brought up on remand charged with attempting to commit suicide in the lavatory of the Alevindra Hotel last Saturday week. Imison was ordered to be medically examined in Walton Gaol, and on Monday he stepped up to the rails looking much brighter for his week's incarceration. His brother pro- mised to look after him with h view shortly of getting him again in the Army.—The Bench had a short con- sultation with Councillor Pal in and his brother, and he , was discharged on promising to take the pledge. FoUND DROWNED.</p>
        <p>Last Friday at Cuerdley an inquest was held con- cerning the death of Mary Hollingsworth, an elderly woman, of 58, Oakland-sixeet, W'does. Evidence was given to the effect that the deceased who had been somewhat low-sp:rited left her home on Wednesday morning.—A [la Lrran stated that he recovered the body from the St. Helens and Sankey Canal on Wednesday evening.—[n the absence of any other evidence an open verdict of "Pound drowned" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>R UNDO G ukaT)l: ANS ELECTION.</head>
        <p>R UNDO G ukaT)l: ANS ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Polling in the Central and Newton Wads takes place on Saturday. There are th.ne Candidates for the two seats in the Central Ward, the Rev. Father Denlevy and Messrs. T. Wood and T. F. Lea. The nomination of the Rev. Father Denlevy is consequent upon the retirement from the Board of the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, a popular and elicient me nber for several years. Messrs. J. W. Temparley and M. Thomas will be in opposidon in the Ness on Ward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>LEAVING A TRAIN WHILST IN MOTION.</p>
        <p>George Ainscough, builder, of 182, Market-street, Earlestown, pleaded guilty at St. Helens, on Monday, to alighting from a railway carriage while in motion. 'Mr. Fenn% prosecuted.—Mr. Lucas, stationmaster at St. Helens Junction, said that on February 15th, the 5.47 p.m. train from Manchester came in and was signalled off again. Just as it started, a door at the rear of the train opened and the defendant stepped out. The train was then going fast, and the man stumbled. He kept hold of the door, and WS swing with his head against the train. The guard put on the emergency brake, and by the time the train got to the ead of the platform Ainscough rolled on his back. Fortunately, he rolled away from the train. Ile was in such a condition that Mr. Lucas would not allow him to go on to St. Helens by the train.—Ainscougli said be was very sorry. It was an oversight that he cl!4:1 not get out at St. Helens Junction —He was fined £1 and ss. 6d. costs. TtE TEAPERANCE PARTY AND THE EA.RUISTOWN ETACTION'S. - _</p>
        <p>A uni.:.ed meeting of representa,tivel of local tempet. fines socieiies and Chric:tian Chu Thes in Earlesovvn- was held in the Wesleyan Lecture Hall on Friday evenii ing last. The peculiar position of the local Coyne e and Guardian election, wbere four teetotalers El 1 s ranged against four members of the L'ceased Vic tualens Association otheiwise pahlicans,—fc -! the four seat, was freely commented upon. Considwahle discussioe took pl ace, a cours3 of anon beim dec'ded upon. Ths following resolution was unan;mously adopted :—" Tbi meeting representing the vaelous temperance societies of Earle stown and d:strict, realis:ng tie u-gency of the situation, and the need for definite action, strongly utges upon all voters and woekersto give anunqualilled support to Dr. Latham in Town Hall Ward, and to Mr. E. Worsley in Viaduct Wad, for seatc on the Urban District Council; and to Mr. Jobn Clarke in Viaduct Ward and to Mr. Astley King in Wargrave Ward for posit:ons en the Warri%ton Board of Gua,rdians. Arrangements were macl3 for actively assisting the candidates named.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ATTACKED BY A CAT.</p>
        <p>_ Ellen Kiernan, aged four, of 3, Hargreave-street, Parr, St. Helens,was the victim of an attack by an infuriated cat on Sunday morning. It appears that a neighbour has a valuable Persian cat which for safety has been closely confined for some weeks. The cat got out of the room on Sunday, and by some means er other flew at the girl, inflicting extensive injuries to her head and face. The scalp was torn open by severe bites and scratches, and her face was also injured. Dr. Bates was calledin, and the child was removel to St. Helens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE SALARIES OF HEAD TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>Should Length of Service be Recognized?</p>
        <p>PECULIAR POSITION AT ST. JAMES', LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>There was interesting and important business at the meeting of the Warrington Education Committee held at the Town Hall on Monday evening. Mr. F. Monks presided, and there were also present :—The Mayor (Alderman Bolton), the Revs. Morley Stevenson, Father Wilson, and John Yonge, Aiaermen Bennett, Smethurst, and Pierpoiat, Councillors Ellison, Brough, James Evans, Davies, Craik, Dodds, Barton, Roberts, Rogers. Hulse, Tinnion and Maginn, Messrs. E. J. Guest, C. H. Unsworth, G. Walker, E. Plinston, T. May, W. Bibby, A. J. Jolley, J. Upson, B. T. Fairclough, J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Dr. J. G. Gornall (medical officer of health),and Mr.J. Moore Murray (director of education).</p>
        <p>A LONDON CONFERENCE. The first business was to appoint Mr. Murray to repre- sent the committee at an Educational Conference in. London, when the training of teachersand other import- ant matters will be considered.</p>
        <p>AN OLD GRIEVANCE. MR. UPSON AND THE CHARGE FOR THE ; USE OF ROOMS. The minutes of the Finance Committee gave rise to a discussion on the old question of the charge for the use of schoolrooms. This time, however, the facts were of more than ordinary interest. The Committee had considered a letter from the Museum Committee, with reference to the proposed use of the Silver-street school for the purpose of a public evening reading room, and the charges to be made in respect thereof, and had resolved that they could not depart from their terms for the use of school premises for purposes other than those of an elementary school, as previously set forth. Mr. UPSON moved that this minute be referred back. He pointed out that the Council granted the Museum Committee £5O per annum for the purpose of establishing a reading room in Silver-street school. The Museum Committee approached the trustees of that building, and the trustees, seeing the limited amount of money the IVluseurn Committee had to spend, granted the use of the school free of cost, although they had the option of charging rent. The Museum Committee also decided to approach the Finance Committee to see if they could relax their resolution charging 6d. per hour for the use of the building. The charge, which was stated to be for heating and lighting, would amount to 9s. a week, but he contended that the Finance Committee would not be put to that expense as they were receiving fees from various organizations which met in the school. If the committee adhered to their charge of 9s. the reading room scheme would be practically killed. They often heard remarks about the lack of sobriety and morality in Town's End. They had some magnificent public houses, but they had no library,swimming beth,reading room or gymnasium. Even the schools in that end of the town were not all properly catered for. He knew it was said "it will be done." He dare say it would be done when the present generation of children were grandfathers and grandmothers. Mr. MAY seconded, as he thought it was a very im- proper thing to make such a high charge. Alderman SMBTIIIIRST thought Mr. Upson's grievance applied with equal force to penny banks, temperance meetings Ste, which were conducted in the schools. Alderman BENNETT, while being in sympathy with Mr. Upson's remarks, said it was difficult to know where to draw the line. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said that when the scale of charges for heating, lighting and cleaning was drawn up, the general feeling was that if the Committee erred at all, it was on the side of leniency. On a vote being taken, the amendment was defeated by three votes. The committee approved a recommendation that the salary of the treasurer and accounting officer of the , committee be £lOO per annum, as from the Ist April, 1904, Mr. A. J. Jolley remarking that. the Finance Com- mittee considered the proposed remuneration was a very modest recognition of the services Mr. Fairhurst had rendered. PECULIAR POSITION AT ST. JAMES' SCHOOL. THE SALARY OF MR. W. H. STANSFIELD.</p>
        <p>The first recommendation of the School Managemen Committee to aro-use discussion was one respecting the salary of Mr. W. n. Stansfield, the head master of St. James' School, Latchford. It appears that on February sth, 1903, the school managers increased the salary from £250 to £3OO. . The matter was recently reported to the School Management Committee who resolved. "That resolution No. c 444 be varied so that the salary of the headmaster of the St. James' School, Latchford, be at the rate of £250 per annum, as from the Ist April, 1904." Councillor TM. lON moved that the minute be referred back for further consideration, as he contended that if they reduced the salary they would be doing an injus- tice to Mr. Stansfield. He referred to Mr. Stansfleld's devotion to duty, to the efficiency which the school had attained whilst under his control, and to the fact that the highest grants had been earned, although they had not been paid by the Department for the simple reason that the managers had not carried'out their part ofthe arrangement.An in- crease to £3OO was granted in April 1902, but as the managers had to meet the expense of street-making,Mr. Stansfield decided to forego a portion of the salary. If the proposed reduction was now made, Mr. Stansfield would have sacrificed £5O for his devotion to his pro- fession and his loyalty to the managers. Councillor DODDS seconded. Mr. Stansfield had been with them sixteen years and he (Councillor Dodds) be- lieved he possessed higher qualifications as an education- ist than any other man in the town. Mr. E. PL/NSTON said they ought not to punish Mr. Stansfield for anything irregular the managers might have done. Councillor CRIIK said that as a member of the com- mittee he did not vote on the matter because he did not understand it as well as he understood it now. Mr. Stansfield was an excellent schoolmaster, and he con- sidered it would be unfair if the committee insisted upon their resolution. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said some members of the School Management Committee had got up apparently to do penance. He gave a definite vote in committee. He was not ashamed of it, and he was prepared to defend his action. The committee acted on a clear and definite principle, and should not throw their principles over, board. This was not a question of raising one man's salary; and the issue ought not to be fought on per- sonal matters. He recognized in the fullest sense that Mr. Stansfield wasan admirable master. It was said that an injustice was being done, but was it an injustice to raise a man's salary? He admitted they were not , raising his nominal salary. but they were raising his , real salary. (Several Voices: "Question.") Behind , all this, there was a question of principle. Was the , committee, by sending the resolution back almost unanimously, to commit itself to the opinion that any' particular schoolmaster who could got some members of the committee to stand up and support his claim ' very strenuously could squeeze money out of the rates • above the scale, which had been already passed? If so • they must prepare to face at least another penny in the on the rates. • What they were asked to do was, in fact, to put a premium on the managers who under the , old system did not act quite, in his Judgment, squarely and fairly, and thus penalize those who did act squarely. There was an unwritten understanding that no salaries should be raised after the Act came into operation until the new authority entered upon the discharge of its duties. The CHAIRMAN thought the matter lay in a nutshell. While Mr. Jolley might be right as to the principle he (the chairman) thought he was wrong as to the facts. The principle was right, and he was satisfied from what he knew that the managers of St. James' Sohool were the people to blame. Councillor TINNION : But why punish the school master? TheCHAIRMAN : If he had attended to his own inter- ests he would have been in receipt of £3OO a year to- day. It is because the facts have not been sufficiently before the committee I suggest the resolution should be referred back to the committee for full consideration. , The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON, as chairman of the sub-committee, did not oppose the amendment, which I was carried, only \lr. Jolley dissenting. THE SAL:N.IIIES OF HE A.D TEACHERS. SHOULD LENGTH OF SERVICE BE RECOGNIZED?</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the School Management Committee correspondence was submitted from the Warrington Head Teachers' Association, urging that the past ser- vices of the head. teachers should be recognized by the Committee in applying the scale of salaries recently adopted by fie Committee, particularly to the cases of the head mistresses. It was proposed :—" That the salaries of teachers who had alreadylseryed four years as head teachers in schools in the borough, he increased by £lO annually, until the maximum provided by the scale is reached?' This proposal was voted upon and rejected, as was also a resolution: "That, in applying the scale of salaries, each five years of service as a head teacher in the borough be counted as one year of service under the committee." It was then resolved: "That this committee, whilst</p>
        <p>BAKE A CA.KE.—lt's easy, it's economical, it's delici- ous—if you use KEENORA prepared Cake Flour. It's a preparation of the finest and purest ingredients and simplifies the art of cake-making so that a child could make the most delicious cakes. Directions for making cakes are enclosed with each packet. Free half-pound packet on receipt of 2d. stamps for postage. Keenora, Co., 38, Sir Thomas-street, Liverpool. '</p>
        <p>recognizing gratefully the value of the services ren- dered by the head teachers in the borough in the past, do not see their way to recognize length of service as a reason for special treatment in fixing their salaries" ; . _ _ ___</p>
        <p>and" That, while the committee -feel that length of service cannot be taken into account in fixing the salaries of the head teachers, they are prepared to give the most careful consideration to any special cases that may be brought to their notice." The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON, in moving the confir- mation of the minutes, said the Head Teachers' Asso-</p>
        <p>ciation wished the same principle to be applied to the heads as had been applied to the assistants in regard to the recognition of past services. At first sight that seemed to be plausible, but there were many arguments which seemed plausible and required careful investiga- tion before they acoeded to them, and this was one of them. The case_ of a head teacher and assistant teacher</p>
        <p>were not upon all fours in this respect. In the case of assistant teacher it was originally proposed that he should begin at the bottom of the ladder on the spAno terms as one who was just entering the profession. That was not just, but in the case of a head teacher that was not so. They did not ask a head teacher to begin at the bottom of the ladder, but in an assured position of</p>
        <p>honour, a position of financial valtie and Of dignity. The two cases did not seem to be comparable at all,and when an assistant was promoted to _ the position of</p>
        <p>_ . head he could not be said to begin at the bottom of the ladder, buta long way up. He thought the more mem- bers looked at the scale of salaries for teachers the</p>
        <p>more they would see that it was a scale that was just and fair,and would compare favourably with the scale in use in other places. In face of the large expendi-</p>
        <p>tare incumbent Upon thorn, the committee had tried to do what was right and just. (Hear, hear.) The Rev. Father WILSON seconded. The Rev. JOHN YONGE then proposed an amendment that the recomr"endation be referred back to the School Management Committee. There was an old saying that "what was sauce for the goose was sauce for the gander," and inasmuch as the committee in their united wisdom deemed it to be right, expedient and just that the teachers should have their years of service recognized in regard to the settlement of their salary, if it was good in one ease it must of necessity be equally good in the other case. If they admitted the principle of calculating salaries with regard to assistants, who ware most of them junior people, and who had had comparatively little experience, he thought they were bound to apply it in regard to the head teachers, who were seniors in their profession, and who I had served in some cases a very long number of years of arduous and faithful and honourable service. (Hear. hear.) Mr. Yonge instanced a case in whichanassistant who had served 28 years would, by the operation of the new scale, he receiving more than the headmistress. Councillor JAMES EVA_NS seconded.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN called attention to the fact that the increases already given according to the scale amounted to £561. If the amendment were carried, it would mean a further addition of £2BO for last year. Next year there would be an increase of £760 according to the scale, and the passing of the amendment would entail an additional increase of £3BO. Mr. Upson said the commit:,e9 by their resolution put a mistress who had served 30 years on the same level as a girl who had just got her certificat.. ! The principle of recognizing past services was universal,and Warring- ton would be unique in adopting the course suggested by the sub-committee._ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. BUCKTON considered it would not be right to force the head teaohers down to a scale made ofter they had given such long service. If it was a scale estab- lished before they came to the town, and they adopted it with their eyes open, it would be a different thing. Mr. MAY thought that instead of referring the matter back for reconsideration, they should ask the schoolmasters to have n little patience until thp neces- sities of the town, in regard to school accomrdation had been provided for. Councillor MAGEN'N said the committee ought to give some consideration to those who had ser qed the town for years past, and pointed out that most of the old servants were ratepayers. Mr. C. H. US WORTH thought the headmistresses had not been quite so well treated as the headmasters. The committee might consider the adoption of a scheme by which, without putting a great increase on the rates, 1 they could make a similar allowance to the head- mistresses as was made to certificated _assistants.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS alluded to .the feeling of the rate- payers in regard to the alarming increase in expendi- ture brought on by the extension of education. He believed that the public would pay anything if they got good education by the best teachers; they would not find fault with an expenditure which paid just wages to teachers, and which dealt with education as it ought to be dealt with, so that it was put on a level which would bear favourable comparison with other towns. (Hear, hear.) As a result of the jealousies and differences of the sects and so on, education had been starved for a generation, and the new Education Committee had had to take it up and deal with it altogether outside the question of cost. Education must be put on its pro ,er level because, after all, it was far more important than saving in rates. (Hear, hear.) Alderman BENNETT supported the amendment. He had sometimes thought the committee had been dis- posed to act extravagantly, and he thought in the early stages of the work there was something in that fear. He did not, however, like to see the committee acting in a way which savoured of parsimony. The figures quoted by Mr. Upson showed how differently other towns treated their teachers, and he did not want to think Warrington was niggardly in the matter. Alderman SMETHURST thought the committee had been extremely liberal to a great many teachers, and he would even have been prepared to support an amend- ment that the whole question of salaries be referred back. Some of the increases were enormous. The Rev. FATHER WILSON said the question of experi- ence was taken into consideration in fixing the salary of head teachers from the beginning. Experience was one of the qualifications by which assistants gained head teacherships. The MAYOR said he was not always in favour of paying head teachers on a fixed scale. They should be paid according to the results obtained. The Rev. M. STEVENSON said it was peculiarly painful to him to take up the position he had taken up that evening, having been connected with teachers for so many years and having always been on such friendly relations with.them. Nothing would have pleased him better if he had followed his inclination,than to have pro- posed a great increase for those for whom he had the great- est sympathy and respect, but he feltlhe must act not as his inclination prompted him, but as a matter of duty. (Hear, hear.) After considering the matter carefully, the committee had come to the conclusion that it was a fair, just and equitable scale, and, in many respects, better than the scale in force in many places. (Hear, hear). The voting was as follows : For the amendment :—The Rev. John Yonge, Coun- cillors James Evans, IVlaginn, Burton, Dodds, Roberts and Brough, Messrs. Plinston, Guest, Upson, Buckton, and Bibby-12. AGAINST—The Revs Morley Stevenson and Father Wilson, Alderman Smethurst, Councill,ors Davies and Hulse, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, R. T. Fairclough and r. May-8. The amendment was therefore declared carried. • TFIE TRAINING or PUPIL TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>Still another amendment was forthcoming—this time from Mr. May. The School Management Committee had resolved that a number of teachers who failed to obtain a sufficiently high position in the recent King's Scholarship examination to enable them to enter Training Colleges be appointed to various schools at salaries of £35 for males and £26 for females, and that they attend the pupil teachers' classes six half days each week, and to sit for the King's scholarship examination in December next. Mr. May contended that the committee were driving pupil teachers out of the town by insisting upon them attending the classes whether they were willing to do so or not. No doubt the principle was a sound one, but it was not absolutely necessary to have certifi- cated teachers to teach children the " a.b.c." There was no seconder to the amendment which fell to the ground. THE ATTENDANCE Ar DAY SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>HOW THE ACCOMMODATION DIFFICULTY IS BEING MET.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the School Attendance Committee, held on the 10th March, a letter, dated the 2nd March, from the managers of the St. Ann's School, with refer- ence to the special report from the Board of Education, which was submitted to thd committee at their last meeting, stating that they are not in a position to ex- tend their school so as to provide further accommoda- tion was read, and it was resolved, that, in view of the overcrowded state of the St. Ann's National school and the want of school accommodation in that district, the Sites and Buildings Committee be asked to provide, as soon as possible, temporary accommodation in the neighbourhood, and that, in view of its urgent need, it be suggested to the Sites and Buildings Committee that this temporary accommodation should be provided by the erection of iron buildings. A letter, dated the 12th February, from the Board of Education, was also read drawing attention to the fact I that the average attendance of the boys' department of I the Hamilton-street school has,for four`years in succes- sion, exceeded the number for which that department is recognized, whilst the accommodation of the girls' department is also exceeded this year, and the average attendance of the infants' department is only one short of the limit allowed ; and, requesting that, *before the annual grant can be forwarded, the committee will obtain from the members some 'explanation of their disregard of the warnings given by the Board in previ- ous years, and some information with regard to the steps proposed to be taken in the matter. A letter dated the 27th February, from the managers ,of the schools. was also read stating that the average attend- ance of the boys and girls' departments have only exceeded the numbers for which they are recognized by a fraction over two, 'and that, in their opinion, the responsibility for lack of school accommodation should fall on the authority whose duty it is to provide</p>
        <p>THE GREAT SKIN CURE.—" Budden's S.R. Skin Ointment will cure Old Wounds, Ulcerated Legs, Eczema, Ringworm, Scrofula, Piles and Scurvy, and all Skin Diseases. Boxes, 'lid. and 1/lid. Agent for St Helens: J. W. Jackson. Warrington: H. M.Ashton Runcorn: Weston and Co. Eldest() wn : A. Peake Widnes: Lewis and Calvert, Ltd.</p>
        <p>accommodation, and not upon the managers. The Director was requested to communicate the managers' reply to the Board of Education. Councillor ELLISON moved the coaarmation of the minutes, and said the attendance returns were still un- favourable, in consequence of the large amount of sick- ness. In regard to the di qculty in the St. Ann's district, the following table shows how the accommodation was exceeded, viz. :—St. Ann's: Accommodation, 551 ; on the books, 687 ; excess, 135. Silver-street : Accommo- dation, 777 ; oaths books, 815 ; excess, 38., St. Peter's: Accommodation, 278; on the books, 321 ; excess, 43. All those schools were measured on. the old basis of eight square feet per child. The case of the Hamilton- street school illustrated very clearly the fact that school accommodation had not increased propor- tionately with the population. For hur or more years this school had been full, and the Board of Education had withheld the grant until they received the explanation. It was hoped the Beard would take into consideration the fact that this committee had taken some steps to meet one want of accommodation in th it district and pay the grant. It must be remembered that other schools were in the same position, and unless something was done to show that the committee were alive to the necessity, they would lose the grants of more than one school. The supply of the temporary school would give the School Attendance Committee time, but it was hoped at an j early date to lay before this committee a plan on the lines suggested by his Majesty's Inspector. Councillor S. DA.VIES seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor BOGERS—S-ald-il-0-&amp;-ind there were 1,400 children under the age of five years attending school. If they were forced out of school until they were five they would have 1,400 places to fill up. They talked about the bad attendance, but it had been brought about by the Medical Oacer closing th 3 schools. Some of the schools were day nursaries and enabled the parents to get rid of the children. (Hear, hear). Mr. UPSON said some parents would apply to the Board of Education, and they would be compelled to admit the children. Children under five were at school to be nursed and cared for. He alluded to the fact that there were 1,211 sick cases. They had additional school attendance officers and yet the percentage was only 82, which was below the average in the county boroughs. The officers should be more in the schools and streets and less in the office.</p>
        <p>- Mr. J. D. BUCKTON did not think they were bound to take children under five years; certainly the parents were not bound to send them to school till they were five. The minutes were carried.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Sites and Buildings Committee, it was resolved, that the Chairman (the Mayor) Councillor Craik, and Mr. J. Fulton Smith be appointed a deputa- tion to wait upon the Open Spaces Committee with a view to obtaining a site on which to erect a temporary school. That the Surveyor be instructed to obtain tenders and full particulars for the erection of a tem- porary iron school to accommodate three hundred children. The minutes were agreed to, on the motion of the 33333.131 by z. v. F IRCLOUGH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>DIFFERENT STORY</head>
        <p>A DIFFERENT STORY</p>
        <p>"Mrs. M. E. Stevenson, Church Walk, Headcorn,Kent in describing how she felt a few months ago says : "At first I did not know what ailed me; rwas always tired and languid. The least exertion fatigued me, and I felt depressed, out of sorts and fit for nothing. Some days I would be so bad that I could hardly crawl about the house. There was always a dull, aching pain across my loins that seemed to pull me down, and any change in the weather, such as excessive heat or cold, affected me greatlyand made me much worse. "I was recommended to try Dodd's Kidney Pills, and soon after I began to use them I noticed an improve- ment in my health. I kept on with them, and now I am glad to say I can tell a different story; I can go about my work with ease and comfort. I have quite lost that heavy, tired feeling, and I have not any longer that aching pain across my loins. Thanks to Dodd's Kidney Pills, I am completely cured."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>SUIDAY OBSERVANCE.</head>
        <p>SUIDAY OBSERVANCE.</p>
        <p>Rev. F. E. Powell's Address to Men.</p>
        <p>The Drones of Society.</p>
        <p>Tile address to men at the Parish Church last evening was given by the Rev F. E. Powell (vicar of SewerbY, and formerly vicar of St. James' Church, Latchford), who took for his subject "Sunday Observance." The words of the text were :—"This is the day which the Lord hath made; we will rejoioe and be glad in it." (Psalm cxviii.24.) After briefly explaining the historic setting, and reminding his hearers of the supreme moments and days ever to be remembered in the life of every nation, instancing in particular the American July 4th, 1776, and the German January 18th, 1871, and our own Jubilee Day-:--the preacher said that the nearest aiSproach to a day which was cosmopolitan, and which supplied common thought and feelings to nations and races various and distinct, was Sunday. The priceless treasure of a day of rest was unquestionably secured to mankind through religion. Without the religious sanc- tion such an institution would not have been possible in the past history of the world. Formerly it was the festival of the old life and looked back upon creation, which was thought to be final and complete. Since the Christian dispensation, it was the festival of the new life which sought those things which were above, where .Christ was. As the Lord's Day it went back to the Resurrection, when occasion for worship was found to necessitate a day of rest. As the Jewish Sabbath it went 15 centuries further back, even to Mount Sinai, when the weekly day of rest supplied the opportunity for worship, while in tablets dis- hovered in the ruins of a Mesopotamian town, centuries older than the books of Moses, they found that a weekly religious Sabbath was known to the Babylonians and Assyrians, who gave it the beautiful name of the "Day of Rest for the heart." Mr. Powell then adduced medical, social, political and industrial testimony to prove the inestimable benefit, even on the lower grounds, this religious institution has been to mankind. But if religion had obtained for man such a splendid boon in that which affected his temporal and secular wants, she had largely justified her claim to be heard, as she enforced her higher message upon us con- cerning the observance of Sunday. At occasional, it might be rare, intervals the spirit of man throbbed with the consciousness that it was meant for something higher and nobler than that which the conventionalism of ordinary life affected. And if so, man had a weekly recurring opportunity to devote himself to that higher nobler life. It was worse than folly, it was culpability of the grossest kind, by our own selfishness and way- wardness to jeopardise the existence of an institution which we had inherited from past generations. Upon us, then, lay a very heavy, a verysolemn responsibility. We were in possession of a trust, and must see that in spite of certain necessary changes which it were foolish altogether to decry, that trust was handed on unim- paired in its main essential feature to our children and the generations yet unborn. He confessed that the appeal which was often made to so-called Society to observe Sunday better was too partial to be of use. The shameless desecration by a society which had all,the week for its frivolity and pleasures of an institution to which they all owed so much, was only a symptom of a much wider selfishness and idleness which possessed it. The fourth commandment was as much a command to work on six days as it was to rest on the seventh. And the pleasure-loving, luxury-steeped drones of society, who "toil not, neither do they spin," could never appreciate the boon of the Sabbath so long as they neglected the duty and missed the blessing of useful work. But better things were expected of those in whose hands, humanly-speaking, was really the destiny of this great Empire. They owed more to their self-respect than to accept these selfish lovers of ease and luxury as their examples and guides. The question was not one of petty rules and details, but rather of great principles intelligently understood and conscientiously held. And these were practically embodied in Him who not only freed the Sabbath from its sternness and barrenness, its legal pedantry and cheerless observance, insisting that it was made for man—for the development, that was, of man's highest manhood—but also claimed to be the Lord of the Sabbath day in which he intended man to rejoice and be glad. With the acknowledgment of that Divine owndrship most of the difficulties concerning. Sunday observance would vanish. Those who made it a weekly bank holiday in which there was no thought of God or of the higher life of the soul, but a sordid determination to get all the selfish enjoyment out of it, regardless of what it cost man or beast to contribute, were as mistaken and infinitely more wrong than those who went to the opposite extreme and planned for man the old, grim, , loveless, joyless Puritanical Sabbath for which the</p>
        <p>Catholic Church was in no way responsible and which had produced the inevitable reaction. There could h(/ no harm—nay, there was positive good—in listening good music, and in quietly enjoying the splendid all unique treasures in our public museums and galleries, the opening of which on Sunday afternoon would involl but little labour. And, speaking for himself, he wo'-ilu have no compunction in entering into amusements which were innocent in themselves and did not give or necessary work to others, provided—and he begged o° one to cite him as an advocate of Sunday amusements, without remembering that, to his mind, all importfrw provision—that proper, proportionate and full attentio° were paid to the higher claims which Sunday sets forth. Only let them temper the liberty, which was on- doubtedly theirs,to rejoice and be glad with the recog- nition of the authority of the Lord who made the dol. Then throe sound principles would be seen to emerge,, to guide them. Life to be true and complete must expres' itself upward, that yeas Oodward. And that highest life of man might find expression in that service of the altar where they might contemplate, unhindered if tbl. would, by any intrasive words of man, the purest, subl! mast ideals the world had,while acknowledging in lovvh" eat self-abasement their own sins and insufficienat• "Worship," then, was the word to express that principle ; and" Rest "expressed the second, not tf., I lazy indolence of the sluggard, but a Sabbath for in°l I vidual development and the deepening of character. The word "Service "expressed the third principle. To.ch; good to others, to make the world brighter and hapPif, was a very proper use of Sunday. In conclusion, t', rev, gentleman begged his hearers not to allow the,°l., , selves to carelessly drift in that matter, but to intenl, gently understand the principles at issue, and to col°, ageously apply them. So long as human nature mined what it was, and so long as the things will% . were temporal and were seen tended to obscure ally, make us forget the things which were eternal and unsed so long would the obligation to rest, and to serve to worship—to look inward and outward and upwarc!,4 survive. And week by week the magnificent opporturn", 1 for that three-fold duty was peesented when on Suoc.l,9ilr they might exclaim—determined to temper liberty 01.',11 lauthority, independence with control, freedom discipline—" This is the day which the Lord hath witu"' we will rejoice and be glad in it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ETOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>ETOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>FORM ITION OP A WOMEN'S LIBERAL LEA G Tuesday afternoon a meeting of the ladies residing Stockton Heath will be held at Mr. Joseph Mounflelo'l Walton, for the purpose of forming a Women's Lbeo League.</p>
        <p>Clair CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR SOCIAL.—On Wednes night a social in connection with the Hill Cliffe ChriStj Endeavour Society was held in the schoolroom of Chile Baptist Chapel, when about 25 members friends assembled. The proceedings w.-re of a val'ee character, and proved to be of an enjoyable nattlf there being refreshments, choice music, games and shc't speeches. Slf ELECTION OF DISTRICT COUNCILLORS AND PA-1t COUNCILLORS.—The• poll for the election of the Distrie, and Parish Councillors takes place on Monday froni o'clock noon to 9 o'clock in the evening. It is exp_etre the result will be disclosed the same evening. lu'ts are three candidates for the two District Council and 17 candidates for the 12 Parish Council seats. v e 5,5 little canvassing is being indulged in, only, so f' can be learned, about three candidates indulging in wity dying electioneering mode. The chief question bef4o the electors is undoubtedly the educational one. present school is admitted on all hands not only to 1)11-0 much too small, but also to be very much behind to times in many other respects. The Stockton 1485,, ratepayers will be in the very fortunate position, •",5t new school is provided, of having the half of the Cvto provided by the County, and also 50 years in which 0 pay the remaining half. It has been calculated, t(); that the grant that can be earned by an increased fib, age attendance of 100 more scholars in Stockton 110.3,, will repay to the ratepayers the cost of a new bulb:110o</p>
        <p>Local Oddfellowshlp. The members of the " Constitutio " Lodge Oddfellows assembled on Saturday evening to reel: nize the past services of Bro. R. G-. Brandreth, F. by presenting him with a framed emblem. There ,6 a large gathering, P.G. Lester presiding. ri-4, presentation was made by Bro. T. S. Steel P.P.a.' who said Bro. Brandreth had proved himself a wortlii,"; member and had striven for the advancement ef Manchester Unity and its great principles.—Ok speeches were delivered and Bro. Brandreth suital/J/0 acknowledged the gift. An enjoyable pregral, was submitted by P.P.G.M. Alban Boyle, Bro. t, Reddish E.S., Bro. A. Pemberton, P.G. A. Burdet, P.G.'s Houghton, H. Lloyd, and A. C. Smithse' Bro. R. Haigh accompanied.</p>
