      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>u:1210 Royal Marriage Act.—There are many good to7,nns why Royal personages should not be permitted t•,...'untract morganatic marriages; but at the same ~1"-Le the British people were never inclined to judge tile Duke of Cambridge veryharshly for his marriage to bre ladYwho was professionally known as "Miss Fare- _ °other." Possibly the Royal Marriage Act of 1772 uld have been more popular if it had been passed in rtkore thr agreeable circumstances. As it was, it was carried irMBh Parliament to please George 111 who freely his:a-Ted in threats to induce his Ministers to carry out VI- 'wishes. In consequence of his morganatic marriage ,e ttere itle of Duke of Cambridge becomes extinct, though .r. ~s nothing to prevent one of his sons being raised Izte Peerage. The Duke leaves three sons and one • nghter, the eldest being Colonel Fitz George, who is °,11,1v sixtY-one. the The Duke's Romantic Marriage.—The marriage of Like of Cambridge with Miss Farebrother was `, ~Puted to be a singularly happy one. The lady. was ii;11811ter of the proprietor of a well-known printing liarin London and was a burlesque actress of note in ttiii,"'Y. The marriage took place in Dublin, where the e, then a Young beau, was quartered with his regi- rs'e• llt• He was not then a Duke, and, after the core- took a small unpretentious house in the neigh- cOrhood of the Royal Barracks. The Duke of trubridge, in fact, was never wealthy for a member of the.,, RA oYal Family, and his sons though brought up in co- inY and Navy services, have engaged in various hanThiercial interests.—Colonel Fitz George appears to v,e inherited some of the business instincts of his ~.,3'errial grandfather, as he was at one time thwpro- ;iient+o:_r _of a Sunday newspaper. and held a share insome WOrks. taourning for the late Duke.—The West End dress- er ders and tailors have been driven almost to the verge wh".esPeration with mourning orders which flowed in It ,:;131the death of the Duke of Cambridge becameknown. dirl,ll4, e custom now-a-days to don sable hued garments thVY the bereavement takes place, and not wait until th- "aY of the funeral as was once our wont, so, despite a 'l:lfact that society women are always careful to have reshack outfit by them, there is invariably a terrible exhi when a death occurs in our Royal Farily. At the kbition of Irish Industries which took place at Gros- r House only a few hours after the death of the beet' almost all the women were in mourning, the Y etea es of Marlborough's toilette being an exquisite in grey and black. Dr:" Postponed Courts.—The majority of the gowns Prep for the two Courts—which had unhappily to bro'°BtPoned—shewed handsome trains of home-made tfo eades, or velvets. This is a mdve in the right direc- /Ssat_rsienso,t only does such a move benefit our home but it also benefits ourselves. When the long thellil) LW'S answered the purpose for which it is created, hr or velvet contained therein will be found ca,q'tstifficient for a dinner gown' while the soft lining 1.;.4.W5, utilised for a tea—or as it, is now called—a tol-,e"'ng gown." The transparent train has been found of „Auite as costly as one of velvet orbrocade, and it aeVtle or no use after it has passed the " Presence, ' Nvit,°olylooks well when the wearer stands quite still, bir", the train spread carefully out, over carpet. ectlY she moves it has an unfortunate habit of curl- irlillland looking anything but handsome or dignified. la 8" Laee--Light, delicate Carrickmacross lace, is reat request, and sold well at the Irish Industries lace" St. Patrick's Day. The collars in this beautiful • th:ere Particularly sought after, and should prove tiNagreatest use to the purchasers. There are two that th(nie is always sure of at this annual sale, one is obtainsLarticle offered will be the best of the kind ,asked e,and the other is that one is sure of not being 'nee th,„-`nre for an article than it is worth. Of dyed _lnateh ';e is little seen, but when it is utilised, it must oress, toe colour of the garment it trims, exactly. it e°' usually get over this difficulty by sending In to he "ice, with a pattern of the material it is to trim, vd..ts apileociallyrs dyed the precise colour. Dressmakers employ the same method with regard to 1„7:!cl, which must also match exactly the fabric upon uA.sleh it is laid. Lo---,11. Interesting Dress Exhibition.—Women visitors to e "0 should not miss the Dress Exhibition at the ArYstal Palace, which remains open until the end of tj)ril• There is much to be seen that is interesting,and , Make one envious too, as the exhibits include all latest " creations" from the leading houses of Paris .7f• d London. Many are of the most costly kind,approach- i,n to threg figures, but some are priced as low as the "tibia five-pound note, and any may be purchased by Crairers who possess the necessary wherewithal. No ~:ans fascinating is the exquisite jewellery, displayed ;13°11 some of the models, and the historical exhibits :re also very interesting. The collection of peasant vostumes of all nations, lent by Mr. and Mrs. Berens, is alillieue, and gives an idea of the different styles in Of rent countries. Princess Christian, Princess Henry Zebnattenberg, and many leaders of fashion including h uchesses of Marlborough, and Westminster have ,"fnPed to make the exhibition thoroughly representa- te, and of interest to visitors. I'°naan Suffrage.—Some people say that female hiV• age would benefit the Conservatives, because the Nrs, of women ih generally in the direction of Conser- hc,"ann, while others think that its introduction would wOOl advantage advantage to the Liberals, because Nonconformist Dan), who are mostly Liberals, pay more attention to Iv "los than their sisters,of the Established Church,and Ol ould go to more trouble to record their votes. An Parliamentary hand "said he was not at all sure t the effect would be of giving the Parliamentary bnte to women, but he was inclined to think it would at‘ sit