      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAL WARRINGTON CRICKET AND Fashion is at best a fickle jade and yet in BOWLING CLUB. her devotion to the blouse, with its ever- widening sphere of usefulness, she has dis- played a constancy altogether contradictory to her character as we have hitherto known it. There are no signs in the dawning modes ot any waning of popularity, but instead are many charming novel features in the latest model blouses which will endear this desirable bit of dress to all beholders and give it a fresh lease of life. The collarless blouse, except for evening wear, is now non-existent for we met it last season too constantly, and often under very inadvantageous circumstances, a state of affairs which sealed its doom in the world of dress. The fashion was certainly a pretty and most becoming one when exploited by the possessor of a white, rounded throat, but when adopted by all promiscuously, whether nature had or had not endowed them with a pretty neck, the result was too often deplorable. A. lean brown throat should not be left exposed</p>
        <p>&lt;Tr „it • 4 '4OO tsl.. • .0. tgdt „ I po I I / •,/ .744, k \\\</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>PRETTY BLOUSE,</head>
        <p>A PRETTY BLOUSE,</p>
        <p>to criticism but skilfully concealed by soft folds of lace or tulle, and the newest blouses certainly are designed to conceal all such deficiencies. Unfortunately, as is too often the case, the reaction is inclined to be too great and the change from the collarless blouse to that completed by a stiff upstanding linen collar is sudden and extreme.</p>
        <p>The pretty blouse depicted, however, is of a more elaborate style and has its lace collar and yoke cut in one. It is made of soft white washing silk and is further trimmed with lace insertion finished off at the points by white silk cord and tassels. The space between the points is filled in by tiny tucks. The yoke extends over the shoulders, as is fashionable, and the sleeves, which increase in width to the wrists, where they form a deep pouf, are caught in at the wrists by bands of lace, finished by a little pleating. The style is one of the newest and best and could be ex- pressed in fine white muslin and embroidery worn over a silk slip if preferred. There are also among the novelties some pretty short blouses of line enhanced by a design of coarse</p>
        <p>f(iS ..,ir g--.:cl, i-: N, , , . cz- *P.* ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>DAINTY TOQUE.</head>
        <p>A DAINTY TOQUE.</p>
        <p>hand-made embroidery. Shirts of this description are invariably completed by a plain linen collar, and these collars, by the bye, differ considerably from the old fashioned ones as they are rounded and shaped to fit the throat easily and closely and are fastened at the back of the neck by tiny studs. They are only worn with linen and batiste shirts, lace or silk bands finishing the necks of silk or cashmere blouses. .</p>
        <p>Even although the weather may continue variable and oftentimes wet and unpleasant there are brief intervals of sunshine and it is then that one's thoughts turn to millinery. The dainty toque illustrated is just the style suited to these occasions as, although dressy, it is not too elaborate. It is made of burnt straw with the brim swathed with lace of the same colour, while the front is finished by an aigrette and ostrich tip of a brownish shade. Brown is, indeed, in all its shades the most fashionable .colour this season and will con- tinue to be much worn all summer. It is at times intermingled with yellow and some crJ</p>
        <p>A SMART TAILOR-MADE COAT.</p>
        <p>the newest toques and hats are swathed with gauze scarfs which shade from the softest yellow into orange and thence deepen into a rich brown. These colours certainly harmon- ize well together and axe very cleverly inter- mingled and contrasted so as to obtain charming effects. It requires a trained and educated eye, however, to select just the tones to become the complexion and colouring of thc wearer if success is to be attained. My third sketch shows a very smart coat of the tailor-made order, the cut and style being of quite the latest mode. A simple coat like this, however, must be well fitted and fashion- ed by a tailor—not a dressmaker—as it re- quires the pressing into shape which a tailor alone seems able to give the garment he turns out. It is in the Plainest garments that experience is necessary, and a plain fitting coat like that illustrated should never be attempted by the home worker, who should confine her attempts to blouses or coats of the bolero style, and get a coat and skirt made by as good a firm as her means will permit during the present demi-saison, when prices are greatly reduced. One such costume each year will enable one always to appear well dressed, especially if it is always carefully removed when indoors, brushed and hung away in the wardrobe by the loops provided for that purpose, as, if left on a chair or thrown on the bed it will soon lose its look. COQUETTEI</p>
