      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Disappoiriting 531331.</head>
        <p>A Disappoiriting 531331.</p>
        <p>Last season was a disappointing one for the Warring- tonCricket and Bowling Club, and at the annual meet- ing held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Monday evening, it was reported that there was an adverse balance to be met. The membership, too, has decreased and several speakerslaid stress upon the necessity for more interest and activity to place the club upon a sounding footing. Councillor TINNroN remarked that what with golf clubs, &amp;c, they were going to have a very hard fight to maintain the club, and if they could find some means of Increasing their membership, the subscriptions and the income of the club, they should certainly do so. They should take the matter seriously into consideration, and endeavour to enlist as many new members as possible, ant thereby ensure what he (Councillor Tinnion) con- sidered would be necessary for their future prosperity. (Applause). They were just on the down grade, and it was always best at that time to take the bull by the horns and stops that retrogression. (Hear. hear). He hoped that whatever they did that night would be done in the best interests of the club. Let them all endeavour as far as they could to try and stop what seemed to him to be a serious decline in the prosperity of the club. (Applause) The balance sheet was adopted. ELECTION OF OFFICERS. _</p>
        <p>Mr. FRED HOWARD was elected president for the en- suing season, and Mr. Henry Greenall, the retiring president, was elected a vice-president and thanked for his services.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. H. Silcock was re-elected hon. treasurer, Mr R. T. FAIRCLOUGH. paying a tribute to the capable manner in which he had managed the finances. The members unanimously re-elected Mr. C. H. Unsworth to the position of hon. secretary which he has admirably fulfilled for some years past. In returning thanks Mr. Unsworth said the balance sheet rather disappointed him. Still, it was not the worst balance sheet the club had had, and he thought that if every member would make up his mind to induce some friend to join, they would go along all right, and be able to bring their membership up to its normal con- dition. It had always been his wish to get the number of members up to 300, and it had reached that figure or bad been very close to it for several years, but they saw that last year it had dropped considerably below that number, for it was now only a little over 200. If every member would put his shoulder to the wheel and assist them in their difficulties, they would have a much better balance sheet than the present one, to show at the end of the year. (Applause.) Mr. E. A. Connor was elected captain of the first team ; Mr. James Callon, sub-captain ; Mr. Edwin Pierpoint, captain of the second team ; and Mr. W. H. Podmore, match secretary. Mr. Thomas Cooper was .elected Bowling Club secre- tary, and Mr. R. T. Fairclougb, captain. Mr. Walter Longdin was unanimously appointed secretary to the Tennis Club. Councillor Tinnion was re-elected hon. auditor,. _</p>
        <p>The committee was elected as follows ,—Cricket Club :—Messrs. J. W. Bibby, E. A. Connor, P. B. Hawten and E. Pierpoint. Bowling Committee : Messrs. H. Daintith, H. H. Evans, W. Gill, and J. Dunne. Tenpis : Mr. J. Bayley Isaac.</p>
