        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and cent in, if possible,not later than Thursday.</p>
        <p>gona, We have been compelled, owing to pressure oit out space, to hob" your letter 'over until next week.— ED. Ex."]</p>
        <p>The Ragged School and the Distress. Sir,—The Soup Kitchea at the above Mission has been opened -daily during the week, and 90 gapons of soup andl3olbs of bread have been distributed each 'clay at four o'clock. The money, collected in the streets chiefly, and the provisions given, are now exhausted, and unless support is forthcoming, we shall be com- pelled to °lose the kitchen after this week. A great number of men, N.vomen and children have been fed on the premises, having aothing to carry the soup away</p>
        <p>in. The donors of provisions this week are Messrs. G. Bowcook,J. Bowcock,Tunstall, Mynekeyme,and Single- ton (meat); Messrs. F. Lindley andJ. Bate (vegetables); :Messrs. Hallwood and James Bate (bread, peas, &amp;c.) We appeal for further help to enatie us to keep the kitchen op3n. Everything received will be acknow- ledged. Provisions may be sent direct to the school; money to Mr. James Skelland, or to the Financial Secretary. JOHN W. HOWARTH Stockton Heath. 25th March,`lo34.</p>
        <p>The Unemployed. Sir,—The reports from the employers of labour, •regarding the condition of employment in Warrington, have no doubt had an effect in prevailing on many to -withhold theft support who otherwise would have willingly given 'assistance towards relieving the distress which undoubtedly, prevails at the present time, owing to unemployment. Personally I attach little value to those reports in ascertaining the extent of local distress and the number of unemployed. The respective employers are only able to give information as to the state of employment at present existing in their own works, some of the reports being interlarded with gibes at the working man, for refusing a reduction in wages, and for attending the football matches in such large numbers. There is no evidence to prove that many of the unemployed were there. Furthermore, a large pro- portion of those present at the matches were not residents in Warrington. Can the employers tell us what has become of the men who have been dismissed in batches from various works during the past month? My opinion is that some of the reports were given with a deliberate intention of minimising the amount of distress which exists. The proportion of unemployed amorp, the skilled trades has been and is at presen,„ much higher than for -some considerable time, and it necessarily follows that a corresponding proportion of unskilled men are out of work in consequence. Then again, in addition to the total-unemployment, many industries, such as the cotton, wire and iron are work- ing short time. It seems to me that instead of invit- ing the opinions of employers, more accurate inform- ation could be gained from men whb are in daily con- tact with the working classes, such as assurance agents, coal dealers, rent collectors, grocers and provision dealers, &amp;c. Without wishing to appear as an alarmist, or to ex- aggerate in the least, I can say that the opinion of the men referred to—and that opinion has been formed by actual observation and experience—is, that much dis- tress prevails at the present time in Warrington, and that amongst the ironworkers families especially isthe distress is very acute. With the object of raising funds to assist in relieving the distress, a concert will be given in Parr Hall on Monday next, I appeal to your readers to give us every assistance uninfluenced by the reports from employers of labour.—Yours, &amp;c., 160, Battersby-lane, Warrington.</p>
        <p>FRED STOTT.</p>
        <p>[We have also received an appeal frora Mr. A" Warburton, Cairo-street, urging the claims of the un- employed and requesting the public to patronise the concert on Monday.—ED. " Ex."]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Chinese Labour —A Defence.</head>
        <p>Chinese Labour —A Defence.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Much has been said on this question,and I may venture to say by people who know little or nothing .about the question of things as they exist in South Africa to-day and have done for many months. I write, Sir, to protest against the enrol, libellous and unjwati- fled word" Slavery "that is being used in this country just now for political purposes evidently and which we have heard a great deal of lately in Warrington. Perhaps a few plain facts would be interest- ing to your readers as well as to some of the speakers who have succeeded in gulling their hearers at different meetings lately, the latter of whom I now defy to proYe that the introduction of Chinese to work down the mines under the conditions proposed is any form of slavery in any sense of the word. They will be recruited voluntarily, and do not nee4 to go to South Africa unless they want. They will be paid a wage for their work, and if they are care- ful will be able to save more by the expiration of their term than a labourinc, man would in England in the same time, and then be taken back to their own country. I will put politics on one fide. It has no interest to me or the majority of the people on the Rand who have had this ques- tion at heart for months, and meeting after meeting has been held to protest against the importation of any kind of coloured labour whatever. Everything has been done to avoid this extreme step. The matter has been thrashed out by workingmen on the Rand, human, free- dom-loving subjects of the King on the spot who are in the best position to judge and who now consent to the introduction of Chinese under the proposed conditions only, but who would never consent to any form of slavery being carried on. In fact the conditions never suggested such a word to them, and I think we in this country ought to trust them and I say with sincerity i that it s very unfair and a libel on our brother colonists to interfere and talk about "slavery." The compound system has existed for years for the Baffirs, who have been under similar regulations pro- posed for the Chinese; in fact, the whole business is similar, only corporal punishment will not be adminis- tered as was done under the Dutch Government. It is easy for speakers to stand on a platform and criticise the British Government on this question. But the British Government no more dare consent to the im- portation of Chinese into the Transvaal against the desire of the workingmen there than they dare bring Chinese into this country to work. It is the wish of the workingmen there now that the Chinese should be imported, as it has been proved to be the only way out of the difficulty, and as we know a bill has been passed by the Transvaal Legislature for the purpose. Surely it would be a scandalous shame if the British Government were now to block the way and so keep out of work the thousands of men on the Rand who are anxious and willing to work and who have waited quite long enough for the solution of this question, and are now in a pitiful state as the extract from a letter herewith explains : "Just a few lines to let you know how things are going on. lam very sorry to tell you things are going from bad to worse. *I feel quite positive if 1. wanted a thousand men to work for their food I could get that number in twenty-four hours. It is simply dishearten- ing to walk round by the Post Olce in Johannesburg and see the hundreds of fellows with nothing to do and nothing to eat,right from the lowest class of individuals to the best class gentleman. Everyone is waiting for things to boom but that will not come off till we get the Chinaman here. Everyone here (with the exception of a few Dutch) are anxious now that the Chinese should come." In a letter by the last South African mail the same writer says :—" I see those Radicals are opposing the Chinese Bill. If we do not get them here shortly the country will be gone to the dogs." Since the previous part of this letter was written I see in your issue a letter by Mr. C. E. Parker. Tam glad he is not so narrow-minded on the subject as many, tut a good deal of his letter is imagination. He speaks of Kimberley Mines. It is not the shortage of labour on the Kimberley Mines at all that has brought about this question, and Kimberley is not in the Transvaal, but some two days train jou.ney from the Rand. Again,ho says :—" Such punishment might have been by lash had it not been for the Chinese IVlinister in London," etc. Nothing of the kind. Mr. Parker and everybody else ought to know that the lash has not been allowed in South Africa since Peace was declared, and there are plenty of police on thern Rand, both Colonial and native. There is nothing at all harsh in the regulations with regard to the compounds. One must remember the class of people. The Ordinance has been drafted for the protection and safety of the white popula- tion. Suppose the writer in your issue was manager of a mine and lived on the premises with wife and family, as many of the mine managers do. Some of the mines are rather isolated. There are some hundreds of black men or yellow men or perhaps both employed an the mine, Would it be wise,would it be nice for theta to have a roving commission round his house or anywhere they liked to go day and night ? The natives always have had" Passes" to go about, and a special pass from his employer if he is out of his dis- trict or out after 9 p.m. White labour has to some extent been tried on some of the mines on the Rand, and, T believe, had a fair trial, for I myself heard a manager who had the experi- ment in hand speak on the subject, and from his remarks the scheme had proved unworkable, and the majority of the people there are not desirous of seeing white men doing natives' work and perhaps working alongside them. The Ordinance has not been drafted without a good deal of fighting and thought to satisfy the workingmen and residents of the Transvaal and when they get their franchise if it is then found that the mines can be worked without the Chinese, they will have an easy remedy, but at present there is no other way out of the difficulty.</p>
        <p>F. 13. GRIMSHAW.</p>
        <p>Suaakey, March 25th, 1904.</p>
      </div>
