      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>"Lucky Warrington"! was the phrase on every- body's lips after Saturday's cup-tie with Wigan. The visitors had nearly all the play. Warrington were clearly out of the hunt until five minutes from time, when they pulled the match out of the fire, and left the Wiganers lamenting. Nearly everybody had become resigned to a re-play at Wigan and the probable ex- tinction of the primrose and blues, when the chance which won the =ltch came, and was gratefully—oh so gratefully—accepted. I don't suggest that War- rington won by a fluke, the try was a fine one in every respect, but Warrington were distinctly lucky to score at the psychological moment. Anyhow, the result wasaccording to expectations before the match, if the game itself was not, and Warrington pass into the third round with the semi-finals looming right ahead.</p>
        <p>The elements and everything else conspired in War- rington's favour on Saturday. Whereas they had a wet afternoon in most places, the rain passed away from Wilderspool before the kick-off and left the turf in excellent order for a fast game. The Wigan folk were by no means deterred by the showers of the morning, and they invaded Warrington in their thous- ands, probably close on 4,000, supporting the wearers of the red and white hoops. Warrington and the sur- rounding districts also poured their inhabitants into the Wilderspool ground which, at 3.30, presented a picture not readily to. be forgotten. The available accommodation was fully occupied and whichever way you looked there was a solid wall of faces. Even the , roof of the stand on the popular side was lined with spectators, while tha hoarding along the railway end carried a load of enthusiasts who drummed with their heels when excitement got the betty of them—and this was not at all infrequently. Over 10,500 people paid for admission, the full number present being be- tween eleven and twelve thousand. The receipts totalled the magnificent sum of £3ls—beating the re- cord for the enclosure by over £BO. With a half share of £l7O on the Saturday before, half £l6O on Wed- nesday, and a goodly proportion of the Wigan gate, Warrington have fairly" struck ile " in this season's cup-ties.</p>
        <p>The visitors had evidently spared no pains to bring about Warrington's defeat, and the men looked 'very fit. Considering the home team's recent performances the Wiganers were remarkably confident. The;r plan of campaign was soon disclosed. It was to be forward game, and the 'visitors adopted close tactics with remarkable success. Whether the mid-week match had taken something out of the home forwards or not I cannot say, but the fact remains that they put up a very poor show against the burly colliers. Time after time the oval came out on the Wigan side, and Warrington were kept busy defending. Still they had chances in the first five minutes which might have won the match, but the backs did not combine well. As time wore on it became apparent that Warring- ton's success was in the nature of a far remote possi- bilify. Wigan attacked with persistency. There was a lucky escape for the homesters when Barr had a try disallowed after charging Hallarn's kick. How- ever, half-time arrived with the score-sheet blank.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the Wigan forwards, carried all before them and there was the pale hue of fright on many a Warrington cheek. The players stuck to their work grimly enough and repelled efforts by Barr and Leytham to cross. Davies of Wigan essayed to drop a goal, and when this failed he visitors seemed to lose hope of breaking, down the defence and set themselves out for playing Warrington to a draw. They seemed certain enough to succeed in this, for they were continually in possession and kicked to touch with a systematic regularity which exasperated the home supporters greatly. They wanted Wigan to open the game out and give the speedier Warrington backs some chance,but, no thank you, that was not Wigan's idea. Time wore on and the visit- ing spectators began to wax sarcastic and issue invitations to Wigan on Wednesday! Warrington seemed in a hopeless plight and when they cot to half way there was an encour- aging cheer. Somehow the home scrummagers secured the ball, Davies threw to Harris and the powerful right-winger dashed down touch until he was near 'the Wigan line. Then another "serum." Wigan brought a man out, Warrington got the ball again, Bate made a sham pass, Davies and Dickenson com- bined and the lengthy centre legged it for the line as if Satan were behind him. That one supreme effort did the trick, for Dickenson rushed over with a dozen hands grasping at his heels. Then what a cheer and what a scene ! Hats and sticks and arms wildly waving in the air as the pent-up excitement of the Warrington crowd found relief. On the field the Wtganers "jest sot down" and swore, while Hallam grasped Dickenson's right hand and shook it as if he were welcoming a long-lost brother. It was a glorious moment for Warrington. Though Fish missed the goal by inches, the home team had won, and what greater joy could life coatain for the'moment !</p>
        <p>My sympathies to Wigan! likewise my severe con- demnation. They will perhaps realize now the folly, of playing for a draw. Had they kept up the pressure and pegged away at the Warrington line they must have won eventually. No defence in the world can withstand constant attack. If they had only played to Barr it would have sufficed, for the big sprinter was a dangerous man and came solnear scoring twice that War rington felt better when Wigan ceased to give him pass- es. The visiting backs were not so poor that they shouid be constantly xeglected. Over confidence has lost many a game, and it was Wigan's certainty of making a draw that proved their undoing.</p>
        <p>All praise to Warrington for the, gallant effort which turned the tables. The backs got just one chance and they took it, Dickenson again proving what a danger- ous man he is, when near the line. There is little more to be said except to give Hallam praise for a fine defensive display. It is not generally known that he was far from well and could only be persuaded to turn out at the last minute. The one mistake he made is readily forgiven under the circumstances.</p>
        <p>Luck has favoured Warrington fairly in the draw i for the third round, and there s nothing but rejoicing that Warrington have to tackle the conquerers of Batley even on foreign soil. Surely they can demon- strate their superiority over a second division team powerful though the latter are in the forward division' By the way, there is no truth in the report that War' rington endeavoured to arrange the match at Wilder pool by offering £l5O. The Pontefract Club were never even approached, and the Warrington Committee have no intention of buying th-3ir way into the semi-finals.</p>
        <p>For a week Warrington are free from cup-tie turmoil and to-day they are at home to Bradford, the leaders of the League, and undoubtedly the finest body of men playing under Northern Union rules. Warrington owe them something for the 14-0 defeat at Park Avenue, but I am doubtful of their ability to avenge themselves to-day. Bradford are sure to have a big pull forward, and it will need all the ability of the Warrington backs to secure points. However, we shall see. Warrington will not lose for want of trying.</p>
        <p>Unfortunately four changes vill be necessary, Dickenson taking a well-earned rest, and Harmer, Fairhurst and Naylor being on the injured list. The team will therefore be: Hallam, full back ; Fish, Isherwo3d, Hockenhall and Harris, three-quarters; Bate and Davies, half backs; Boardman, Thomas, Swift, Jolley, Edmondson, Morrison, Cook, and Lunt, forwards_</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows For. Against.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. -T-.-- P. G. 714. P. Pts Bradford 28...20... 6... 2...37 64-266...13 19-83...42 Salford 27...19... 6... 2...47 65-289...11 20-82...40 Hunslet 28...19... 8... 1...39 43-207...17 28-118 39 Br'ghton R 27...17... 6... 4...25 68-254...23 19-103..33 Le.ds 25...16... 5... 4...21 38-156-12 21-87...36 Oldham 28...16... 9... 3...28 40-176-11 17-73...35 Warrington 26...13...10... 3...22 38-158...17 23-103..29 Hull K't'n R 28...13...13... 2...21 28-125...16 35-137 28 Halifax 27...12...12... 3...19, 20— 98...21, 20-102..27 Swinton Runcorn Widnes 29... 8...16... 5...17 17— 85...26 52-208..21 Wigan Leigh Batley Hull Keighley 26... 5...16... 5...24 12— 84...36 54-234..15 Huddersfi'ld 27... 6...21... 0...12 30-114...39 58-252..12</p>
        <p>In connection with the Cup-tie at Pontefract next Saturday two specials will be run from Warring-ton one on the London and North-Western and the other on the Cheshire Lines. Several saloons have already been engaged, and parties desiring to make similar</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>MUSICAL SOME'TY'S DICERT.</head>
        <p>THE MUSICAL SOME'TY'S DICERT.</p>
        <p>Gounol's "Redemption."</p>
        <p>The Warrington Musical Society closed its season on Wednesday by a performance in the Parr Hall of Gounod's 'Trilogy "The Redemption." Although by reason of its sacred character such a work might be considered peculiarly appropriate for performa.nce dur- ing the Lenten interval, the oratorio failed to attract anything approaching to a crowded audience. Nor, thus disappointing reasonable expectations, did the presentment of the work by the society—entirely com- mensurate though that was,—aeouse any great enthusiasm mongstsuoh is 1,33.) a 1s1) to listen to it. &amp;lona result is the more inexplicable because public interest in "The Redemption" is, at this time of day, really the main if not the only justification for its being per- ' formed at all, for it is pretty generally agreed that fail- ing its popularity the work has slead3r claim to be regarded favourably, much less of ranking as a truly religious work in the sense in which we are accus- tomed to speak of other oratorios. But though "The Redemption" may possibly not grow in one's estima- tion on closer acquaintance, it must be acknowledged that the grandeur of its dimensions and the imposing character of many of its "effects" are undeniably fit matters for admiration. It is pleasing therefore to record the fact that the Aasical Society's second per- formance of Gounod's masterpiece after a four year's interval was from a musical point of view in all essentials complete and adequate, and one accordingly worthy to rank with the society's most brilliant achievements. Members one and all sang the bigger choruses with a unity of purpose and a precision which entirely realised what must be conceived to have been the composer's intentions, while now and then as in "The Reproaches" there was infused into the numbers depending on greater sublety of expression, a maximum amount of genuine and devotional feeling. The fine ens enable realised in "Unfold ye portals" and in the Finale was especielly admirable, and here and throughout the oratorio the singing of the chorus was of a uniform eeoallence which was highly credit- able and praiseworthy ; weak entries and anything in the nature of serious slips being singularly few and in- conspicuous. Miss Winifred Wynne, Miss Maud Turner, Mr. James Davis and Mr. Charles Knowles i proved themselves an efficient if not exactly an ideal quartet of principals, and individually and collectively they sang their parts with a becoming reverence and a musical delivery which was almost always entirely satisfactory. Mach of the music for the tenor and bass associated with the character of" Narrators" is, owing to the exigencies of the "book," necessarily of a monotone character, and effort to relieve the dulness and tedium of those parts of Gounod's score would probably be wasted. Yet the trilogy is not wanting in opportunities for the display of musicianly qualities on the part of both and of these good and effective use was made. For Miss Wynne some allowances must be made inasmuch as it was early apparent that she was suffering from indisposition. Handicapped though she was in this way she nevertheless so far rose to the occasion in the melodious portions of the Pentecost music, as to bring out its best points. Miss Mary Ciollin, one of the society's members, took part along w th the two ladies already named in the trio of Holy Womon &amp;c., a tasteful and effective rendering of these coneerted portions of the oratorio being the outcome of their joint efforts. The orchestra is to be congratulated on the all-round excellence of its work, and remember- ing that the laying on, so to speak, of tone-colour in a wealth of detail, as well as in broad misses, is an im- portant pert of Gounod's stock-in-trade, it will be gathered that this work was of an arduous nature. The two pairs of extra trumpeters, whoseantiphonal calls in the" Untold ye.portals" and other choruses give them such an imposing effect, played their parts with remarkable steadiness thereby contributing not a little to the success of these numb3rs. And similarly, in dealing in solo form with the oft-recurring theme representative of the Saviour, the leachr of the orcUestra Mr. R. H. Pearson, is also deserving of a mused of praise, while Mr. Crossley, in wielding the baton and in this way skilfully guiding all and sundry through the intricacies of the ' score, was, of course, quite indiepensible to the undoubted success achieved at this concert. Adverting again to the consensus of opinion held by competent judges in regard to this the most ambitious work of Gounod's life, it may net be without interest to quote the following strictures from The Manchester Guardian:—"lf the question be asked on what grounds so 'large a measure of public favour has been accorded to The Redemption' we are not aware of any convinc- ing or satisfactory answer. First and foremost mist be reckoned G3anod's cool intentions added to his antecedent popularity. No one doubts the sincere piety of the French composer or the earnestness of his endeavour to make his oratorio of the Redemption worthy of its tremendous theme. But unfortunately good intentions cio not make a work of art. No doubt a certain grandeur is achieved in one or two of the choruses such as "Unfold, ye portals," and the finale. But it is the grandeur that results from piling up external material rather thail from true poetic force in the music. The phrase significant of happiness and innocence set to the words "For Thy love as a Father," akin to a passage in the prologue referring to the state of man before the Fall, has sweetness in a remarkable degree, but the sweetness is of a kind allied to triviality. In some of the narrative passages, notwithstanding the mechanical and tiresome manner of progression, the composer has succeeded in infusing a suggestion of the reverent spirit with which the work was composed. But what interminable lengths of dreary and unmeaning progressions up and down the chromatic scale! 1 What ugly and forced harmonies in the early numbers! What a heterogeneous mass of different styles, with reminiscences of every noted composer from Handel to Wagner! What abuse of the" pedal " device and of those jigging rhythms in 6/8 time like the chorus behind the scenes at the beginning of" Faust," the song" Dttes, la jenue belle" and dozens of other secular and trivial works by the same composer! There is also a much more serious indictment to be brought, namely, that whatever the composer's intentions may have been, the music of the "Redemption " often calls up associations that are utterly remote from religious feelings and religious ideas. When it is stated that the " March tceCalvary " represents the jeers of the multi- tude, this seems to be thought a justification of its peculiar .character. But no about of explanation can justify that tawdry and rollicking music which suggests scenes of fashionable frivolity or the clowning of a carnival possession. The admission of such a number in a work of religious purport is simply astounding." And again, referring to performances of "Elijah " and the " Redemption " at one of the Festivals, we may further and not unprofitably call atten- tion to what The Timer says: "The hackneyed oratorios chosen for the morning concerts of to-day and yesterday have a few characteristics in common, though in all ordinary respects they differ from each other as widely as possible. The glamour which each in its own day possessed for musicians as well as the public at large has worn off, and a large class of musical people are so accustomed to speak of both in much the same tone of indifference that there is created an impression of their being in the same category. But no impression can be more erroneous. While Aendelssohn's "Elijah" may be compared to a weather-beaten and battle-stained banner that, after its half-century of active service, may be held to have earned an honourable repose, Gounod's " Redemption " can only be likened to some tarnished article of 1 theatrical costume which ceases to serve any useful pur- poses as soon as its tinsel and fustian are exposed to the general gaze. Its tawdry splendour and complacent insincerities were once again exhibited to a large audi- ence this morning, and the performance certainly went far to set them in as e favour ble a light as possible." li Needless to add that with ich that is said by these . critics upon the pretentious f Gounod's "Redemption" Ito rank as a great and lasting work, we are in complete , I accord.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</head>
