      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>SOCIAL SERMONETYES FOR WORKING MEN,</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETYES FOR WORKING MEN,</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of "Search, Lights on, Lanl, Labour, and Social Problems." JOHN CHINAMAN IN SOUTH AFRICA.</p>
        <p>"Taxing the poor in order to compel them to labour at. work which they dislika, and at a rate of wages far below the market price, is, I believe, an economical doctrinepurely of South African origin." —SIR WILLIAM HA.RCOURT. If some fearless, honest historian would lift the veil that hides our Colonial labour troubles, and tell the whole truth about the dark and disgraceful things that have been done with our approval, and in the name of civilisation, he would make our ears tingle, and bring a blush of shame to our cheeks. I.—OUR COLONIES HAVE ALWAYS BEEN FOND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>OF CHEAP LABOUR.</head>
        <p>OF CHEAP LABOUR.</p>
        <p>From the earliest times they have been clamouring for" ',ewers of wood, and drawers of water," and menial serfs who could be trusted to do as they weret told, and ask no questions. And our home Governmen has permitted them to supply their demand for cheap labour by" ways that were dark, and tricks that were mean."</p>
        <p>When Cromwell was subduing Ireland in his own drastic fashion, he took an enormous number of prisoners of war. He did not careto cut all their throats in cold blood, so at the seige of Drogheda and else- where he marched them out in single file and knocked every tenth man's brains out and sent the other nine as slaves to our New England and Wait Indian planta- tions. . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>In later times we emptied our prisons of their surplus population, and sent out felons, and murderers, and thieves to our Colonies, and sold them as slaves. They were flogged,and starved,and dragooned into hard work, by cruel taskm Isters, and if they rebelled, or ran away they could be hunted up and hanged or shot. When the supply of white slavas fell off we embatked in the African slave trade. We hunted niggers, and shipped them off to our American Colonies to raise our cotton and sugar harvests, and thousands of our merchants grew rich bavond the draim3 of avarice by this trallc in flesh and blood. lI.—THE ABOLITION OF SLA,VERY ONLY CHANGED OUR, iVIETEIODS.</p>
        <p>We carried-Wri-the same old gamas,but we called them by different names. We could not legally capture ntggers, and force them into our service, but we could enslave them by fraud and deception. We could attract them to our gold and diamond mines by the promise of high wages, and when we had got them there we could reduce their wages, and tax them heavily, and compel them, by their necessities, to work on our own terms or starve. And we could get them to sign away their liberties by "indenturing" themselves to us for a term of years. Vie tried these games on the Kaffirs and other native races at first, but they had more wit than we gave them credit for. So long as our Rand mineowners would pgy them four or five pounds a month, and their keep, thelb was no trouble about the supply of labour. But when their wages were reduced by one half, and their huts and their wives were heavily taxed, and they ware locked up in"compounds" as slaves, they quietly served out their terms of agreement, and then went away and left no address. And I fancy there are thousands of working men in England who would have done all that, and probably a bit more. Now let me show you that the mineowners could afford to pay liberal and generous wages if they would, and that they have deliberately starved and taxed the labourers to increase their enormous dividends. Take the following evidence given before the Commissioners of Inquiry, and published in the Blue Book:— Mr. James Hay said that "the natives were paid too highly, and the mineowners were all agreed that it would be desirable to get forced labour." Mr. George Albu, the chairman of the Association of Mines, said that " the wages of the' boys' should be reduced thirty to forty per cent., and their taxes should be increased till they could not afford to live without work." Mr. Goldman said that "if the dignity of labour were impressed upon the natives by a tax of fifty shillings a year, and a stringent law passed to prevent them from running away, the mineowners would get a lamer supply of labour." Mr. Brakhan slid "such taxes must be imposed on the native as will virtually compel him to work, not only for a short period which is now the case, but for the best part of the year." It is clear enough from this evidence that the mine- owners are unwilling to pay the market price for any man's labour, and theyare Using every form of compul- sion, and coercion, and taxation to compel men to work for starvation wages. They could well afford to pay the old rate of wages if they would, for many of those mines Ihave returned in dividends their original capital over and over again. Let ma quote again from the Blue</p>
        <p>Book : - Mr.'llammand,the consulting engineer of the Consoti- dated 'Gold Fields, said that the profits of the mines for 1898 amounted to £4,817,505, which had been paid in dividends, but under good inanagem.ent their profits could be increased by £2,413,268 a year." Now, here you have a company paying nearly five millions a year in dividends, and yet it wants, by good management" —which is another name for Slavery—to cut ,down its labour bill nearly two and a half millions more. In order to enrich a few German Jews and Park-lane millionaires we are to squander our treasure and shed our blood in stealing the south African mines, and then we are to banish every freeman from those mines, and work them with the lowest and cheapest type of slave labour we can find. The whole of our labour troubles in South Africa can be explained by this policy of reducing men's wages and limiting their freedom. The "compounds" in which the natives are confined to prevent them from running away and stealing the gold are many of them unhealthy and badly managed. The Da Beers mine compound had a death rate of 30 per thousand a year or two ago, while the Rand Mines lost 70 per thousand. The na- tives are men, and not machines, and if they are kindly treated, and well paid,they will work cheerfully enough. 111.--JOHN CHINAMAN MAKES A CHEAP AND DOCILE SLAVE.</p>
        <p>And that is the reason the South African mineowners have set their covetous eyes on Mm. They dislike the white labourer because he is both costly and unmanage- able. He set § up a high standard of living, and he maintain. it by combination and trade unions that com- pel even millionaires to pay him a living wage. He claims all the rights of citizensitip, and if he pays rates and taxes he insists on the franchise, and ha does not hesitate to vote against his emnloyers and kick their i representatives out of office. This s a very sore point with these foreign mineowners, and it explains a good deal of their antipathy to free white labour. And they dislike theft Keats and niggers for their sturdy independence. If these 'boys" have a hut, and a mealie patch of their own, and two or three wives to supply their needs and dance attendance on them, they need not work at all. They have a mortal objection to going down into a mine, and toiling in the dark, to pick up gold for other people. So they will not work in the mines unless they are well paid and fairly treated. Lord Milner in 1902 agreed to the imposition of a capitation tax of two pounds a year for every adult male native, with an additional tax of two pounds a year for every wife over one that they may have, in ,laddition to the hut-tax that we have had so many rows about.</p>
        <p>The mineowners at the same time reduced their I wages once more from 30 to 40 per cent. Kaffirs who earned £2 7s. Id per month were reduced to £l. 6s. 4d. and one of the mining directors said :—"We have settled' the question of native labour, and have reduced the wages by one-third without any difficulty. We have now the more difficult problem of white labour before us. I do not at all approve of suddenly re- ducing wages and so producing a strike. That is in .every way undesirable. What we have to do is to shut down some of our poorer mines, and let the men' walk about the streets for two or three Imonths, until their feet are on the pavement. They'll be glad enough, then, to take whatever we may choose to offer them." So you see it is &amp;question of low wages and big dividends, and absolute control over the coun- try that these mineowners want. John Chinaman has neither patriotism, nor political ambition, nor stubborn independence. One country is as good as another to him,and he does not care who rules I him so long as they let him work out his own plans and 'schemes. He can live on next to nothing a week, and he will save something out of that. He is a born trader and he can turn his hand to anything, and the white 1 man who despises him had better beware, for he usually I has a few good trump cards up his sleeve. He is not afraid of work, and his initiative faculties are so largely developed that he can do almost anything that he has seen any other man do. So from the slaveowner's stand- point he is a desirable workman. But John has some serious faults that outweigh all his virtues, and in all civilised countries where he has gained a footing he is compelled to live in some China- town were he can have quarters to himself, and be separated from all other races. Do not ask me what he eats. If you were to peep into his pantry you would find anything from rats, and cats, and dogs, to snakes, I and horseflesh, and frogs. He does not keep himself I sweet and clean. He indulges in habits that are never mentioned in decent society. He is fond of overcrowded and insanitary surroundings. He is a confirmed gambler and opium smoker. So Yon can take it from me that if there are any vile and loathsome diseases to be had anywhere, John will pick them up and pass them on. His ideas of morality are of the most primitive sort, and he never makes a good citizen in any country. There is a world of meaning in that message from South Africa : "If you are dumping Chinamen down here, make them all bring their wives with them and shut th.m all up in compounds." . If You can read between the lines you can Lsee that John is not a desirable neighbour, and</p>
        <p>if he were turned loose among the white an coloured races of South Africa he would soon make the country a worse hell than it, is now. And yet Mr. Lyttelton told a deputation from the Society. of Friends recently, that he is unable to see that a man is under" demoralising conditions" who voluntarily under akes a time contract to do honest work for wages vastlyexceeding those which he :-,an elm in his own 'country, especially when he may terminate the con- tract on making good expenses thrown away, and when he may bring with him his wife and family at his em- ployer's expenses. If Mr. Lyttelton can see no harm in what is called tho Yellow Peril I should like to know if he would per- mit his own wife and daughters to live in the same town as these Chinese slaves. And while he is about it he might tell us why his own Government hedged them reund with such conditions as these No labourer may own any property, or lease any land. No labourer may leave the master to whom he has been assigned. No labourer may be without his passport in which he is described. No labourer may live outside the premises on which he is employed. No labourer may go outside these premises without a special permit, and in no case must this permit exceed 48 hours. Any labourer may be pounced upon by the police, and if without permit or passport, may be arrested without warrant and punished. Any person who harbours a runaway labourer exposes himself to severe punishment. Any employer may transfer his labourer to some other employer as freely as he could sell a horse or a sheep, and without his consent. If this is not slavery I should like to know what slavery is, and my only regret is that the Government dare not try it on in this country. We have colliery strikes and lo3k-outs here sometimes. Let the Tories import a cargo of indentured Chinamen here as "knob. sticks" and employ them to get our coal while our own colliers are hungering and starving. That is the way to bring it home to the man in the street. I make Mr. Balfour a present of this suggestion, and I dare him to adopt it and then face the music.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>PASSIVE RESISTAIOE.</head>
        <p>PASSIVE RESISTAIOE.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER SALE OF GOODS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Speech by Rev. W. J. Townsend, DD. THE REV. J. F. MATTHEWS' POSITION.</p>
        <p>Although shorn of the novelty which naturally sur- rounds the first local event of the kind, the second sale of Warrington passive resisters' goods seized in default of payment of the education rate, aroused considerable interest on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. William Hickman again gave his services as auctioneer, and a large gathering assembled in the Market Place to see the goods "put up." A lurry served as a rostrum and an orange box as a desk, and promptly at half-past four the auctioneer got to business. Mr. W. H. Woodcock (borough collector) and Mr. Thayerley attended on behalf of the authorities, and most of the passive resisters were present. Mr. Hickman, cheery as usual, at once reminded his audience that that was not the first time he had under- taken a sale of passive resisters' goods. "I hope it will be the last," he added," because it is not a very profitable transaction to the auctioneer." (Laughter.) Mr. Hick- man went on to say that while he was a law-abiding citizen and could not think of being an out and out passive resister, he had a great deal of sympathy with the passive resisters in their protest against the 'miserable Education Act." de concluded by announc- ing that after the sale, the resisters could "talk them- selves black in the face if they liked" as he had pro- del a dii.te irca an' ta3raf )fe sasy Ariald IV3 L.) jia la to the e Li: ir) iii. The sale was soon over, everything passing off in a quiet and orderly way. The following is a list of the articles seized, together with the amount of the rate required and the sums realized : Pair of bronzes (owner, Mr. Evan Hughes) ; rate re- quired 13s ; purchased for 14s by Mr. Balmford. Silver-plated tea-pot (Mr. T. Dale), rate 13s 2d ; purchased for 12s by Mr. Balmford. Ring, (Mr. Mayer); rate, 13s 2d; purchased for 13s 6d by Mr. B. Few. Marble timepiece (Mr. Howard) ; rate 9s 6d.; pur- chased by Mr. do ward for 17s. Gold hunter watch (Rev. W. J. Roberts),rate lls.10d.; purchased by Mr. Balmford for 13s. Pair of pictures (Mr. Balmford), rate 15s. 11d.; purchased by Mr. B. Few for £l. Silver watch (Mr. Scriven), rats 12s. 4d.; purchased by Mr. E. England for 12s. 4d. Carriage clock (Hiss Fieldhouse), rate Hs. 11d. ; purchased by Mr. Balmford. for 17s. Pair of silver candlesticks ( Mr.Clatworthy), rate 135.; purchased by Mr. Tanner for 17s. Silver watch and chain (Mr. Gallop) rate 16s. 4d. ; purchased by Mr. Evan Hughes for 95. It was understood that the goods had been "bought in" on behalf of the passive resisters. Any sum remain- ing after paying the rate will, of course, be refunded to the owners. At the close Mr. HICKM AN remarked that the days of the government ware numbered. He expected that the new Liberal Government would give thema new Educa- tion Act. Then there would be no more passive resist- ance and he would be" out of work." Laughter.) The resisters, accompanied by the Rev.W.J.Townsend D.D., of Prestatyn, then mounted the improvised plat- form and several speeches were delivered. Mr. BALMFORD said that was the second time they had been there on such an occasion. Evidently, if Mr. Balfour had his way, they would be there a great many" more times yet. Mr. Balfour, poor man, did not under- stand them. There were a great many people he did not understand, or he would not be in the muddle he was in to-day. The passive resisters were not making that protest for themselves alone. They were protest- ' ins- on behalf of their children, and the children of all th'ose present. They were fighting for a principle. They were there to plead for liberty and freedom of thought and action,for opportunities which had been taken from them, for the control of their money, and for no religious tests. For that they were prepared to suffer, if need be to bleed, and to work. A good many of their sympathisers like Mr. Hickman, seemed to be afraid of becoming resisters because they would be twitted with seeking Cheap notoriety. (Mr. Hickman: nine's cheap. I have had nothing for it to-day.) Although he detested cheap notoriety and painless martyrdom, he would rather have those than cheap principles. (Hear, hear.) There seemed to be some doubt as to the result of the e no doubt about it action(the speaker)whic hoped theirhero, there rw.ouwould Matthews,b had taken. He and that the men in authority would see that Mr. Matthews suffered the just results of his action. He thought no man had a right to interfere with another man's principles. If Mr. Matthews thought the policy he had adopted was the bast, no man had a right to interfere with that policy and with the interests which he sought to serve by that policy. He trusted the law would be allowed to take its course in the matter. , The Rev. J. F. Matthews said his action had been absolutely dictated by just one thing—that he would not, under any conceivable considerations, be com- pelled to pay for a sectarian Education Act. That was his policy, and whatever ultimate issues might be, he intended to stick to it. If the present Government amended the Act so much the better. If, on the other hand, another Government ca me in,the Act would most certainly have to be remedied, and would be remedied in the right direction. What form the remedy would take he did not know. Personally, he as much objected to pay for the teaching of the Bible in State schools as he objected to pay for a Roman Catholic or Anglican catechism. He believed in the absolute secularisa- tion of the schools of the people. It was the work of thechurches to teach religion, not the work and duty of the State. He was sorry to tell them that RAS not the position of his confreres oaths Free Church Council, and most certainly it was not the position of the National Free Church -Council. At the marvellously large meetings held fie Newcastle, the National Free Church Council introduced a preamble consisting of eight clauses, one of which specially sug- gested that local authorities might if they chose, teach the Bible in the schools. Dr. Townsend informed him that he thought the bulk of the workingmen in the country were in favour of the reading of the Bible in the schools of the people. If that were so, all right. But, as far as he was con- cerned, he did not thinkhe ought to be called upon to pay even for the teaching of the Bible in the State schools. That, in his opinion, was the only rational principle, and the principle which it seemed to him that they as Nonconformists would ultimately be com- pelled to adopt. He hoped the time was not far distant when some amicable arrangement would be come to, whereby the churches themselves, out of school hours and at their own expense, would be able to teach their own creeds and leave the teachers in the schools of thepeople to teach government subjects only. He wished to acknowledge most cordially the courtesy and urbanity of the magistrates who, although they (the resisters) had no iocas standi in law, gave them an opportunity of registering their protest. He wanted them to compare the action of the bench with the action of the Mayor, when a man, who had a perfect right in law to ask for an exernotion for his child from vaccination, when that exemption had been granted had to listen to the Mayor, who said he could not understand any man having a conscientious objec- tion to vaccination, and thus allowing smallpox to be introduced in the town. He (Mr. Matthews) said that any man who used his high position to say that to a man who by the law had a perfect right to state his conscientious "objection, was nothing more norless than a cad, even though'he might be the' chief magistrate of the borough. They had only got to remember that he was a brewer and Perhaps that would explain all. In conclusion, Mr. Matthews moved a vote of thanks to the Bench, Mr. Hickman, Mr. Woodcock, and his helpers, and to the police officers who executed the distress warrant. He wished with all his heart that in the vari- ous parts of the Kingdom the magistrates and oficials had acted with regard to passive resisters as the magis-</p>
        <p>trates and officials in Warrington had acted. Then, he been spent altogether under the most pleasant circum-- thought, the hateful sting and opprobrium to which stances. He had been the subject of a great deal of many passive resisters had been subjected would have , criticism, most of it unfriendly, largely of a personal been taken avray,and a great deal of ill-feeling produced by I character, begotten in most cases of jealousy and anta- their protest would not have been forthcorning.(Applause) ,Igonism, and it was not difficult to understand that a • The Rev.' W. J. Townsend, D.D., delivered an ad- man who had stood for something like 20 years in the dress. He said that 17,000 ladies and gentlemen had forefront of public work in Warrington could hardly - been haled before the magistrates and orders for dis- expect to escape altogether from making a few enemies. traint had been made on account of the unjust education He had been a hard hitter himself,and he had to receive- rate. They had 16.410 denominational schools put upon hard hits. He was afraid that would be his particu- their taxes and Wes for every farthing of their expense- lar characteristic to the end of his time. He They had to pay the salaries of about 70,000 sectarian had never hit a man below the belt as far - teachers. They did not ask as Nonconformists that one asle knew, and he had always resented being hit penny should ever be spent in teaching their dogmas below the belt himself. He was afraid that the and special beliefs. They th,oroughly disapproved agitation had done a considerable amount of mischief of sectarian teaching in public schools at the to the general credit of the town and if to a certain public expense. Let the churches, and their extent it re-acted on its trade, they must not be sur- churchez alone, teach their own dogmas and prised. He thought he could claim that Warrington doctrines and train the children as they ought to be occupied to-day a far different position to what it did trained in a knowledge of the Bible and of the will of some 20 or 30 years ago. (Hear, hear.) He attributed God. They were there that afternoon to hear a strong the lack of eoterprise in former days as responsible for and clear protest against a new form of injustice and the burdens they had now to bear, for in addition to persecution in this land of ours. He could tell them the growing and expanding necessities of the town they the most shameful stories of how passive resisters had had also to contend with the unfortunate legacies left been bullied by Dogberry's on the bench. Passive re- by their predecessors. Warrington stood as well to the sistance would go on,—even for thirty years if the forefront now as any town with which he was acquainted. Education Act continued in operation. He was not They had been accused of all kinds of things—they were allowed to be a passive resister because the Act was as told they were practically universally corrupt and while A dead as a door nail in Wales. He wanted to know how it they now found that one of the founders of the purity w was that the Act could be killed in Wales, and it was not party had been convicted of bribery, that was no reason killed in Eagland. If the Nonconformists would only why the whole body politic was corrupt. (Hear, hear.) stand shoulder to shoulder as they were standing in He declined to subscribe to that opinion. He hoped Wales the Act would be a dead letter next week, and the mistakes of 30 or 40 years ago were not going to be would never have a chance of being applied throughout repeated. He was never one to believe in cheap economy, the country. The Government had dared to invade the and money well spent always brought a safe constitutional principle that ,where the people found and satisfactory return whether in municipa 1 o the money they must have the control, and they would or private enterprise. (Hear, hear.) In regard to the have to pay a tremendous penalty before they were trade of the town he mastagree toa certain extent with ' , very much older. All the recent bye-elections told the Mr. Kay that it was not quite as satisfactory as they story that the Government was doomed, and any day,or vronld like to sea it, and in thatr espect it did not differ any hour almost, that Government might be driven out from other towns. Owing to Warrington's many-sided of power and sent into disgrace for a generation to industries,the fluctuations of trade were not as severely come, because it had dared to t tamer with the most felt as was the case where a town depended on one or sacred principle of the English constitution. If they as two large industries. Still trade in the United ' Nonconformists and men of principle paid the Edam- Kingdom was not as prosperous as they would like to ' tion rate they would stamp themselves as slaves. They see it, but there was no reason why they should lose would resist the Act. They meant to fight it out to the heart; on the contrary there was every reason to be bitter end and to the very death, and they would never encouraged. A question of great importance in regard' sheath their sword until they had a system of education to the future trade and government of the town was that was free and unsectarian, national, and of the that referring to the dredging of the river by the Ship' highest possible standard that the world could produce. Canal Company. The cost of the litigation was going They had not sought that battle. The priests of to be somewhat heavy,but that was no reason why they England had thrown the gauge of battle down should shrink from ascertaining what their real legal before them, and they would step into the position was from the highest legal authority in the- arena and take up the gauge, and, if it meant United Kingdom—the House of Lords. The interests war for a generation, they would fight on until the of Warrington were to his mind of the extremest pos- victory came on the side of religious liberty and equality sible importance because at the present time the trade and of a free and higher standard of popular education, upon the river was directly associated with only a few England could never be as free as it ought to be with firms. When they took into consideration the fact that, that ghastly and cruel Act fastened upon them and the area of the river which was to be dredged to the. • working untold mischief and sorrow among them. (Ap- requisite depth was something like a.mile and a 'half in- plause.) length, the frontage of which on the whole of both Mr. A. SCR,IVEN seconded the vote of thanks to the sides might sooner or later be bounded by wharves magistrates and officials,whichwas carried, and warehouses—and he hoped to see the day This concluded the proceedings. when that would be accomplished—they would see that while at the moment the interests of the plaintiffs seemed to be the paramount interests, looking forward to the time when that which he had suggested would be an actual fact, it was of the gravest possible importance to the future of Warrington that everything should be done that could possibly be done to secure to the town whatever rights it possessed in regard to the river. It was a question in regard to which necessarily, because, of its complex nature, there might be a division of opinion. He congratulated the Grocers' Association Upon having so strong and united a body, and he wished* it every prosperity and success. (Applause). COUNCILLOR WILKINSON'S COIVIMANDIVIENT: Councillor WrmuNsoN also spoke in response to the I toast. He referred to the progress of the town. With reference to the sore point Mr. Roberts mention ed in, regard to municipal trading, he (Councillor Wilkinson) vvould always be a non-trader. It was commanded "Thou shalt not trade with the Corporation of which thou art a member." (Laughter.) If there was any mud-throwin with it. ghe would decline to have anything to do</p>
        <p>OTHER TOASTS. Mr. W. Fl. OWEN proposed the toast of " The- Guests " in felititous terms, and Mr. Cowden brieflY seconded. Messrs. Walmsley, Wardlo w, Light-- body, Macdonald, and Parkinson responded. ' Mr. J. GANDY, in proposing the toast of the "Local Association," said lack of combination in Warrington had been a. long felt want for a great many years, but they had risen to the occasion and now had a Grocers' Association. The grocers now met each other in a friendly spirit, and were nut afraid of going into each others shops and exchanging compliments and notes. This was as it should be. (Hear, hear.) Mr. MONKS seconded, and said a great compliment 'had been paid to the grocers by Mr. Parkinson in his observations on his invariably pleasant experiences with Warrington grocers, in his capacity as a traveller. The PRESIDENT, in reply, said he found the members thoroughly loyal to the association and he tcas very proud of their organisation. There was no danger of them going back to the old system. They seemed to have started the association at the psychological mo- ment. He had now been president for three years and he would like to retire from that position at the end of his present year of office. He paid a tribute to the services of Messrs. J. P. Gorst and W. H. Owen in, making the arrangements for the dinner. The PRESIDENT then proposed the toast of" The Federation." He regretted that Alderman Jarvis, of Birmingham, the president of the Federation, was un- able to be present. Alderman Jarvis had sent a tele- gram expressing his regret. The President's health was toasted on the proposition of Mr. J. GANDY, seconded by Mr. STEEL—Mr Richardson's services to the association were eulogis ed and regret was expressed that the President's father was too unwell to be with them.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT acknowledged the compliment, anct the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>SHIP CANAL LITIGATI3N. INTL° An Appeal to the House of Lords.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL LITIGATI3N. INTL° An Appeal to the House of Lords.</p>
        <p>Ai a meeting of all the plaintiffs in the recent action. of Crosfield and others v. the Manchester Shin Canal Company and the members of the Rivers Committee held on Wednesday under the presidency of the Mayor, it was unanimously resolved that an appeal be made to the House of Lords. It was also decided that the Corporation cannot under the cireumstanees support the Ship Canal Company in their Bill before Parliament.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-03-26_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>GROCERS ANNUAL DINNER. Mr. Roberts antitie River Litigation.</head>
        <p>GROCERS ANNUAL DINNER. Mr. Roberts antitie River Litigation.</p>
        <p>Since the rormation of the Warrington and District Grocers' and Provision Dealers' Association it has been customary to hold an annual dinner at the Winruarleigh Cafh. The dinner held on Thursday evening was the third of its kind and the attendance was representative of the trade. Mr. E. Richardson, the popular president of the association occupied the post of honour and amongst those present were: Mr. J. Kebty Fletcher (president of the Liverpool Wholesale Grocers' and Provision Merchants' Association), Mr. H. Roberts, J.P Mr. Councillor Wilkinson, Mr. J. Fulton Smith (Chamber of Commerce), Mr. T. S. Steel (solicitor to the asso- ciation), Mx. G. Bowcock (Butchers' Association), Mr. John Gandy and Mr. William Monks (vice-presidents of the Warrington and District Grocers' and Provision Dealers' Association), Mr. J. Povey (treasurer), Mr. J. P. Gorst (secretary), Messrs. H. Redding, J. Gerrard, J. Kay, W. Leigh, W. H. Owen (committee), Messrs. Dunn, Cowden, Miller, E. Gandy, Duckworth, and reprosenta- tives of merchants and manufacturing firms. The PRESIDEST announced that Mr. A. H. Crosfield had telegraphed and also written from Cannes,regretting his inability to be present, and wishing the association success. On the removal of the cloth, after an excellent repast, a long toast list was submitted. The speeches ware agreeably interspersed with songs by Messrs. J. Gandy, E. Gandy, W. H. Owen, Harvey, Lenton, Smith and Collins. Tin duties of accompanist were ably dis- charged by Mr. Tom Taylor.</p>
        <p>The PRESCDEN'T proposed the loyal tOasts. THE VALUE OF COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIA.M MONKS, one of the vice-presidents of the Association, proposed ," The Chamber of Commerce and kindred Associations." lie remarked that the Chamber of Commerce was now in a more flourishing condition than ever. He could assure the local chamber of the bast wishes of the Grocers' and Provision Dealers' Association. (Hear, hear.) He had attended many committee meetings of the association and he had always found the prevailinn, desire, to be to support that which was right from a trine point of view and condemn that which was wrong. (Haar, hear.) He welcomed the members of other associations present and felt con- vinced that if the different trades organisations in the town could join hands by sending representatives to a central body it would be a very wise proceeding. The PRESIDENT said they felt particularly honoured by the presence of Mr. Fulton Smith,vice-president of the Warrington Chamber of Commerce. It showed that the Grocers' Association was now being recognised, and that they had the sympathy of the Chamber of Commerce. Mr. FULTON SNITTFT, in reply, pointed out the good work which the Chamber of Commerce was doing, and thought similar organisations of commerce were wanted in every town, especially of an industrial character. (Hear, hear.) Union was strength, and when different chambers of commerce met and discussed questions of public polity and usefulness they were listened to by the Government of the day and their suggestions were very often the embryo of future legislation. Those of the same trade should meet and interchange views. It was not necessary for anyone, to give away his own "show," but at the same time there were broad points which could be discussed for their mutual benefit. That reminded him of a couplet composed by one of the lords of the law courts in Edinburgh who was famous for his adaptations of old proverbs. "Two of a trade, 'twas early said, do very ill agree. A beggar hates at rich men's gates, a beggar's face to</p>
        <p>see, But trades there are, the rather rare, where men are not so jealous. Two lawyers know the coals to blow, just like a pair of bellows." (Laughtert) Proceeding to refer to Mr. Monks' suggestion about the federation of trades bodies, Mr. Smith said he might mention that in 1901 a joint board of conciliation was formed between the Arbitration Committee of the Chamber of Commerce amid representatives of the War- rington Trades and Labour Council. That was a very good idea, and although nothing had been done actually, it spoke well for the relationships betweenemployer:and employe,and showed there was a disposition on the part of the labour community to combine with the members of the Chamber. This was a stop towards usefulness to both, for when such combinations met on bro&lt;bd lines like that nothing but good would follow. (Applause). Mr. J. KE9TY FETCHER, president of the Liverpool Wholesale Grocers' and Provision Merchants' Associa- tion also responded. He expressed the opinion that if grocers had been more combined in the past the co- operative societies would te-day be paying income tax. (Hear, hear.) However it was not too late for them to do a lot now. Something ought to be done with regard to the competition which was going on between Olt co-operative societies and the traders all over the country. (Hear, hear.) It was impossible to regulate prices in the trade because that wetild lead to frictio and the bi-eak-up of their organisation. Mr. GEORGE BOWCOCK also replied, as a member of a kindred organisation. Combination, he pointed out,was necessary in order to fight for the interests of traders. The grocers in Wtrrington, he believed, had been hard hit by the co-operative movement. Up to the present, the co-operative society had not commenced in the meat trade, with which he was connected, but if they should do so. the butchers would act together and certainly get on terms with them by selling as reasonably as their oneonentsicould,and giving people valuelfor their money. He was of opinion that such organisations as those of the butchers and grocers should have their own reore- Isentatives on the Town C)nn6il. The butchers had on two occasions tried unsuccessfully and he believed the grocers had also tried, though in vain, but they must buckle to and endeavour to be represented, as their- interests were very great. (Hear, hear). THE TOWN AND TRADE OF WARRINGTON. SPEECH BY MR. H. ROBERTS, J.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. KA.Y in nronosing "The Town and Trade of Warrington" referred to the numerous improvements which had been made by the Cornoration in recent years. In a reference to the Non-Trading agitation,he said some of the members of their Corporation were a bit obstreperous and some people in the town felt that they should import a stranger to get them out of the difficulty. They knew it would not be all straight- sailing, but he thought they had men enough in War- rington to get them out of the difficulties they had to contend with, without bringing in a stranger. (Hear, hear.) They had practically seen the back of that agitation which now seemed to be dying,out. One of the founders of the purity party had got a little bit dis- gusted and left them. Alluding to the trade of the town, Mr. Kay said it was not very good at present for he did not think there was one works on f ull time, which was rather a singular state of affairs in Warring ton, where they had so mstly varied industries. The PRESIDENT in calling upon Mr. Roberts to re spond, observed that if they wanted to point out a man who had made his mark there was no one who had done more to imoress his -personality upon the town of his birth than Mr. Roberts. Mr. ROBERTS, who was greeted with loud applause On rising, said that the last two years of his life had not</p>
