        <p>ELECTION OF POOR LAW GUARDIAN. TO THE ELECTORS OF LATCHFORD WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— Having been asked to allow myself to be nominated for the forthcoming election of a Poor Law Guardian for Latchford Ward, I have much pleasure in placing my services at your disposal. I disclaim all political feeling and have received many promises of support from representatives of all shades of opinion. I am fully aware of the responsible and manifold duties attaching to the office of a Guardian of the Poor. I have for ten years visited at the Warrington Work- house, chiefly in the Infirmary, and have been a mem- ber of the )3rabazon Society since its commencement. I have for two years been Brabazon Secretary and have been greatly pleased to see what a cheering effect the work has had on the spirits of the invalids, some of whom are condemned to pass the remainder of their days within the Hospital walls. I have ample time at my disposal and, if elecled. I should devote myself energetically to all the duties coming within the sphere of a Guardian of the Poor, &amp;s- -pecially in connection with the care of the women. the girlt, the children, and the aged, sick and infirm of both sexes, who together comprise the vast majority of those who have to be dealt with under the Poor Law. There are also many case; arising in connection with out relief which Women Guardians are specially qualified to deal with b. aseistinq women and girls who are willing to wore, to obtain emplcyment of van i us Rinds, and by helping them in of her ways to avoid the brand of pauper ism. Experience has proved the desirability of having on every hoard of guardians a fair proportion of women to deal specially with their own sex and with the chil- dren. and to supervise the innumerable details which are necessary in a large Institution, and which they are more fitted by nature and training to look after than men. When it is considered that there are 163 women and a large number of children to be cared for, it will be obvious to any thoughtful person that there is plenty of work for two or three women on the Board. I am deeply sensible ol the necessity for the strictest economy in the management of such a vast organization as tke Warrington Guardians have to control. and on this point I should endeavour to safeguard tbe Rate- payers' interests tay the most judicious expenditure of their money. My only desire in seeking election is, that if success- fel. I may be able to devote my time and energies on behalf of those whose misfortune and infirmities make them dependent on the help of others, and in whose interests as well as your own, I earnestly solicit your support. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours faithfully, CONSTANCE H. BROADBENr. I The Hollies, Latchford, March 15th, 1904.</p>
