        <p>rifiESHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL,—The EDUCA- -1,, / CATION COMMITTEE offer FOR COMPETITION:- 120 DAY SCHOLARSHIPS to boys and girls who are in attendance at Elementary schools, to proceed to Secondary schools. Candidates must be not more than 14 yens .of ago on August Ist. 1904. 180 CHOLAItSHIPS for boys and girls desirous of be- coming pupil teachers, and who ate in attendance at any echool,—Elernentary or otherwise; and who will not be more than 15 or less than 12 vesrs of age on August Ist, 1904. Forms of application for either set of tcholarships can now be obtained from the uniersignad, to whom they must be returned not later than April 2nd, 1904. RICHARD P. WARD, Director of Education, City Road, Chester.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF DISTRICT COUNCILLOR TO THE R ATEPAYBELS OF GREAT SANKRY. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, I have now had the honour of representing von on the Board of Guardians and the Rural District Council for the past nine years. On Monday next you will again be asked to elect a representative. and I beg to offer myself for re-election and to appeal to you for your support. Both as a Guardian and Rural District Councillor, I have always worked ia the Interests of the Union in general and of Great Sankey in particular. My best services have been at the comman I of the ratepayers, and. if re-elected. they will continue to be at your disposal. My experience of Poor Law Administration and of matters connected with the Rural District Council is now considerable, and that my services have met with the approval and appreciation of my colleagues is shown he the fact that they have elected ire to the highest office they can confer. I hope the result of the poll will prove that they have also met with your approval and appreciation. During my nine years of office many improvements have been made in Great Saakey. Many others, much needed, remain to be w.rried out. These I am in full touch with, and should you re-elect me you may rely upon me sparing no effort to see that they are 'one. I beg to thank you for your support in the past, and to ask you to vote for me again on Monday next. Believe me, Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours respectfully, T. HALTON. Vine Cottage, Chapel lane, Great Sankey.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE COUN FY COUNCIL. TG CARTERS, FARMERS, AND TEAM OWNERS. 17.)ERONS desirous of 'I ENDERING for CARTING MACADAM and other Materials for the Main Roads, for Ih3 year ending Msrch, 1905, can ob:ain forms of Tender, and narticul.rs of Material to be carted at the County Surveyor's Offices, Custer Castle, on end after Saturday, the 19:h March,tetween 10 a.m. and 5 p.m. end on Siturdaye:beswecn 10 a.m. &amp; 1 p.m. Tenders to be endorsed " Tender for Carting," to b 3 delivered at the said Offic3 on or before Stturday, the 2nd day of April next. The Main RDads Committee of the County Council do not bind themselves to accept the lowest er any Tender. HARRY F. BULL, County Btir. vey or. Cheater Casle, .1.6% March, 1904.</p>
        <p>TO THE ELECTORS"' OF VIADUuT WARD. LADIES AND GENTLKMEN, After an zbsence of three years from the Newton-in- Makerfield Urban District Council, I again offer myself for eleetion to that important body. as one of the repre- sentatives of the Viaduet Ward, at the request of many electors resident In the Ward. During my last tenure of office, I consistently worked In the interests of the Ratepayers, and, whilst not affect- ing any particular creed or party, devoted all my efforts to the well-being of the whole Township. Since I retired from the Council, many matters of importance have transpire 1, requiring the earnest atten- tion of the Council, some of which are still under consideration, and you may rest assured, that, if I am honoured with your confidence, I will ant in connection with them in a manner which, in my opinion, will be to the best interests of the Township. The offer of a Free Library made by Mr. Carnegie is one of the matters engaging the attention ot the Council and any action taken by the Council, it for the welfare of the Township, will receive my earnest support. My viewellon Labour questions are well known, and I am an ardent advocate for efficiency in the Townstito Fire Brilade. The recent lamentable fire in Market Street. and the evident unpreparedness of the brigade. renders it imperative that a perfect state of efficiency should be secured, and this point will receive my beet attention. I am in favour of a Volunteer Fire Brigade, and will work for the inauguration of ouch a Brigade. My previous experience as a Councillor will enatoe me to deal with matters corning before the Council from a practical standpoint, and whilst I shall always vote for increased efficiency in working, I obeli direct my efforts to seeing that this condition of things is maintained at a reasonable cost. The que-tion of Public Swimming Baths is one, which will come to the front before long, and when it does, I can assure you it will receive my careful consideration. I appeal to you as an Independent Candidate, untram- meled by party or sect, and as a resident amongst you. and trust I may be honoured with your confidence -.t this Ballot. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Faithfully yours, WILLLIAM BHATTY. Griffin Hotel, Farie St. Earlestown. March 176 h, 1904.</p>
        <p>OTICE IS HEREBY WEN. tbat the next 11 GENERAL QUARTER SVS_IONS of the Pi ai?.ei for the couNrY OF CHESTER will be held at the COURT HOUSE, KNU ISFORD, in and for the said County, on TUESDAY, the sth day of April next, at Eleven o'clock in the Forencon, when the Grand Jury will be empannelled. The hearing of all Appeals ari ine within the Hun- dreds of Buciclow, Macclesfield and Northwich, forming the Knutsfurd Dis:rict, and the trials of all prisoners committed from such Hundreds, will take place at such Sessions. The Grand and Petty Jurisa for the Knufsfcrd District must be in attendance at the Court House, Knutcford, at t' e opening of the C urt on TUESDAY, the sth day of Aoril next, at Eleven o'clock in the Forenoon. THE COURT will be Adjourned to the Castle of Chester, en WEDNESDAY, the fila day of April next, at Eleven o'clock in the Forenoon. Toe bearing of all Appeals arising within the Hundreds of Broxton, Eddis- bury, Nantwicb, and Wirral. forming the Chester Dis- trict, and the trials of all prisoners committed from the last-mentioned Hundreds will take place at such adjourned Sessions. The Grand td Petty Juries for the Chester District must be in attendance at the Castle of Chester, at the opening of the Court on WEDNESDAY, the 6% day of April next, at Eleven o'clock in the Forenocu. REGINALD POTTS, Clerk of the Peace for the County of Chester. Chester, 14th March, 1904.</p>
